How have travel prices changed since 1989
Until 1989 there were in Czechoslovakia only 5 state travel agencies: Cedok, Sporturist, CKM, Rekrea and Autotourist.
Traveling agencies at that time offered trips especially to the countries of the socialist bloc, because trips to the west were very limited.
Czech tourists were traveling for holidays in East Germany, Soviet Union (Sochi, Crimea and Black Sea), Bulgaria, Romania (both in the Black Sea) and Hungary (Balaton).
A great exception for the period from 1962 to 1972 when it was allowed to travel to Yugoslavia and then the regime began to have an inclination for the west and travels to Yugoslavia were very limited for Czech tourists.
The great paradox of the socialist era was that it was not permissible for the citizens of the socialist bloc (also for the Czechoslovaks) to travel individually even within the framework of the socialist countries.
In the event that the Czechoslovak citizen wanted to travel individually for example in Poland or the Soviet Union he needed an invitation letter otherwise the individual trip was not allowed.
Traveling agencies at that time also offered trips to the West however it was necessary to queue all night with a sleeping bag or to have a "good knowledge" within the travel agency.
And even after that it wasn’t won yet.
The agreement for currencies which was the condition to travel to the west was denied “politically incorrect” people who could not travel at all.
The Czechoslovak currency, however, was not exchangeable and it was not possible to obtain financial means without the currency agreement (Western currency) to travel to the west.
The Czechs most often spent their holidays in the country where there were therefore many country houses and chalets that the Czechs bought and this habit lasts until now.
The travels of the Czechs have since experienced a great revolution since 1989.
Although until 1989 the holidays in East Germany and Bulgaria were at the maximum, the Czechs spent last year abroad 4 million days on holiday in countries around the world.
The great paradox of which we are unaware today is that the most popular country is Croatia where it was not possible to go freely until 1989 because part of Yugoslavia at the time.
The abolition of visas in the 90's of the last century in the neighboring countries of the west provoked a huge wave of short-lived visits especially in Austria to Vienna and Germany.
The Czechoslovaks could not for a long time fly to the west that everyone wanted to see at least a moment.
For example in Paris, travel organizations made the Czechs sleep in tents in the parks because our income did not allow us to pay for the hotel or a pension.
The typical feature of a trip by Czechs was at that time to take with you his own canned food, sausage, etc... to save.
Another important moment was also the interchangeability of the Czech krona which allowed our tourists finally after decades of being able to freely buy Western currency.
Our standard of living as well as the average wage have increased what caused another important time around 1997.
Thanks to a growing demand for travel, travel agencies have begunined since 1997 to prepare tours with air charter connections that thanks to contracts have tremendously reduced costs compared to what was before.
At the end of the 90's of the last century the Czechs had visa-free links with virtually most developed countries all over the world apart from the EU which later facilitated our travels.
Another interesting moment in the history of post-revolution tourism, when was prepared the first aerial charter to an exotic country, Thailand, from the Czech Republic.
Since this year as every year direct aerial charters take Czech tourists to the distant warm countries; this winter there will be 12 exotic destinations from the Czech Republic.
That’s more than 100000 Czechs who visit exotic countries every year!
Another interesting moment in this period that symbolizes our post-revolutional development arrived in 2008.
on 17 November 2008, the US abolished visas for Czech tourists and allowed our country to fit fully into the group of developed countries.
In 2008 also the Czechs for the first time in history spent more than half of their holidays abroad (50, 3%) and for the first time in history spent more than 15,000 kronors per person for holidays abroad including the ancillary charges.
The General Assembly must find its President but this may end with a situation without a solution "one step"
Almost 5months already Czech football is without President and it risks that the successor of Ivan Hasek at the head of the Football Association of the Czech Republic will not yet be known after another General Assembly to take place Thursday in Nymburk.
However, there is still no agreement on which voting by-law to proceed.
Without this agreement it is not possible to start voting for one of the three candidates.
Jindrich Rajchl, Miroslav Pelta and Tomas Paclik are interested in the management of Czech football.
Hasek resigned in June and during his departure called on his colleagues to unite and find a strong personality who will lead football in the future.
Only during the era after Hasek football returned backwards to divergences between the chambers of the Association that cannot agree on a common candidate.
This was completely shown on September 16 when it was necessary to choose a new president.
Delegates of the General Assembly met unnecessarily in the National House in Prague as the negotiations remained stalled and there was not even a question of dealing with how to elect.
There was no agreement on who had the right to vote in the General Assembly - only, it seemed, the statutory representatives or also the public servants vested with full powers.
In this single point there has been a partial agreement since that moment with the help also of the Office for State and Law, according to that representation on the basis of full power is not possible.
Discrepancies are still ongoing with respect to the settlement for the election.
It is too clear that the Executive Committee, which in its planned or extraordinary negotiations, did not bring anything to the situation either
Views on the possibility of voting have changed several times, but the focal point always remains the same.
The question is: how to proceed for the third round so as not to fall into the pat.
"The apple of contention lies in the following fact: either proceed according to the statutes or find another model", noted one of the three vice-presidents of the Football Association of the Czech Republic Dusan Svoboda who represents professional football within the association.
According to the rules of the planned model wins in the third round that of the candidates who will have received the most votes in percentage within the chamber for which he is a candidate.
Moravia is ready according to what is said to withdraw so that the candidate who has received the largest percentage of votes in both chambers wins.
And the Czech side studies the condition that the winner should have at least two-thirds of the percentages of votes.
"Only it would then lose the idea of two previous rounds and it would completely deny the bicameral concept," admits Svoboda who understands the objections of the moravian side and according to whom it would best be not to change the by-law and vote according to the statutes in force.
It is surprising that Tomas Paclik also agrees even if he believes that the settlement of the elections established according to the statutes benefits the most Pelta among all the candidates.
"I hope that we vote as much as possible according to the statutes of the association."
"So that there are not yet crazy ideas imagined by a representative as during the past General Assemblies," said the owner of Victoria Plzen who is a candidate for the first time and who in his presentations has defined himself mainly against Pelta.
Pelta remains optimistic.
According to him in Nymburk there will be a new President and believes that the candidates will cooperate in the future together and constructively.
"The possible qualification today in the European Championship should calm everyone down."
"Football will once again be on a wave of happiness and there will be more enthusiasm for cooperation" adds the Jablonec officer who among the three has the greatest experience in the management of football both professionally and at the regional level.
Rajchl is more pessimistic.
"I believe in this agreement, but unfortunately the situation is very tense."
"It's not a problem between the Czechs and the Moravians, but there are tendencies to want to change drawing regulations."
"This can again cause a blockage in the General Assembly."
"I still hope that the healthy reason will win," Rajchl said.
There is not much time left for joint agreement for all parties.
Some meetings should take place directly in Montenegro but who can say if the entire football management will be complete for the match.
The specially charted theft to Podgorice, among others, Vice-Presidents Dalibor Kucera and Rajchl did not take off for technical reasons and a variant was sought to bring part of the executive committee to the field of reprisals.
In the end it is the ministerial meeting, which could take place on Wednesday, which should be decisive.
The Minister of Youth Education and Sports Joseph Dobes would be very pleased to receive the torn parts within the country's strongest sports association and bring them to an agreement.
In the game there is a lot: a state endowment, the credibility of football and also the General Assembly of the Czech Union of Sports which must determine the direction of the Czech sport after the financial problems of Sazka.
Paclik's going into the fight for President's position because he doesn't see any correct candidates.
If the leader of the footballers of Plzen Tomas Paclik had seen before the General Assembly whose profile would have responded to everything he asks for for a new President of the Football Association of the Czech Republic (AFRT) he would not be campaigned for this high office.
"If such a candidate exists I support him, I fight for him and I do not run for election."
“But in the current situation it has not been possible to find such a candidate,” Paclik told the Czech News Agency (CTK).
Paclik's motivation is to compete with Miroslav Pelta and Jindrich Rajchl to try to prevent Pelta from reaching the head of the AFRT which would a priori be given favorite for this election.
Paclik also points out that in the event that he wins the election he would hold this position of President only until 2013 year scheduled for a General Assembly.
He would see in the function an experienced and high-level manager who is not connected with the world of football.
“I’ve talked to people who have the profile,” he said.
"For me it was terrible when some told me they didn't want to be identified with Czech football right now."
"I'd like to change this image," he added.
Which, it is said, embarrasses especially Paclik, is the relationship between Pelta and the President of the Regional Football Union of Plzen Roman Berbr, a man of thwarted fame who is known behind-the-scenes as a lobbyist capable of influencing the vote of the delegates the General Assembly.
For Paclik it is not acceptable for Pelta to hold this position as President.
"If he won, the current state would remain and there would be nothing new in football."
"And with the support of Berbr, who is a high-flying trader, it would be even less favorable to football," Paclik said.
What still shocks this is the fact that Pelta's name was pronounced a few years ago during wiretaps as part of an investigation into a case of corruption, for which Pelta escaped without any trouble.
"I don't know what prestige Pelta would bring to football."
"And how would we be considered in the eyes of UEFA and FIFA," said Paclik, who said some time ago "if Pelta becomes President, I'm selling the Plzen club."
In the event that Paclik triumphs in Thursday’s vote, he should prove he could cooperate with Felta.
Rajchl, another candidate who had been supported by Paclik before the September General Assembly which had ended with a fiasco, thinks the same thing.
"He (Pelta) would be the first one I'd go with."
"He has great abilities"
"He would be able to present himself, for example, as head of representation."
"But as manager of the Association he wouldn't be capable."
"I don't put my nose into what I don't know," says Paclik, who in September drew a lot of attention when he spoke out for the cancellation of the Referees' Commission with Ludek Macela replaced by Dagmar Damkova.
Paclik claims that he would be at the level to lead the Association.
He takes as an example his abilities to run private firms and that he also manages since last year the club of Plzen which celebrated with his support last May his triumph in the first division.
"I have the strength to change something in the world of sport and to bring about a certain culture."
"I would curb the influence of some interested groups who, for example, have a tendency to influence the Disciplinary and Appeals Commission, which leads to situations of bad taste," added Paclik, who does not chease his antipathy for Sparta leader Daniel Kretinsky.
It would also consider harmonizing relations with the Czech Olympic Committee whose lobbying power with high-level policies would make it possible for sport to obtain more money from gambling taxes.
“That’s one of the key points the new president should deal with.”
“If it’s the gambling tax like the political parties say, I understand their arguments but they should also say where the money goes,” he said.
Paclik does not take his current affair with Plzen's club as something negative even if he himself claims that the President of the AFRT should be "above the parties".
Pelta is again linked with Jablonec and Rajchl with Dukla of Prague.
"It's not ideal."
“I don’t see any reason to pass on my actions from Plzen’s club to someone if I have to come back a year and a half later,” Paclik said.
Pelta has promised new statutes for AFRT that are expected to end patient situations.
Two days before the General Assembly of the Football Association one of the candidates for the Presidency Miroslav Pelta has promised that if elected, AFRT will have new statutes that should end in the future to quarrels over the settlement for elections.
Jablonec's official is ahead of Thursday's meeting in Nymburk very optimistic and thinks that today's success of the football performance is expected to calm this fight before the vote.
Pelta before the September election, which did not take place, promised precisely to devote himself to national representation and to obtaining financial means if he is elected.
He decided to be a candidate at the last moment just before the past General Assembly and this two-month delay could well allow him to defend his vision of the direction of football and to present it in a broad context.
"People at all levels have been playing football for a long time and are waiting until Thursday there's a President."
“They feel it’s the best time for someone to take over the management of the Association,” he adds, but he’s primarily connected to Jablonec’s club, has worked a large number of years at Sparta and the Union’s Executive Committee.
That’s also why he has enough experience leading Czech football.
He is convinced that behind the current problems with the election is a euphoria that prevailed at the time of Ivan Hasek.
"Unfortunately at the June General Assembly of the statutes were approved who were not perfect as all realized."
"If there weren't all these various interpretations we would have already had a President since September," Pelta said.
It was precisely the revision of the most important document of the civic association that took all the attention of the delegates.
"If I become President, I take it as a personal mission and am ready to take full responsibility if it doesn't."
"It's a principle without which we can't exist," Pelta added.
Then he strives, it is said, not to confront his opponents.
He has previously stated that he can cooperate with Jindrich Rajchl that he acknowledges with regard to his training in law and that he knows fully that his opponent-candidate would be his First Vice-President.
It has also not been attacking Tonas Paclik for a long time, which on the contrary does not stop criticizing Pelta.
"He's been playing in football for a year and he's already had a few successes."
"His attacks, however, weren't the most honorable and surprised many people during the campaign."
"That's also why some people now have doubts about his character," Pelta told his adversary but remains, he said, faithful to the proclamations he made in September.
According to him, his strongest weapon is his knowledge of football from the competitions of the lowest level until the national representation.
It should be a football window.
"And I would also try to finish all the cases that begin with that of the Bohemians or the continuation of the corruption scandal."
"I would immediately enter into negotiations with the public and private sectors so that we could get enough money for all football," the 46th candidate told Czech News Agency CTK two months ago.
He also added that his appointment at the head of football would not lead to any change of people.
"I should get acquainted with all employees," he told Strahov, where there has been understandable nervousness since there was no direction.
Although there are catastrophic scenarios planned with the General Assembly, he thinks delegates will not go to Nymburk for nothing.
"If there's a single point of discord about how to vote in the third round we'll be able to overcome it."
"Football needs credibility and it won't get it until there's a new leader at its head," Pelta said optimistically two days before the day, which was probably a defining day for his career as an official.
Rajchl promises that under his leadership AFRT would begin to work hard
"If current Vice-President Jindrich Rajchl is elected new President of the Football Association of the Czech Republic at Thursday's General Assembly it wouldn't please many people.
Rajchl promises, however, that in the event that he is elected we will start working hard.
He wants systemic changes and to make the members of the Executive Committee accountable for the different areas of football.
He also vowed to settle the Bohemian affair, improve relations with UEFA and FIFA, bring more money to football and continue the work started by predecessor Ivan Hasek.
"As President I want to take care of football as a whole."
"International contacts with UEFA and FIFA through national representation, professional football and successful football for young people."
“But I’m not saying I’ll do all of this alone,” Rajchl said in an interview with CTK.
"I'm a team player and want to distribute the skills within the Executive Committee so that each member takes care of a specific area."
"And I'll hand out difficult missions so that football faces a big step forward."
"Many people won't be happy because it's going to start working hard rather than criticizing others," he added.
He said he wouldn't represent himself as a candidate because of a critical atmosphere.
"About me, we know I work 20 hours a day."
"but I need to know if it's worth it and if I'll have people behind me who'll constantly put sticks in my wheels and cast doubts on each of my actions," he said.
"Many people told me I shouldn't give up because they'd give up too."
"It's a trust I can't betray," he said.
What motivates him, it is said, is also to continue the work begun with Hasek.
“Over the last two years there’s been a lot of work and I don’t want to drop it.”
"There are processes that have been launched that will have consequences in a few years and I would not like someone to arrest them," he said with the fear that in the case of the election of Miroslav Pelta the situation within the Union would return as it was before Hasek.
"I believe in systematic work and that's something Mirek Pelta can't do."
“Behind him there are people who would play football in such a way that it would only be for those who have been chosen.”
"I can be blamed for a number of things but surely not supporting someone."
"I'm trying to be objective."
"I want to make football for everyone because everyone will be happy not only those who have been chosen
And he again confirms that he would only be a President for the Czech Republic.
"And if there are any who say otherwise, I'm ready to do the maximum for Moravia."
"For example, it's time to consider that the second division is divided into two parts one Czech and the other morave whose winners would find themselves in the first division."
"There would be no situation like currently with only three Moravian teams in the division because the problem is currently to find young players.
As Tomas Paclik, president of the Plzen Club, has entered the competition for the position of President, Rajchl cannot evaluate his own chances of winning.
"I constantly feel support from the Czech House that has elected my Vice-President and knows that with me there will be successors," said Rajchl, who envisions among his future successors to save tens of millions of crowns on disadvantageous contracts, contract negotiations with televisions and on the gambling law.
On the contrary, he says that the fact that the Association is not united is a fault common to everyone.
As he says about Pelta "that he is disqualified because with him the Czech football would lose his credibility", he thinks the second opponent Paclik is a strong candidate who has something to offer football.
"No one can doubt Plzen that he did a great job this year."
"If he were elected he would surely continue in what was started by Hasek and would not make a noticeable change among the people in the Commissions."
“He wouldn’t want to mess with his successes,” said Rajchl who evaluated his rivals.
Europe's Most Beautiful Swimming Pools
Leap in the Blue
by Julia Stanek
UFO optic paddling pools, beautiful Budapest swimming pools: Traveling across Europe can experience its blue miracle – and after turning the city or taking a long walk – can let its tired legs rest in sparkling water baths.
A book shows in which swimming pelvis you can experience the most well-being.
When Iris Meder went on a research trip for the last 18 months, there is an object that should not be missing in his luggage: his swimsuit.
Extended on her back in the water, she observed the artistically arranged domes, and swam against the Putten marbles waiting for her on the other side of the pool and did not fail to admire the meticulously restored Art Nouveau of these converted pools.
If an indoor pool pleased her, she noted in her notebook without delay all the details worthy of interest on the type of construction and on the history of the building.
More than 200 pools have been surveyed in this way by architectural historian Meder and she presented them in her book already published: "Badefreuden (Joies du Bain". The municipal indoor swimming pools of Münich, the historical bath palaces in the black forest, "Hardcore concrete buildings" in the Taunus.
It has conducted its "travels to the most exceptional baths of Central Europe" in 13 countries. Next to Germany, Austria and Switzerland, it has visited places respectively in Italy, France, the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Slovenia, Hungary, Romania, Poland and a city in Luxembourg, Serbia and Croatia.
The 46-year-old German is a real survivor of bathing culture.
Meder, explains that she doesn't have a lot of interest in the sport but she feels attracted to the water in a totally different way.
It’s irritating that swimming fans should just contemplate blue tiles and accommodate from the stink of bleach while joggers exude bewitting scene of spruce forests or can listen to cheerfless of birds from the park.
"I once wondered why swimming pools actually have to always be so ugly," she said.
"So I started a collection of beautiful baths."
The surface of the water doubly reinforces the architecture
Diving into a swimming pool and volatile sauna oils - well-being factor in swimming rooms are huge because all senses are awakened, according to Meder.
And if the builders would've given themselves a little more trouble, the swimming rooms could be a real delight for the eyes: "Instead of a floor we have a surface of water in the swimming rooms."
"It's on a transparent side, but on the other hand reflects the beauty of the entire building."
All surfaces are optically in a double way: high vaults, multi-colored tiles, springboards.
The small-format image volume "Badefreuden" makes a report worthy of interest of 190 pages on the history of architecture and culture on the oasis of historical well-being - it certainly makes you want to make a leap in blue.
The collector Meder has discovered remarkable copies during her research in Hungary.
Next to splendid wellness baths such as the 100-year-old Széchenyi bath in Budapest, she found nitrate-containing waters in a natural karst (cavern bath in Miskolc-Tapolca) as well as a bath of thermal baths whose pools are filled with water containing alcaly and (Városi Termá
On the other hand, the outdoor heated swimming pools of Switzerland and Austria offers spectacular views. While from a roof of the Zürich bath you have the whole city in your eyes, you can admire the snow-capped Arlberg from an outdoor swimming pool in St. Anton: The Turkish bath is equipped with a window from which you can observe the excitement on the ski slope.
Perhaps the most extraordinary pool design awaits swimmers in Längenfeld: The Aqua Dome Ötztal looks like a UFO that has just landed on the Alps.
In winter, steam rises from the three open plateaus, while in the background rise the mountains of Ötztal towards the sky.
Alhambra in the Black Forest
The smell of bleach does not emanate from all the pools in the Meder collection.
Many thermal baths emanate an odor of sulfur, from Árpád-spa in the Hungarian Bekescsaba emanates a strong smell of oil, which it is hard to believe, to find yourself in a healthy spa.
The odor comes from therapeutic alkaline waters containing carbon hydrogen which is almost black and its silt sticks to your skin as soon as you let yourself slide into the pool - a special spa experience.
During her quest for the beautiful swimming pools, Iris Meder also suffered disappointments: When she wanted to visit Bad Luhatschowitz, a free and open-air outdoor swimming pool in Czechoslovakia, she found herself in front of a barricaded field.
The bath designed by Dušan Jurkovic architec in 1902 has meanwhile been closed.
Taking a look through the window of the Art Nouveau building in red-white-yellow, she observed the development of weeds between tiles.
She observed an opposite effect in a charming pool in the Black Forest: Bad Wildbad Thermal Bath Palace.
This building has already survived three phases; we can discover not only neo-Roman elements that come out since its conception in 1844 but also a Moorish room in Alhambra style that was added to it in 1900.
Since its careful modernization in 1995, the thermal palace has remained the favorite bath of the pool expert, Iris Meder - and not only because she is from Schwarzwald.
“It’s already a great yield to blend such different building styles into such a great unit,” Meder says.
With such a palace, diving into the thermal pool is apparently only a minor issue.
Agricultural Speculation
The German bank examines in details the speculation on raw materials
by Christian Teevs
Is the German bank abandoning its speculation on commodities?
According to information from SPIEGEL-ONLINE, Josef Ackermann has tasked a working group to seek the consequences of speculation for the world's poor.
In January, the leader of the group then wants to draw consequences.
Hamburg -
For the citizens of the Federal Republic, this is clear: According to a study, 84% of Germans find it unacceptable that banks speculate on raw materials such as maize and wheat.
Two-thirds of respondents even demand that the German bank and other credit institutes disrupt this avenue of business because it increases the problems of the world’s poorest.
This is the result of a Forsa survey on the order of consumer organization Foodwatch.
The study builds on a report presented by Foodwatch in mid-October.
Author Harald Schumann points out in this article that stock market speculation on global commodities pushes prices upwards and that banks share complicity in global famine.
The consumer protection campaign aligns concretely against Deutsche-Bank leader Josef Ackermann, even if big banks like Goldman Sachs or Morgan Stanley are acting the same.
Speculation on raw materials was initially introduced to protect merchants from price fluctuations.
But the majority of experts in the meantime share the opinion that bettors have detached themselves from offers and demands - and benefit the very poor.
Ackermann reacted to criticism differently from U.S. competitors: He promised to investigate reproaches and said, "no business is worth putting the good reputation of Deutsche Bank at stake."
According to information from SPIEGEL ONLINE, an international working group is currently reviewing Foodwatch-Report's accusations.
The review must be completed by the end of the year, then the results of Deutsche Bank's Board of Directors must be presented - with recommendation, usually.
At the end of January, the head of the Bank will indicate the consequences to be drawn.
This could result in a reduction in the raw materials market, or even a complete abandonment.
Ackermann builds his Image
Foodwatch CEO Thilo Bode hailed the news, but at the same time confirmed his criticism of the financial sector: “Spelucation of rising food prices shows in a particularly draconian way, how today’s banks are harming the general interest.”
Ackermann is the first banking lobby to be particularly under obligation today.
“Deutsche Bank needs to remove all investments from its portfolio, for which money has been invested on food speculation,” Bode said.
"It doesn't matter to Mr. Ackermann, whether it's about hungry people or whether Deutsche Bank's reputation is at stake."
Ackermann’s realization may seem surprising at first glance, but it’s clear that the group’s CEO wants to present a perfect picture, a few months before leaving office.
Deutsche Bank announced Monday that the Chairman of the Board of Directors will not change what is planned within the Board of Directors.
It could improve its brand image with an abandonment of controversial agricultural speculation.
And with regard to possible losses, he does not have to care - his successor will be concerned.
One out of tweves would subside investments on raw materials.
Foodwatch strives to maintain strong pressure on the Manager.
So far more than 30 000 Internet users have already participated in the organization's campaign.
Bode published the survey Tuesday, with which on 7 and 8 November the Forsa Institute made questionnaires among 1001 people.
Just eleven percent have found it legitimate for Deutsche Bank to offer investments for which bets on food prices are made.
Many of the bank's clients would also derive personal consequences if they learned that their bank participated in such operations: one in two would terminate, following the survey, all investments for which money is injected into commodity speculation.
43 per cent would not recommend such a bank to those around them, 49% replied that they would think about the termination of their account to change banking institutions.
21st House in Vienna
Pavilion who moved
By Ingeborg Wiensowski
A history of architecture with highs, lows and a Happy End: the Austrian pavilion for world exhibitions in Brussels in 1958 has won prizes, has been a museum in Vienna, has remained emptied and then fallen into ruin.
Now it is open again and is home to an exhibition house.
Enthusiasm for modern post-war architecture, for its clear forms, glass sheds, bright and daring appearances and new building materials that are typical of the belief in technical progress, then the pavilion of the Austrian architect Karl Schwanzer for the Universal Exhibition in Brussels in 1958 is a good and famous example.
Schwanzer Pavilion was once awarded the "Grand Prix d'Architecture" for its steel and glass construction, lightweight and suspended.
Construction is considered an important milestone in contemporary architecture until today.
He made the architects so famous that he also built the Pavilion for the following Austrian Universal Exhibition.
With its legendary four-cylinder home of BMW and its adjacent bowl-shaped construction in Munich, it eventually became internationally famous in 1973.
His pavilion project as a temporary construction had been exhibited after the Universal Exhibition in the Swiss Garden at Vienna’s South Station, was transformed, and in 1962 a 20th-century museum opened –
But the building was neither physically nor technically suitable for a museum - no walls to hang works of art, so not a good environment.
However it has also long been used, until contemporary art received a new building in Vienna.
Since 2001, the building has been empty and has become increasingly dilapidated.
Now architect Adolf Krischanitz has transformed the Pavilion, it now bears the name "House of the 21st century" and will in the future be used as a museum for contemporary art.
Tuesday evening the Belvédère museum opened, to which the house belongs, the first exhibition - a stroke of luck from any point of view.
The new start was led by Agnès Husslein-Arco, who became the new director in 2007 of the Austrian gallery Belvédère.
Husslein-Arco, promoted an art historian to a considerable career, among other things, in her youth as a figure skater, subsequently at Sotheby's, the Guggenheim Museum and as founding director of the Museum of Modern Art in Salzburg, she carried out particularly the work of transformation with politicians and the protection of the monuments, she obtained public and private sponsorship funds.
Apart from contemporary art, the works of the sculptor Fritz Wotruba whose foundation bears the same name have been exhibited, a café and a bookstore can be found there, and an office must be built there.
Strict supervision, small budget
For realization Krischanitz was therefore the ideal performer.
Not only because he had transformed and expanded the secession of Joseph Olbracht with sensitivity and had won the competition, but because he had studied at the Technical University of Vienna and for him the pavilion was "an extremely important place" where he had seen "virtually every weekend" the exhibitions.
He knew the original project, and he was aware of the problems of the edifice, which lost in generosity and lightness as a result of his trip to Vienna and his change of use.
During the current transformations, there were big problems: the building was "a chasm in energy", says Krischanitz.
The window profiles of the glass facades were changed, glass-fiber reinforced insulating glass was installed and the roof replaced by a particular safety glass.
The beavers of the thermal bridges have been reinforced or replaced, and asbestos-filled coatings have been rehabilitated.
Two basements have made room for the new operation, a panel of sifted light between the house and the road ensures the lighting, a bridge leads to the entrance - it is almost a new construction, but still the light Schwanzer Pavilion.
The original exterior doors have remained, the house is painted in a hue protection against red-brown rust.
And the movie theater is even exactly identical to what it once was.
And all this under the close supervision of the competent authorities for the monuments and with a small budget.
“Often materials that are good and inexpensive had to be found, and that also please the office of protection monuments,” says Krischanitz and he draws attention to raw soil in the basement.
Despite all the constraints, problems and transformations, the building has remained supple and lightweight.
It would have been difficult with the Pavilion, "which came from Brussels," Krischanitz said recently, a typical "Drop-in-the-City" story.
Such an architecture is "always in the wrong place" and still wants to "awaken the good conscience".
But in this case the House of the 21st century is not in the wrong place, because if it is a stroke of luck of urban construction: around the Swiss Garden stands the "neighborhood of the newly planned central station", with offices and apartments.
And another stroke of luck may happen: the House of the 21st century could benefit from the reinforcement of another construction Krischanitz, because the collector Francesca von Habsburg has bought the Berlinese temporary art center, and as it says, it is already negotiating with the city in order to obtain a site in the vicinity of the Swiss Garden.
Bahn is planning a winter crisis package in millions.
The railway company has developed a crisis plan, which aims to prevent train accidents in the winter.
The number of employees should double.
It's costing millions.
In the fight against train accidents related to the winter season and delays, the railway company "Deutsche Bahn" will spend more than an additional 70 million in this regard this year.
The newspaper "Bild"-Zeitung reported beforehand by invoking an internal crisis plan to the group, that investments of a total of about 300 million euros would be planned until 2015.
This should allow better availability of vehicles and the railway network also in the case of extreme weather.
Among other things, the number of internal and external employees for the snow removal of railway facilities and docks is expected to double to 16,000 people.
The goal is to rid the docks of snow and ice until the start of the service phase and 90 per cent of the lane devices required for service within four to five hours.
Two months ago, Station Manager Rüdiger Grube warned the government and the industry at a conference at the top of train accidents that also occurred this winter.
Last winter the railway company had a lot of problems for the transport of users, because it lacks, among other things, reserves in case of difficult weather conditions.
For example, many new regional trains have been awaiting authorization from the Ministry of Railways for years.
In addition, there is a delay in the delivery of ICE trains.
The railway company has had something to do for years, since it has to have the axles of ICE trains checked significantly more often in the workshop, after an axle failure has taken place on an ICE in Cologne Central Station.
German bank "Deutsche Bank" pays millions of penalties in the U.S.
Following the affair of Chief Josef Ackermann there is something new in the United States: the German bank "Deutsche Bank" has to pay a fine amounting to millions.
As if Deutsche Bank’s problems weren’t already enough, the past of this giant of the sector catches up with it in the United States: the Frankfurt institute pays in comparison 145 million dollars (106 million euros), to settle a dispute due to the bankruptcy of five major cooperative banks during the financial crisis.
It's the sale of mortgage documents.
The financial markets regulator NCUA brings to the big banks a whole series of accusations of encouraging cooperative banks to make false promises regarding the purchase of financial products; they would have minimized the risks.
These documents lost enormous value during the financial crisis and dragged banks down.
“We are pleased to have been able to find a solution on this subject, without the parties beginning proceedings in court,” a Deutsche Bank interlocutor in New York said.
In comparison, the Bank did not acknowledge any faults.
This also applies to the Citigroup, which is committed to a payment of US$20.5 million.
NCUA President Debbie Matz welcomed concessions from both banking institutions.
The National Credit Union Administration (NCUA) is responsible for cooperative banks in the United States and intervenes in the event of bankruptcy, in order to protect customer deposits.
NCUA is trying to repair multi-billion-dollar damages.
The comparisons concluded now are the first in their genre.
The regulator also took other major banks in part and in the summer he sued JP Morgan Chase, the Royal Bank of Scotland and Goldman Sachs in court.
With regard to the issue of mortgage documents, this is "Mortgage-backed Securities".
The credit houses are the basis.
When the U.S. real estate bubble broke out in 2007, this was fatal for a large number of banking companies.
In September 2008, at the peak of the financial crisis, the U.S. Lehman Brothers investment bank collapsed.
Even if the events are already a few years old, at the moment it is blowing on the American banks a stormy wind.
Investors and supervisory authorities have filed numerous lawsuits to obtain redress or to punish breaches.
The Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA) has sparked the largest wave of complaints.
It blames 18 major international banks, the two U.S. real estate financiers Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, for capitalizing in in-kind cases of mortgages of about US$200 billion.
The Deutsche Bank must also file an appeal with the FHFA, this is several transactions with a total amount of US$14.2 billion for the years 2005 to 2007.
Supervisory authorities are calling for the Frankfurter to address "substantial losses" but without quoting a specific amount.
Deutsche Bank had rejected the claims as unjustified and said it wanted to defend itself.
WARREN Buffett grows 10 billion at IBM
Buffett, the finance guru, invests colossal sums in IBM’s TI company.
Since March, it has bought shares worth more than ten billion dollars.
So far then the big American investor Warren Buffett constantly explains not investing in IT companies, since the long-term evolution of the companies show only bad predictions.
However, he read IBM's reports and changed his opinion on investments in this sector.
It had already had to understand much earlier that IBM mainly offers services and IT systems for the IT sectors of other companies.
An investment by Warren Buffett is seen as a knight attack in the world of finance.
As a result, IBM TI is granting itself the right to now be called "Mr. IBM".
Because Buffett revealed today to the CNBC economic show, which he has bought since March IBM shares worth 10.7 billion dollars.
Its investment holding company Berkshire Hathaway has thus become a major shareholder of IBM with a share of 5.5 percent.
Buffett said that IBM has never known anything about this commitment so far.
He praised the management, which even during the economic crisis ensured reasonable benefits.
"You've done a great job," Buffett said strategically.
For some time now, IBM has been relying on lucrative IT services as an operation of accounting centers.
The 100-year-old IT pioneer offers both Software and consulting and high-performance enterprise calculators.
Buffett had so far avoided tech companies.
He says he only invests in the companies he understands.
But on the contrary, Buffett rather tackles railway freight, manufacturers of lubricants or machines.
Its Berkshire Hathaway Holding has nearly 80 subsidiaries as well as shares in a series of major groups such as Coca-Cola or Munich Re, the former Munich Rück.
The 81-year-old octogenarian had taken over in the 60's the small textile company Berkshire Hathaway and erected it as one of the world's most prestigious companies, thanks to smart investments.
However, his lifestyle is modest.
That’s why he’s a cult figure for many investors around the world.
His almost infallible sense to make money earned him the nickname "Oracle of Omaha".
After the announcement of Buffett’s investment, IMB equities grew by one percent on the stock exchange.
“Big Blue,” as IBM, has a market value of more than US$220 billion, and with Apple and Microsoft among the world’s most valuable technology companies.
At Microsoft, says Buffett, he doesn’t want to participate.
Founder Bill Gates would be a very good friend.
The threat is palpable
After the discovery of the "Zwickauer cell" the Turkish media doubt the German rule of law.
A newspaper even speaks of a "bloody ideology", which would now be reborn.
The killings of eight Turkish nationals and a small Greek company in the years 2000 to 2006 had an extremist context.
As a result of this news, Turkey and the German-Turkish population are reacting with fear and concern.
Because for many German-Turkish the spectre that was thought to have been dead for a long time reappears: since Mölln's inflammatory attacks in November 1992 and in Solingen in May 1993, there has been no violence comparable with this right-wing extremist context against the Turks.
That there is xenophobia in Germany, it has never been mentioned.
However, the right-wing extremist network that has been detected reveals proportions whose size is far from foreseeable.
"Is this a return to the bloody ideology", "This was the title of the publication on the internet of the Turkish daily "Haberturk".
On the other hand, the newspaper "Sabah" points out that one of the authors would have been a v-man of the Protection of the Constitution, and is concerned that the German authorities could be involved in the far-right quagmire.
It is in a reasoned way that the Turkish community in Germany reacted: she called this weekend for a silent commemoration to the victims of racial violence in front of the Brandenburg Gate.
It wasn’t until two weeks before that the Turkish community in Berlin and the German and Turkish politicians had thought of a ceremony of the fiftieth anniversary of the German-Turkish recruitment agreement.
It is with cheerful colors that the cohabitation had been staged, symbolizing a success story.
The reports of some Turkish media make the effect of a slap: "Germany's gift for the fiftieth anniversary" this is the title of an article in the Turkish platform - "Haber X", which refers to the context of the series of murders.
Also the German publication of the "Hürriyet" does not deprive itself of deriving unambiguous conditions.
"it's starting to stink," this is the title of Ahmet Külahci's column.
He points out that people would have been murdered, would have paid taxes and would have made an important contribution to the reconstruction of Germany through their work.
The action of the police was perceived very critically on the German-Turkish forums and they had subsequently debated the success of their research.
Why it has not already come much earlier to the mind of the German authorities to make research in certain suspect circles of the extreme right, are surprised by the perpetrators.
More than one is ready at least for rhetorical or drastic means: "If the case continues to be sloppy, there will be nothing but self-defense and armament."
“It will be the best self-defense.”
"Inner peace in Germany strongly depends on the quality of information and the relevance of the punishment of the entire network," writes "Selen".
What this means if inner peace in Germany is in danger, the attacks of Mölln and Solingen in the 90’s explained well.
The photo of the ruins after the Solingen fire is deeply rooted in German and Turkish memory.
For no other event has shown more clearly to the Turks living here that there is a tangible threat, and to the Germans, on which distrust and distance by acts of violence could lead.
However, the attacks sharpened our sense in the Turkish community.
Never repeat the mistakes of the past
In the past, it is above all the failure of German policy after the attacks that the Turkish press complained: Helmut Kohl refused to visit the survivors of Mölln.
Following the Solingen fire he sent a telegram of condolences to the Turkish head of state, was represented at the funeral in Cologne by his foreign minister Klaus Kinkel - Kinkel counted at the time in his speech, how many taxes produced the local Turkish population.
As if the value of a man could be valued on the amount of his tax expenses.
The current report of the Turkish media shows once again how deep the mistrust of many German-Turkish is towards the local rule of law since.
In addition to the efforts of further clarification and reassessment of far-right terrorism in Germany, a clear message this time is needed.
Zürich Lake Scandal Bank
The most adventurous bank in Europe is not found in Italy or France, but in Switzerland.
UBS has gone bankrupt almost in the financial crisis and has been skating from one crisis to another in the meantime.
It's a German who has to lead now.
That's certainly not what Axel Weber imagined.
Following his spectacular departure from the head of the Bundesbank; it was known in July that he would hold an almost as spectacular position in 2013: Chairman of the Board of Directors of the great Swiss bank UBS - a big step towards an unexpected and lucrative career.
Axel Weber has publicly rejoiced, hailed; Switzerland for its beauty and UBS for its recently achieved solidity and has passed friendly observations on future colleagues.
"UBS has passed the Turnaround."
"I look forward to the collaboration with Kaspar Villiger and Oswald Grübel".
And yet, as early as September, it was clear that everything was going to change.
An UBS securities dealer in London had hijacked all of the Bank’s security systems and soared about 1.7 billion euros.
UBS Board Chief Oswald Grübel, a well-knowned banker not only in Switzerland, had to leave.
of the Chairman of the UBS Board of Directors has since been considered suffering - and all ask: When does Axel Weber finally return?
We have already learned that Weber can be advised by the bank from February 2012.
And he will resume the presidency of administration already in May - not in 2013, as the bank announced Tuesday.
For Axel Weber it becomes difficult.
He's not a banker, much more professor of economics and president of the bank, and he would certainly have needed a period of adjustment.
Suddenly he assumed responsibility for the strategy of a bank, which had just proved once again that it had long since earned the title of "the largest scandal bank in Europe".
Indeed, UBS is on a huge side - until recently, it was the world's largest asset manager, and on the other hand, it has been skating for years from one scandal to another.
There seems to be saving the so proud Swiss bank.
It began during the financial crisis.
Until 2007 the UBS had an excellent reputation.
The image of the Swiss asset manager, silent and solid, but also cunning, she had combined it with ambitious objectives: she also wanted to become, in the field of banking investment, a very large bank.
So the UBS managed on one side in 2007 with 1.6 billion dollars more money coming from a well-to-do clientele than any other bank in the world.
On the other hand, it had advanced as an investment bank in the league of the world's largest banks.
That made an impression.
Former banker Hans Geiger and later banking professor for many years in Zurich says: "I have always taken UBS as a good bank model in front of my students."
"From the current perspective, one wonders: how could I have just been so wrong?"
He's deeply mistaken.
In 2007 it appeared that for the first time something was wrong at UBS.
In the middle of the year President Peter Wuffli withdrewed surprisingly, which was previously seen as a top intellectual as well as a brilliant banker.
A few months later we approached why he was gone.
The division of the bank had flamed with sub-premise documents and had to make a cross on billions.
To support this, the UBS has announced a capital increase amounting to 13 billion francs and made a cross on it.
For this it has indeed found investors, but who, shortly before, would have frowned: a non-transparent Asian state fund from Singapore and an Arab state fund, which was overlooked, to the point that its name has never even been learned.
However, this was only the first blow.
In April 2008, this worsened.
The bank had to announce losses again amounting to billions, the chairman of the board Marcel Ospel, who was still in 2006 the second highest-paid board of directors in Switzerland, withdrew.
In October 2008 the disaster followed: UBS found itself, given its investment errors, in the financial crisis before bankruptcy and had to be saved by the state.
The national bank bought tens of billions of problematic securities from the UBS, the Swiss Confederation gave six billion francs for bonds convertible into shares, for which it could become co-owner of the bank.
The whole of Switzerland has been shaken - because it has noted how dramatic it can be for a small country, to house such large banks.
Finally, the total balance sheet of UBS 2007 amounted to more than five times Switzerland's economic performance (see graph).
“We couldn’t save UBS again,” said bank researcher Geiger.
After the first disaster he followed the second.
Already a few months before, the UBS had capitulated because of a tax dispute with America and declared itself ready to publish the data of wealthy customers, who had defrauded the tax authorities perhaps with the help of the Bank.
In addition, it has accepted a payment of 780 million dollars - in repayment of unjustified profits from these transactions with an increased fine.
Thus the UBS has more or less published that it has helped with tax evasion.
At the same time it has lost something that has always been its capital in competition with wealthy customers: the prestige of discretion.
Following the scandal, Swiss banking secrecy was no longer the same.
This was followed by the withdrawal of the head of the Board of Directors and the Chairman of the Board of Directors.
And exodus money from customers.
Thus the bank had lost in a few months its credibility in the two main business sectors: banking investment because of bad speculation and asset management because of aid for tax fraud.
And, as there was somewhat a return to calm, and as we thought, that the UBS would get away with it, it followed by this unfortunate history of security merchants: only one merchant of title blazes 1.7 billion euros, before the bank noticed that something was wrong.
And we publish: with risk management in investment banking it still doesn't seem to work.
"With the Adoboli case, we could see that the control of risks has changed too little," says Martin Janssen, economist and professor of finance at the Banking Institute of the University of Zurich.
Also the chief financial officer of a large German bank is sure: "the UBS problem is that it does not have control of the risks in hand."
However, however, this was exactly the idea of prudence, with which the Swiss bankers had long since taken office, that of becoming guardian of the great fortune of the world: not too great risks, but security for money - and the world could well collapse beyond the alpine republic.
The treasures of the Swiss banks had become the symbol of unwavering reliability as Lindt and Sprüngli are for a particularly noble chocolate.
The UBS plant in Zurich still goes hand in hand with the solid cliché of the private Swiss bank - it's only a little bigger.
Already the situation is classic.
The Bank has its headquarters at Zurich Central Station, which drives directly from Central Station to Lake Zurich, which itself is surrounded by the snow-capped peaks of the Swiss mountains.
The buildings here are prestigious, the rents of shops are among the highest in Europe.
Jewellers like Tiffany's, they can be found here between shops of Swiss traditions as well as those of the Sprüngli confectionery.
For centuries Swiss bankers have resided here: from the National Bank through the Credit Suisse Palace to elitist private banks like Julius Bär.
We're coming to the UBS lobby through a heavy door.
Inside, the walls are covered with marble, in front are thick leather chairs, and next to the logo of the bank, a large label for "safe".
UBS knows what the customer is looking for here: security.
This idea of the Swiss bank has one day apart from the market - or betrayed the two major Swiss banks, UBS and Credit Suisse, as the head of a small Swiss private bank finds.
He would prefer not to read his name in the newspaper, finally like all Swiss bankers he also does business with the two major banks.
The UBS would have become far too large, he finds - especially in banking investment, where the Anglo-Saxons would also have spread.
The Swiss would have nothing more to say.
"And even though, they have long adapted to the manners of the Anglo-Saxons."
This has indeed been the Anglo-Saxon investment bank that has changed UBS, it has made high profits and then the debacle: no European financial institution has lost so much money in the sub-prime jungle like the big Swiss bank.
The disaster became apparent on October 15, 2008.
"On Wednesday the small country's governing coalition with seven million people undertook the largest rescue operation in their nation's history," author Lukas Hässig wrote in his book "The UBS Crash."
With up to about 68 billion francs, i.e
How could this happen?
If we talk with Swiss bankers, they see the problem especially the size of UBS.
In June 1998 two of the three major Swiss banks concluded a huge group together.
A bank was created, which was already too big for little Switzerland - and recorded an ever-increase.
What has helped is that UBS’s two different predecessor banks have completed: a rich bank, but in lethargy with the strength of asset management in Zurich and a poorer one, but certainly already more strongly internationalised.
Suddenly, international investment banks had positive access to a lot of money, with which they were able to spin a huge wheel.
Two names are associated with the rise of UBS: Peter Wuffli, from 2001 to 2007 Head of the UBS Group, and Marcel Ospel, formerly Chairman of the Board.
“I’ve always thought Wuffli and Ospel were a dream team,” said bank researcher Hans Geiger.
"Wuffli, the great modest intellectual, who agrees very well in models and statistics, and Ospel, the intuitive, who made his way to the top with the learning of the bank."
But Ospel would have relied until the end in Wuffli.
And wuffli would probably have made in the end a decisive mistake: “He thought that his models were reality,” Geiger says.
"It's a tragic fall."
Tragic, perhaps.
One thing is clear: exuberance came before the fall.
UBS staff clearly remember the leaders’ meetings in which the leader of the Wuffli group always said the same slogan: dethrone Goldman Sachs.
The U.S. bank was the most powerful investment bank on the planet.
She ordered the "League Tables" the rankings, which at that time documented the success of the banks.
After all UBS wanted to go upstairs.
Some steps have already succeeded him.
In terms of currencies and securities transactions it has played a very important role.
Corporate advisers, who have had the responsibility, stressed, however, a weakness: in international interest transactions, in the "Fixed Income" banking jargon, there would be a huge difference compared to the largest banks.
UBS would have gaps in U.S. mortgage products.
To fill this gap, the UBS launched itself into an adventure, which almost cost him his life.
Thirsty for growth, it is both the bank itself and a management fund created before it in U.S. sub-prime investments that have invested.
It has turned into a massive machine that has packaged and passed valuable documents based on U.S. mortgages – but has also retained risks.
Even when other banks had been looking for a long time to go down.
"Still in the summer of 2007, when the U.S. real estate market was in flames, UBS bought there," says Geiger, the banking expert.
The greater the euphoria, the greater the fall.
One manager after another had to leave, when putting an end to the attempts proved more difficult than previously thought.
Positions at UBS have become ejectable and have so far been wobbly.
Whoever runs the big bank has an almost impossible task.
He has to find a strategy for a bank, for which all suggested business models have disappeared.
Banking researcher Geiger already has an idea: to put an end to investment banking, concentration to property management, return to Swiss roots.
But the bank doesn’t (yet) want to go that far.
But the investment bank must atrophy vigorously, as far as it is clear until now.
Next Thursday the head of bank Sergio Ermotti wants to present to the world his future strategy.
The 51-year-old Italian-Swiss was only supposed to be transiently when the bank partered with surprise from its predecessor Grübel.
In the meantime, it has imposed itself: it must carry out sustainable business of the UBS, said Tuesday the Bank of Zurich.
Ermotti wants to bring the investment bank back to the progress of the mid-1990's.
It cancels 3500 jobs.
But that won't be enough.
UBS needs a vision.
Who is she?
What does she want to make money with?
These are the questions that Axel Weber has certainly asked himself.
He says publicly until now only that he was going with his wife to look for an apartment in Zurich.
Next year.
