Smart crows spread to cities
In many places, frows are considered "bird problem °" But experts claim that these very intelligent animals no longer allow themselves to be hunted cities for a long time.
Some love them, some others want to get rid of them as quickly as possible. Crows have belonged to the city for centuries, but in many communities the complaints of residents multiply because of the dirt and noise of animals.
An antidote against the ° problem of birds is not yet in sight.
At the first ostfriesisch symposium on crows, experts said it is no longer possible to hunt cities.
Experts from all over Germany have deliberated on possible solutions.
They are looking for alternatives in cities
The protectors of nature based the problems of cities also on the exodus of particularly protected froxes: The use of chemicals in agriculture increasingly destroys traditional vital spaces.
Hunting, fitting out, and felling of typical brooding trees made life difficult for birds.
Crows are under the protection of nature.
There are large settlements with more than 600 pairs of brooding in the westfälischen Soest, in the north-west including Jever, Diepholz and Achim near Bremen as well as in Leer.
In 2005, a controversial project won negative advertisements due to the massacre of crows in this region.
Critics have found the capture of birds in traps and their killing with particularly brutal clubs.
"The normal eviction, the ousting, is useless," said Leer's city and landscape planner Werner Klöver.
The destruction of nests using pressurized water or cutting down trees, often produces undesirable consequences, environmental expert Manfred Kaiser from Lahr in Baden-Württemberg said.
"The animals move away, the large colonies divide and they disperse in other places."
The ousting is only imaginable in exceptional cases such as in hospitals, nursing homes or schools.
"We find ourselves obliged to live with them, we can no longer exterminate them."
The problem cannot be solved in the cities without the inclusion of the periphery, according to the opinion of Mayor Wolfgang Kellner of Leer.
Conceivable is a combination of the crowding of the animals to an aversive packaging and eye-catching offers, in order to allow them to relocate elsewhere.
Psychologist and ethnologist Uta Maria Jürgens prospected "news panels instead of chainsaws" in order to establish completely different relationships with: "We can ultimately better support a button in the face with a dose of self-confidence."
Jürgens co-founded the Ascheberger-cheminment-education of baskets in Schleswig-Holstein, road signs informing about the life of these very intelligent frews.
This project has meanwhile been adopted by tourists.
Jürgen gave his own opinion about criticism of the noise caused by birds: "Here's a lot of communication, these birds have a lot to say."
The intelligence and skill of animals are legendary: Freux not only use working instruments to arrive at a treat, but they make their own tools as instruments of work.
Ethologists at the University of Cambridge were testing four frews recently, the extent to how the intelligence of birds arrives.
In an experiment, the birds quickly learned that they had to collapse a scaffolding with stones to reach a larva.
Without much training, they clearly acknowledged that a stone must have certain lumps and shape to collapse the platform.
In a second trial, clever crows then had to solve a more stocky problem.
In a cylinder placed vertically, the researchers had placed a small bucket with a larva.
The birds could not reach their booty with their beak.
However, the researchers had put pieces of wire at their disposal.
All the four animals formed a hook with the help of which they fished the small bucket of the small pipe.
Three of the four frews succeeded even from the beginning.
