SAP wants to double the number of employees in China
Europe’s largest software company, SAP, intends to invest billions in China in the coming years and will almost double the number of its employees in the country.
Until 2015, about two billion dollars (1.5 billion euros) will be awarded, the Dax Group said Tuesday.
“We’ll create additional research facilities and development centers and hire employees accordingly,” CEO Bill McDermott told his clients on a fair basis in Beijing.
The workforce will grow from about 2,500 to 4,500 employees.
The number of branches of the world leader in enterprise software will double from five to ten or eleven.
So far, SAP has a presence in Beijing, Shanghai, Guangzhou, Chengdu and Dalian.
Approximately 4000 of SAP’s 176,000 global customers are located in this rapidly expanding country.
China’s growth plans are also an important part of Dax Group’s 2015 Strategy.
SAP plans to increase turnover bis 2015 to 20 billion euros.
Last year, the Walldorfer recorded a revenue of nearly 12.5 billion euros.
