SAP has been active in China for over 20 years.
“Now we want to expand our business ...,” McDermott said.
"°Our goal is to foster sustainable growth in China, backed by comprehensive information."
Personal Mass Slaughter
Peter Englund’s new book offers depth and generosity. Entitled “The Beauty and the Sorrow: An Intimate History of the First World War”, it begins with a long list of characters who shake your heart.
A tip to get started: tear this page thoroughly and make it your bookmark.
You’ll get to know these people in detail. In this novel story by Peter Englund, this list of characters will be your GPS, a beacon during those moments when, like one of his men or women, you will be troubled and hopeless in the mists of war.
Peter Englund is a Swedish historian and journalist.
He is also the new Permanent Secretary of the Swedish Academy, which awards the Nobel Prize for Literature.
He wrote here an out-of-the-ordinary book that he accurately describes as "a work of anti-history."
It has some well-known names, important treaties or famous battles; there is almost no ambassador or discussion about strategies and material.
It’s not so much a book about what happened, he explains, that a “book describing what it could look like.” You’re tracked up with “feelings, impressions, experiences and moods.”
"Beauty and grief" summarizes the wartime experiences of 20 more or less ordinary men and women, on both sides of the war, from young schoolgirls and botanists to climbers, including doctors, paramedics and sellers.
Some will become heroes.
Other prisoners of war. Some will lose members, become crazy or lose their lives.
Their lives wobble here like the candles of a church: while some are extinguished, new ones are rekindled.
Peter Englund's book departs from traditional history books.
He brings a denial to the idea that the First World War is summarized only by the terrible trench war on the Western front.
"Beauty and grief" skilfully describes other theatres of war: the Alps, the Balkans, the Eastern Front, Mesopotamia and East Africa.
The soldiers present in this book are under hives; one Christmas pass in Egypt under the pyramids; tse-tsee flies are an intractable problem.
This book is moving from the very first page.
The war invades the lives of these people like a natural disaster, like a Katrina hurricane that stinks the cordite.
When cannon shots ring in the distance, and you’re a stay-at-home, do you stay or run away?
Who'll come anyway?
Almost no one can understand what happened or even the reasons for this war.
According to Peter Englund, "the lack of facts has been fleshed out by assumptions, hopes, fears, fixed ideas, conspiracy theories, dreams, nightmares and rumors."
"Beauty and Grief" follows individuals such as Florence Farmborough, an English nanny within the Russian army, as well as Richard Stumpf, a young sailor from the German high seas fleet.
Their stories are mainly taken from memoirs, letters and other documents already published.
Versions of their lives can be terrifying or exciting, but are most preserved thanks to the accumulation of small moments and isolated details.
Most of them talk about deprivation and the ability to get away with it.
Recounting the story of a German schoolgirl, Peter Englund writes: "Ersatz, everywhere ersatz."
"Replacement coffee, faux aluminum, rubber imitation, paper bandages, wooden buttons."
He continues on replacement food in Germany.
It describes "meat made of crushed cooked rice in sheep fat (and finished with a fake wood bone); tobacco made with dry roots and dried potato skin; wooden shoe soles."
He claims that "837 allowable surrogacy meats are recorded in the production of sausages. There are also 511 substitute coffees."
Some stories are about honor and courage.
An American acknowledges his own drive towards savagery and says about the war: "You feel that after all, that's what men were destined for, rather than sitting in a comfortable armchair, a cigarette and a whiskey in his hand, reading a newspaper or a best-seller, and rather than pretending that such a varnish is synonymous with civilization, and that there's no barbarian behind your shirt
Conversely, a British soldier realizes that he is very likely to die, and that no one will notice or pay attention to it.
"When you're resigned to sacrificing yourself, you hope to have an audience," he wrote.
Unfortunately, it is rather comparable to "a convicted man strangled in secret".
Other observations concern the decline of an old Europe and new types of terror.
"Conflict is increasingly becoming a history of economic competition," writes Peter Englund, "a war between factories."
He grasps the arrival of what he calls "a new species within the bestiary of the beginning of the century: the well-articulated and ideologically convinced mass murderer, in well-cut clothes, who executes his butchery while sitting behind a counter."
People act unexpectedly; there are as many basic behaviors as there is heroism.
Peter Englund is talking about soldiers who have tried at all costs to catch a venereal disease with prostitutes in order to avoid servicing at the forehead.
"We find the most grotesque expression in the pus trade due to gonorrhea, which the soldiers buy and spread on the genitals in the hope of ending up in the hospital," he writes.
"The most desperate apply it to the eyes, which results in lifetime blindness."
In this Swedish translation by Peter Graves, Peter Englund's prose is supple but non-ostentatious, perfectly adapted to his task of humanity.
In dozens of short scenes, he seizes the way in which the war has "triggered exclusively uncontrollable forces: extreme nationalism, a social revolution, an aversion to religion."
People are beginning to wonder why their leaders are waging war on them.
The best works dealing with the First World War are often indirect, such as "Great War and Modern Memory" by Paul Fussel, or "To the West Nothing New", by Erich Maria Remarque, rather that complete.
Peter Englund's book is an unconventional pantheon.
The end of the book is the most overwhelming I've read in a non-fiction work.
I won't reveal it.
But it's as if he'd come out of his book, grabbed from your hands this page that's the list of people and burned with a match.
After a first misstep, a carefully planned operation
Hundreds of police officers have been involved, some wearing riot helmets.
The night from Monday to Tuesday was chosen because Zuccotti Park would be empty.
The operation remained covert apart from a few high-ranking officers as well as others informed from the outset that they were going to carry out an exercise.
Police Commissioner Raymond W. Kelly was at the heart of the operation, his presence highlighting the risks and challenges for the police department.
There could be no rehearsals of episodes in recent weeks, such as the use of pepper gas against protesters, who went against the department’s regulations and spawned a storm of public solidarity for protesters.
The operation to clear Zuccotti Park from its protesters was deployed after two weeks of planning and training.
The policies were prepared by observing the course of occupations in other cities.
A capital exercise was organized on Randall Island, with a view to the operation at Zuccotti Park.
Police officers receive better anti-riot training — anti-terrorist measures that quickly displace large numbers of police officers — to focus on the Lower Manhattan neighborhood.
The last training session took place on Monday night on the Manhattan side of the East River.
Orders given to move around the park came "at the last minute," a regular said, referring to the operation as "a simple exercise."
“The few police officers I know and who have been called did not know they would go to Zuccotti Park,” he said on anonymity.
"The only ones to know they're going there are at the top of the Police Department hierarchy."
The city was able to learn a lesson, hence the secret of the operation.
On October 14, police officers wanted to clean up the park but were pushed back by hundreds of protesters informed of the plans.
Tuesday’s operation involved officers from various police units, including operational forces from across the neighborhood – dozens of officers accustomed to particularly criminal neighborhoods.
Mr. Kelly said that a lot of people would come and go to the park during the day, in the manner of migrants, so an hour in the morning was an auspicious schedule.
"It was necessary to proceed when the park had the least number of people," he said.
Emergency service vehicles equipped with spotlights and loudspeakers gathered on Pipe Slip and Franklin D. Roosevelt Drive, near Manhattan Bridge, before intervening.
The resounding lights and recorded messages of the loudspeakers seemed to intimidate the protesters.
As community affairs officers entered the park equipped with their light-blue windbreakers, many protesters simply rounded up their belongings and left.
No tents were hit until 1:45 a.m
Other police teams were sighted on the perimeter, ready to intervene if arrests were necessary.
Journalists inside the park were forced to leave.
Paul J. Browne, the spokesman for the head of the police department, said the move had been taken solely by security.
But according to many journalists, the police prevented them from observing the intervention inside the park, harshly detained by the officers.
Browne said television camera trucks on Church Street, along the west side of the park, were able to capture footage.
As the police moved west across the protesters’ thick business clutches, including luggage, plastic turf and leaf bags filled with clothes, crews of the sanitation department were following, cleaning up everything the occupants left behind.
The few protesters who refused to leave were dragged. The images were disseminated on the Internet shortly thereafter.
The main group of protesters took seat near the encampment’s kitchen, not far from the center of the park.
Some have formed a barrier that the police have moved to stop methodically.
A dozen people at the epicenter of the encampment clung together.
Finally, twicks chained themselves to trees, Kelly said.
The emergency services were called to cut the padlocks.
No arrests took place in the park until 3:30 a.m. said Mr. Kelly.
According to police, the clean-up operation ended about an hour and a quarter later.
According to Mr. Browne, nearly 142 people have been arrested in the park.
Most of them for "disturb to public order and rebellion".
Joseph J. Esposito, head of the police department, is the highest-ranking officer on the scene.
Phil T. Pulaski, the head of the detective department, was also present.
Mr. Kelly, although present, "was not directing the operations," Browne said.
A second group of police officers were waiting along the east side of the park between Liberty Street and Cedar Street in the event of a necessary intervention.
Barricades have been laid on Cortlandt Street, a block north of the park, as well as Pine Street to the south.
We saw the Police widen the perimeter far beyond Zuccotti Park.
Several officers, most armed with shields, would have even been seen pushing people away.
There were nearly 28 people on the north side.
One of the brightest actions of the night took place south of the park.
Around 5:00 a.m
At one point, a piece of plywood popped up stealing from the crowd.
Finally, a police officer and a protester were hospitalized.
The school aid union sues the city for redundancies
The union that represents hundreds of school aid having lost their jobs last month plans to file a trial in the city for unnecessary and discriminatory lay-off on Wednesday. The impact is disproportionate on schools hosting underprivileged schoolchildren.
The lawsuits are probably the union's ultimate weapon, the 37th District Council, in an attempt to have the lay-offs cancelled.
Six hundred and seventy-twelve school aides, coordinators of parents and other employees, who are among the worst paid in the city, lost their jobs on October 7. Union leaders compared these layoffs to political revenge for not allowing the city access to a health care fund headed by groups of workers, which would have closed the budget gap last spring.
The municipal authorities categorically denied this accusation.
At a city council hearing last month, Chancellor Dennis Mr. Walcott said the lay-offs were a portion of the budget closed in June.
A member of the Council retaliated, mentioning that the redundancies of school aid were not specified in any way in the budget.
Officers of the 37th District Council said they would fill out a notice of claim, warning the Department of Education that it would be prosecuted; a news conference will be held on Wednesday to officially announce lawsuits at the Manhattan State Supreme Court.
Dennis M. Walcott and the city's Department of Education will constitute the defendant. The plaintiffs will be represented by eighty redunned workers, all of them black or Latin American in origin, like most of the school assistants who have lost their jobs.
Elizabeth Thomas, spokesman for the city's legal department, said the municipality has not yet been able to see the reasons for the trial and therefore cannot comment on the allegations.
According to a draft of complaints obtained by the New York Times, the union would focus on the number of layoffs in schools in the city's most disadvantaged neighborhoods to support its complaint against unfair treatment, stating that the poorest schools would lose the most aid due to regular underfunding.
While there are no layoffs on Staten Island, and just a few at Upper East Side or TriBeCa, 17 school assistants have lost their jobs in Brownsville’s 23rd District, and 46 in the 8th and 9th Districts in the southern Bronx neighborhoods.
Noting the loss of five out-of-eighh school aid workers at Harlem Public School 36, where 68% of schoolchildren live in poverty, the lawsuits claim that "It's not a neighborhood of well-to-do parents who can raise funds to fill the budget gap."
The budget of the public school 36 has been reduced by 3.26% this year. Funds have also been losted when the city changed its calculation of the additional funding for each registration of disadvantaged students.
The union also claims that high school principals may have been misled by certain terms in a memo authorizing the dismissal of parent coordinators.
The memo used the term "overtaking" to describe the action, even if "overtaking" more generally describes the action of moving a teacher from a school to a group of available workers whose wages are paid by the central office.
According to the draft complaints, "sixty-six parental coordinators have been dismissed", and "not only because of overtaking".
Mission completed: Congress reaches agreement on the issue of Libya
It seems finally that the Republicans and Democrats have reached an agreement at the Capitol on the issue of the uprising in Libya.
It took a successful uprising, the capture and death of long-time dictator Muammar Gaddafi as well as American involvement.
The Senate Foreign Relations Committee will have to make a decision next Tuesday, backed by President John Kerry (Democrate - Massachussetts), with Senators John McCain (Republicain - Arizona) and Joseph I. Lieberman (Independent - Connecticut), applauding the Libyan people for their revolt and American troops for their "courage."
“This resolution is about honoring the courage of Libyans as they begin to rebuild their country,” Kerry said.
"It's bipartisan support for their democratic aspirations."
Language may be harmless, but the debate on Libya has never been simple until now.
This summer, lawmakers from both parties came into conflict, as well as with the White House, over the wording of several resolutions aimed at approving the military role of the United States in the NATO mission in Libya.
In June, Kerry and McCain introduced a resolution to allow limited use of U.S. military forces in Libya.
The bill was defeated by a wide bipartisan margin and was never voted on in the Senate.
But on the same day of the bill’s failure, the Chamber of Deputies also rejected a measure to reduce funding for U.S. operations in Libya.
The U.S. mission thus remained in a form of legislative purgatory — Congress frustrated enough to criticize Obama’s management of the mission, but not enough to cut the grass under his foot.
The indecision of Congress was overwhelmed by the events.
Tripoli fell under insurgents in August and Gaddafi was killed on October 20.
The NATO mission was officially completed on 31 October.
The Senate can now take action.
The Foreign Relations Committee resolution “congratulates the Libyan people for their extraordinary courage and determination for freedom” and “congratulates the men and women of the U.S. armed forces and their coalition partners engaged in military operations to protect the Libyan people, for their fantastic courage and professionalism.”
The resolution also affirms “the U.S. national interest in reaching an irreversible and successful transition to democracy in Libya.”
Will the new measure win the full support of the Senate Foreign Affairs Committee, or will it divide legislators as in June?
Senator and Committee Chair Richard D. Lugar (Independent) objected to the latest version.
His spokeswoman said last Monday that Richard D. Lugar had not yet read the latest version of a new project.
Within the Capitol, the House is due to pass an amendment to the Constitution this week to secure a balanced budget, a legislative priority for almost all Congressional Republicans, and some Democrats.
The measure, backed by Republican Bob Goodlatte (Virginia), will constitute the First Constitutional Amendment relating to the budget, or any other question, to obtain a floor vote in the House or Senate.
Other legislators have so far failed to get their amendments approved, but it is not without having tried.
Goodlatte's amendment is one of 68 constitutional amendments to be tabled in Congress on a wide range of issues.
Some are recurrent - the same bill proposed in both the House and the Senate - and others are slight variations of a precedent.
There are at least 15 versions in the House only of the amendment for a balanced budget, and another handful that would "control federal spending."
Some of these bills are identical, but give their support - especially the new Republicans who wish to gilder their CV - the chance to boast of being the "author" of such a measure.
The Budget Control Act approved last August called for a vote by the House and Senate on an amendment to a balanced budget.
In March, 58 senators voted in favour of a non-binding resolution supporting the idea of such an amendment.
Beyond the budget, several members wish to limit the number of mandates during which members of Congress can serve, while José E. Serrano (Democracy - New York) wishes to lift the limit of two terms for presidents.
Jesse L. Jackson Jr. (Democrate - Illinois) has proposed a multitude of amendments guaranteeing the right to quality education and health care, among other issues.
An amendment prohibiting same-sex marriage is being perpetually proposed, as is the amendment prohibiting the desecration of the flag.
Some legislators also wish to authorize the annulment of federal laws if two-thirds of the states oppose them.
As eager as the members have been to propose amendments, they are not as fast as in the past.
At the 111st Congress, legislators proposed 77 amendments, and introduced 66 in the 110th.
The pace slowed compared to the early 1990s, when members generally proposed more than 150 amendments every two years, according to figures provided by the Senate Library.
But these amendments were not much more successful than they are today.
The last amendment to have been added - the 27th - was ratified in 1992 but proposed to Congress in 1789.
"Massacrated but not beaten," says Gabrielle Giffords in her first televised interview since the shooting
Ten months after an armed man shot him at close range in Tucson, Representative Gabrielle Giforfords (Democrat - Arizona) appeared Monday night in a TV show, sang "The Sun Will Come Out Tomorrow" of Broadway and said she wanted to recover above all rather than return to Congress.
During her appearance on the "20/20" television news on ABC, alongside Diane Sawyer, Gabrielle Giffords struggled to form sentences and needed help to walk.
The show approached his difficult return following the bullet that fractured his skull and pierced the left side of the brain, crossing his head.
Three months ago, she made a surprising return to the House of Representatives to vote on the debt ceiling.
In her first televised interview since the January shooting, which killed six people and injured thirteen others, Gabrielle Giffords smiled, laughed and sung – and called her recovery “difficult.”
After Diane Sawyer asked her how she felt, Gabrielle Giffords, 41, responded "rather well."
The interview with Gabrielle Giffords and her husband, astronaut Mark Kelly, took place during a one-hour special edition of "20/20", broadcast the day before the release of the couple's new book entitled "Gabby: A Story of Courage and Hope".
The short has traced Gabrielle Giford’s recovery since the January 8 shooting.
In videos filmed by Mark Kelly during the first few weeks, he can be seen on his hospital bed, his head shaved and a large scar on his forehead.
She manages to use only one or two fingers, with the encouragement of her husband.
A few months later, Gabrielle Giffords sat in a wheelchair as the doctors taught her how to perform simple actions.
A sign of the head.
A movement of the lips.
And finally, his first word: "What."
A few days later, Gabrielle Giffords whispered another word ("Toast") to ask to change her breakfast menu.
The videos released by ABC show the important role of music in Gabrielle Giffords' recovery: we see the Representative singing with the doctors the air of Cyndi Lauper "Girls Wanna Have Fun" and Tom Petty "Free Fallin"?".
In an interview with Diane Sawyer, Gabrielle Giffords, who spends two hours every day with the doctors, said she didn’t remember the shooting.
According to her husband, he was reading an article about the March 12 shooting when she halted him and asked for information for the first time about the six people killed in the incident.
The gunman, Jared Lee Loughner, is being held in a Missouri prison and vigorously treated while awaiting trial.
“There have been a lot of deaths,” Gabrielle Giffords told Diane Sawyer.
"Hard, hard, hard."
During her recovery, Mark Kelly told Diane Sawyer, she said, "I've been beaten."
"So I'd say, 'Gabby, you weren't beaten," Kelly said.
You've just been slaughtered.
And you're going to get away with it, recover and come back even stronger than ever.
More pity than respect for the troops
The event: a Wall Street gala to raise millions of dollars for homeless veterans in New York City.
Kid Rock sang a balad about helplessness, frustration and loss.
Hundreds of soldiers, sailors, pilots and Marines around him.
The crowd in suit-tie stood at his feet to acclaim him.
“The military, men and women, are considered heroes,” said David Saltzman, organizer of the springe fundraiser.
A high-ranking soldier present at the gala, in which Admiral Mike Mullen, Chief of Staff of the U.S. Armies, also participated, saw the role of the troops in a different way.
"They were portrayed as a kind of orphan," the senior soldier wrote in an e-mail.
I'm sure organizers had good intentions.
Well, I know that.
But it wasn't respect.
The absolutely contradictory impressions illustrate the difficult relationships that have settled between the military and a passionate but often distant American audience.
Praises were given to the troops for their sacrifices.
But they have a certain price, say a few soldiers.
The public acts more and more as if he is sorry for the men and women in uniform.
"We'ren't victims at all," said Brigadier General Sean B. MacFarland, who led the troops in Iraq and will soon leave for Afghanistan.
"But it would seem that the only way to support us is to lock ourselves in the role of unlucky spirits."
This is a very sensitive issue for military leaders, who do not wish to appear unrecognizable or at odds with the public they serve.
They also realize that the anger of troops returning from the Vietnam War has been much worse.
Thus, most conversations about pity take place confidentially among veterans.
Following the return of his two sons of combat with the Marines, retired Colonel Mark Cancian warned them that the public would consider their service from two perspectives.
Some would look at them with respect because they were confronted with insurgents and traveled to distant lands.
Others would wonder if “an angry and violent veteran hides beneath the surface,” said Cancian, who fought in Iraq and came back to work with the government in Washington.
When he was looking for a job, he said, he felt that a few people making him interviews subtly asked if he would be able to stand under the stress of a demanding job in Washington immediately after his return from the fight.
“When you address the subject of service, you’re faced with negative impressions,” Colonel Cancian recalls when he talks to his sons.
The difficulties of the military arose, in part, from American indifference towards wars.
The victories are seldom praised by such an unfamiliar country with the military, which sees few direct benefits from the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan.
“As a nation, we no longer value heroism in the same way as for the Second World War,” said retired Lieutenant General David Barno, who led the troops in Afghanistan.
Instead, congratulations from politicians and the people focus largely on the depth of a soldier’s suffering.
Troops are recognized for the number of periods of services endured, the number of friends lost or the extent of injuries.
The army in the face of mounting pressure against rapists
This week, a capital hearing will decide whether 28 men and women will go on trial against the military for alleged inaction regarding rapes.
If so, hundreds of complainants will prepare the next one.
First-class Soldier Elizabeth Lyman was 25 years old and 11 weeks pregnant the night she claims to have been raped by a soldier.
It was in October 2008. She was returning to her barracks at Miramar Air Station, north of San Diego, after having dinner with a friend, when she met a colleague carrying a pack of beers.
He asked her if he could meet her in his room for a drink.
"It was a wake-up call," says Elizabeth Lyman.
But the Texane has set aside her concern.
I thought, no, he's 19.
"He's from Texas," she said.
"I should've followed my intuition."
A few minutes after entering Elizabeth Lyman's room, the man grabbed her from behind, thrown to the floor and raped, she claims.
This week, on November 18, Elizabeth Lyman and 27 other soldiers, some still in service and others will anxiously wait for the court decision at a historic trial in Arlington, Virginia, which will decide whether a trial against former Defense Secretary Robert Gates and his predecessor, Donald Rumsfeld, will take place.
The latter are accused of not having stopped the generalization of rapes within the army, thus going against the constitutional rights of soldiers.
Ranked in February by Susan Burke, a Washington lawyer, the trial initially involved 17 plaintiffs. This number amounted to the next 28 months - 25 women and 3 men, all claiming to have been raped or sexually abused by fellow soldiers. They also claim that the army did not investigate, prosecute or provide adequate justice after the alleged attacks.
Susan Burke declares to attack the gilled soldiers, because the problem begins with them.
“The army is a vertical structure,” she said.
"Who are individuals in a position to eradicate the culture of reprisal? Leaders."
The lawyer also states that since February, she has been contacted by almost 400 other survivors, most of whom could be part of future lawsuits.
Her strategy: instead of bringing together all the plaintiffs in this case - thus putting all her eggs in the same basket - she will bring several lawsuits, if necessary, to maintain pressure.
The defense team, for the Department of Defense, led by U.S. Attorney Neil MacBride, tabled a motion to dismiss the case.
The court documents, obtained by Newsweek, detail the defense's strategy, and essentially the fact that the army cannot be prosecuted by soldiers on duty or not for injuries caused in the armed forces.
According to a 1950 Supreme Court decision, known as Doctrine Feres, the Federal Government is not responsible for injuries sustained by active-duty personnel.
"The alleged evil is inherent in the military service of the plaintiffs," the documents said.
The Department of Defense declined to comment.
Things have changed since the 50's.
While women still don't, officially, have the right to go to battle, this distinction is inscribed only on paper: the front lines of recent wars are rarely male.
In both Iraq and Afghanistan, teams of female soldiers are playing an increasingly important role, particularly in getting in touch with local women.
As a result: of these two wars, 150 female soldiers lost their lives – two-thirds in a combat situation.
It's time for the military to modernize says Susan Burke.
She is not alone in leading this fight: three other trials are ongoing in addition to hers.
At Yale Law School, the Veterans Legal Services Center prepares a case against the four major military academies for supposedly encouraging a misogynistic atmosphere.
On the other hand, the non-profit foundation Vietnam Veterans of America prepares a lawsuit against the military, focusing on diagnoses of "personality disorders" attributed to victims of rape in order to give them leave - a relatively ordinary fact, according to activists such as Anu Bhagwaru of the Service Women's Action Network, a human rights group.
His group sued the Veterans Administration in December, accusing them of discriminatory practices in dealing with claims for benefits for those who reported being the victims of sexual assault in the service.
Nine bills independent of each other have been introduced in Congress through a bipartisan mix of senators and representatives, proposing various solutions.
In 2010, 3,158 sexual assaults were recorded according to the Sexual Assault Prevention and Response Office centre.
But it is well known that the number of sexual assaults is underestimated. Indeed, according to the Pentagon's own estimate, this figure represents about 13.5% of the 19,000 incidents that occurred that year.
The report published in March also examines the percentage of legal proceedings: in 2010, 20% of cases recorded in the army were tried - half of the percentage of the civilian justice system.
Complainants in Susan Burke's case describe their assault as the first in a series of injuries.
In Lyman's case, she informs the Military Police of the rape less than an hour after the incident.
That night, she said, she was asked to tell various police officers, doctors and commanders 11 times in detail.
She underwent a "rape kit" (literally a rape kit), and the blood of her attacker - due to a cut on her arm - was found in her bed.
Six months later, in April 2009, Elizabeth Lyman sat before a judge, at a trial during which she was questioned about her sexual relations with her boyfriend earlier the day of her assault.
Six people testified as morality witnesses for the perpetrator, who ended up being acquitted.
"I remember the day when the verdict was delivered," said Elizabeth Lyman.
"I thought I'd start having contractions. I started screaming after the yard."
"I was placed in a psychiatric unit, and when I went out, I remember saying to my commander, 'This wasn't his trial but mine.'"
Elizabeth Lyman was forced to consult a military psychiatrist, who ended up diagnosing a personality disorder, rendering her unfit for service.
In January 2010, she was dismissed under less-than-honour terms, preventing her from receiving any benefits.
Is the Lyman case and other plaintiffs moving forward or not, it’s a long history, says John Turley, a professor of law at the Faculty of Law at the University of Washington.
However, he notes, "In my opinion, they should be able to move forward."
Only this doctrine criticized since its introductory is between them and a verdict.
I've always criticised the Feres doctrine.
The army finds itself decades behind because it does not have the same advantages and deterrences posed by responsibility.
"The odds are heavily against them... but it's important that they try," he added.
These things only change when good people have the will to fight.
If the trial doesn't turn in their favor, Susan Burke, the plaintiff's counsel, will gather her hundreds of other plaintiffs for further trials.
“We will continue to fight against the military on this issue as long as there is no reform,” she said.
Where until we die, one or the other of the two options.
Walls Have Eyes: Seekers Are Studying You on Facebook
Before becoming the new face of the far-right in Europe, Anders Behring Breivik was a simple guy expressing his anti-immigration opinions online.
On Monday, Breivik, who admitted to having been taken from a murderous madness in Norway in July leaving 77 victims, was confronted with his first trial.
While Breivik acted alone, he was far from it on cyberspace: he had spent much of the time before the attack on his computer, chatting with some of the millions of nationalists supporting far-right groups on social networking sites.
Following the tragedy this summer, the researchers want to learn more about these people.
But how to find them?
Easy... Connect to Facebook.
"We realized it wasn't that difficult to find them," says Jamie Bartlett, leading author of a report recently published by DEMOS' think tank on digital populism in Europe.
Facebook is full of so much personal information, Bartlett says that researchers could simply use the site’s advertising tool to find the people they’re looking for with scientific precision — the way mercaticians have been doing it for years.
Jamie Bartlett’s team discovered that half a million members are fans of far-right bands in Europe. It has targeted them with announcements, but instead of linking them to a new group or product, the announcements are inviting users to respond to a survey. The questions asked concerned their level of education, their attitudes towards violence and their optimism about their own future.
Coming into contact with these people through Facebook, Jamie Bartlett and his colleagues were able to interrogate 10,000 supporters of 14 far-right parties in 11 European countries – without leaving the office.
“It’s a new way of doing research,” says Jamie Bartlett.
Some results aren't at all surprising: online supporters of right-wing groups are rather men, young and against immigration.
But surprisingly, “those who combine online and offline activism are more reasonable, more democratic and less violent than those left behind their computer screen.”
DEMOS’s study is just one example of how Facebook has become the inescapable new tool in the hands of scientists.
Thought groups, medical researchers, and political scientists use the site to study all topics that affect health issues such as social trends, thanks to "Likes", publications on walls and status updates.
With more than 800 million active users adding an average of three content per day, the "data supernova" generates a search explosion, increasing the number of search work whose site name appears in the title by almost 800% compared to the last five years.
For some researchers, Facebook’s advantage lies in the fact that it allows you to study people you couldn’t approach on the street.
“Before, when you were trying to investigate the people of the British National Party, it was very difficult to identify them.”
“You had to go to the party, but they wouldn’t have allowed you in,” Bartlet said.
“Facebook avoids all of this – you have direct access to their information.”
Others say that the site could offer a way to identify and address health and social issues.
According to a recent study by the D. Megan Moreno of the University of Wisconsin-Madison, and his colleagues, the young students who were talking on Facebook about their intoxicated exploits were far more likely to be alcoholic than discreet students about it.
Megan Moreno suggests that student colleagues, such as advisors, could monitor the site and intervene to eventually help a student whose status would talk too often about alcohol.
“You can’t deal with a problem if you don’t diagnose it,” Dimitri Christakis, co-author of Megan Moreno and director of the Center for Health, Behaviour and Child Development at the Children’s Research Institute in Seattle.
"We know how to identify children at risk, who would not be recognized in another case."
Facebook also constantly accesses the information of its users.
The team of the site that handles the data has studied the statistics concerning the love situation and on Valentine's Day, the turnout in the 2010 mid-term elections and an index of national happiness (thanks to the information only available - the champions of digital freedom have long maintained that the site could make available its treasure trove of data to others for research purposes).
But Facebook users know that the site observes them, whether they like it or not – in exchange for being able to play Scrabble with a friend who is thousands of kilometers away, the site is allowed to access your information.
But what happens when Facebook users are part of an investigation they haven’t even heard of?
While researchers have well-defined guidance on how to collect information other than through the Internet, it’s something else online.
“I don’t believe that a large number of users have already thought about the fact that a researcher can study their profile,” said Neil Selwyn, a sociologist at the London Knowledge Laboratory, according to a 2006 study he conducted observing his students’ Facebook walls.
"As far as they know, exchanges were seen only by their friends."
Then comes the question of methodology.
Even without going through the Internet, there is no guarantee that the subject of a study is completely honest.
On Facebook, it is impossible to know the degree of truth of a profile or on the wall of a user.
“What you say on Facebook and what you’re doing in reality are two totally different things,” Selwyn said.
That's why, despite his experience as a technology researcher, Neil Selwyn thinks that a notebook and pen remain the best tools that can be used.
“Nothing can replace the real world and contact with people,” he said.
“Social studies are supposed to address the social aspect – and the social is not in front of a computer.”
"Cold and inhuman" : first appearance of Anders Behring Breivik
Anders Behring Breivik, the man who admitted to having staged the two attacks that killed 77 people in Norway this summer, described himself as a "resistance" fighter during his first appearance at trial in Oslo.
"I'm a military commander of the Norwegian resistance movement and the Templars of Norway," he said.
"When it comes to the jurisdiction of the court, I reject it, because you hold your mandate as organizations supporting an ideology of hatred and because it supports multiculturalism."
"I acknowledge the facts, but I don't plead guilty."
Judge Torkjel Nesheim interrupted Breivik during this monologue because he "did not want to give him the opportunity to use this trial to express his opinions."
For the same reason, he refused to leave Breivik, which brought with him a speech on paper, speak to the parents of the victims at the end of the hearing.
Judge Nesheim also asked the police to detain Breivik for another twelve weeks, forbade him to speak to the media for four weeks and warned him that the authorities would closely monitor all visits and correspondence for eight weeks.
About thirty relatives of survivors and victims were present at the hearing.
Some came in the hope of looking Breivik in the eyes, others to make sure that he would be placed behind bars.
All participated in the hearing in order to see the end of the trial.
"I found him cold and inhumane," a Utoya survivor told Norwegian television channel NRK.
"I wasn't comfortable, but I personally advanced a bit after seeing and hearing the suspect."
Dressed in a dark suit and a blue tie, Breivik remained calm and professional throughout the trial. He looked journalists and survivors in the eyes when entering and leaving the court.
Despite the strange idea that Breivik belongs to a vast "resistance" movement, the judge determined that he was not crazy. He also stated that there is no evidence that he acted with accomplices.
The July 22 attacks therefore appear to be the work of an extremist rather than an entire group of radicals, which may comfort some of them.
But this does not reduce the grief and confusion felt by the whole nation.
"I'd rather he looked like a monster, but he didn't," said a relative of a victim.
"It would have been much easier."
7 Lessons from Steve Jobs' Lost Interview at the Cinema This Week
After the death of Steve Jobs on October 5, some compared him to Henry Ford - distinguished by the technical genius and inventor of modern computing.
But what Apple fans can find most revealing in an interview with Steve Jobs recently found that will air this week in the first part of a film in major markets, is how much this creative spirit had understood the processes and workflows of products.
He was undoubtedly a creator and a dreamer. He identified with a hippie but was also very meticulous and organized, as well as an improver of organizational charts - a man who believed that many corporate executives were suffering from a "sickness letting them think that a brilliant idea does 90% of the work... [but] a considerable amount of manual work separates a great idea from a product."
In 1995, Bob Cringely developed the television series "Triumph of the Nerds". This series traces the birth of the computer, and it sat down for more than an hour with Steve Jobs for a long and rare conversation.
At that time, it had been about 10 years since Steve Jobs had had to leave Apple. He was already working hard on his new computer company NeXT, aiming for a popular adoption of the Internet.
While a small portion of the interview with Cringely is used in Nerds, he states that the main copies have disappeared in transportation.
It is only after the death of Steve Jobs that a complete video is found in the director's garage.
This video, slightly reassembled, is the basis of the 68-minute film entitled Steve Jobs: The Lost Interview, which will premiere at the cinema next Wednesday and Thursday.
For someone known for hating in-depth interviews, Steve Jobs here seems surprisingly excited to expose technological philosophies and his business strategies.
And incredibly attentive.
Out of four different points during the interview, the answers come a long moment after the questions - between 10 and 15 seconds of reflection, during which Steve Jobs clearly weighs his thoughts, in order to provide an accurate answer.
We also discover moments when the perfectionism of Steve Jobs resurfaces, when he seems eager to hear Cringely's questions, elbowing him to accelerate him and move on to questions or topics of greater importance.
Filmed before the era of Pixar, the iPod or the iPhone, there is a strangely prescient aspect on Lost Interview. Steve Jobs evaluates what he considers good or bad in other companies and shares his own vision on the future of computing.
It’s one thing to read the posthumous evaluations of Steve Jobs’ career, but there’s something raw and inspiring about the man we’re seeing here, the anxious dreamer on the eve of greatness.
He has a vision of how things can and should be.
He explains them and then immediately implements them.
Here’s a quick summary of seven outstanding sequences from Lost Interview:
About how he learned how to run a business:
“For all these years I’ve always asked, ‘Why do you do things?’ and the answers are constantly the same, ‘oh, just because you have to do them.’”
Nobody knows why, no one thinks deeply about things in a company.
That's what I discovered.
Steve Jobs goes on to give details of his efforts to simplify accounting at Apple.
Perplexed as to how the costs were recorded - we often start with a "standard cost" valued at a nose view, then adjusted with a "variation" - he developed an automated factory that guaranteed the precise calculation of commercial costs.
On the opportunity to play a trick on the Pope:
Steve Jobs tells the anecdote of the "Blue Box" he created with Steve Wozniak - a device that was used to hack telephone networks and phone for free long-distance.
The device has been the subject of many writings beforehand, but the euphoria of Steve Jobs here in his description of the Blue Box as an act of appropriation is contagious.
It describes how he and Wozniak tested the box using a public phone, making a call and then connecting from one AT&T network to another while bypassing as many satellites as possible.
"We wrapped the ball of different things half a dozen times and you were screaming in the public phone and it would happen to you a minute later on the public phone next door," Jobs laughed.
“We were young and thanks to this Blue Box, we learned that we ourselves could create something ourselves capable of controlling billions of dollars in infrastructure around the world.”
We didn't both know much, but we were able to invent a small thing that could control big things. It was an incredible lesson, and I don't think Apple would ever have existed without it.
Steve Jobs goes on to recount a wonderful trick that he and Wozniak developed: he phoned the Vatican with the Blue Box in the middle of the night pretending to be Henry Kissinger and asked to talk to the Pope.
While several members of the Catholic hierarchy are summoned in the middle of the night to speak to the American diplomat, the two accomplices go laughing just before the Pope himself is awakened.
On his love of technology (and Bill Hewlett canvassing):
At the age of 12, hunting spare parts to make a frequency meter, he searches for Bill Hewlett's number in the yearbook and gives him a phone call.
Shortly after their 20-minute conversation, Steve Jobs gets a part-time summer job at Hewlett-Packard.
"It had a remarkable influence on me. It was the only company I'd seen at that age, and it formed my definition of a company and how employees are treated."
Steve Jobs later became one of HP’s employees visiting the company’s research labs, located in Palo Alto, where he discovered the “first never-before-designed desktop computer.”
It was as big as a suitcase, had a small cathodic tube. I fell in love with it.
The teenager I was went back to HP and started writing programs for this machine.
On the Improvisation of Innovation:
At several points in the interview, Steve Jobs talks about inventing new products on a whim.
At the beginning of his career, as he installs to sell a few printed circuit boards, a customer asks him to assemble a complete computer.
Working at only 30 days of credit, he had to take care of the assembly as well as the delivery of finished devices.
Later, at Apple, he recalls the difficulties encountered in developing a computer mouse:
"I remember big fights... I was yelled at pretending that it would take me five years [to make a mouse that would cost $300]. I've had enough, I came out and went to see a designer."
Ninety days later, we had a mouse that we could make for $15 that turned out to be unbelievable.
On the big companies that are running out of steam:
Long before it revived Apple, Steve Jobs had anticipated how many industrial leaders were going to be wrong and lose control of their market share.
“Let’s say you’re working at IBM or Xerox, you’re making a better copier or printer, what next?”
You have a monopoly on the market, so the company is not more successful.
Sales and marketing can make it evolve, so [it’s these types of people] that end up running the companies and the specialized product staff are sidelined from decision-making.
Productive engineering that led to this position of monopoly is completely rotten by people who can’t imagine conceiving a good product compared to a bad product – the required manual labor.
That's what happened at Xerox...
Xerox could've owned the entire IT industry.
It could've been ten times its current size. Xerox would have been the Microsoft of the 90's...
They snatched defeat from victory.
On innovation as an artistic form:
"A huge part of manual work separates a great idea from an exceptional product... as you develop this idea, it changes and grows."
You learn a lot about its subtleties.
There are compromises to be made... Some things you can’t do, electrons can, glass, robots, factories too.
“You have to keep thousands of things in mind (these concepts) by assembling them together.
Finally, decisions are made out of personal taste. It is about trying to expose yourself to the best products that men have created, and trying to bring these things to the product you create.
Picasso once said that good artists copy and great artists fly.
We're always embarrassed about stealing great ideas.
This is partly what makes Macintosh exceptional. The people who worked on it were musicians, poets, zoologists, historians, who simply proved to be the best computer scientists in the world.
If it wasn't for computer science, they'd have done extraordinary things in another field.
Scalia and Thomas have dinner with opponents of health law as the court meets
The day the Supreme Court meets behind closed doors to examine the issue of political discord, as to whether opposition to President Obama's health law will be heard, two of his judges, Antonin Scalia and Clarence Thomas, were noticed at a dinner sponsored by the law firm that will defend the case in high court.
The event was last Thursday, when the nine judges met for a conference to discuss petitions for review.
One of these cases involved a lawsuit filed by 26 states challenging the ambitious health care removal passed by Congress last year, a law that has been a rallying point for conservative activists nationwide.
The judges have agreed to hear the complaint, indeed, a monumental argument of 5h30 is expected next March, and the result will probably promote the destabilization of the 2012 presidential race, which will be booming at the time when the decision of the court will be rendered.
The lawyer who will stand before the court and argue about the rejection of the law, will likely be Paul Clement, who served as Solicitor General of the United States during the administration of George W. Bush.
Clement’s law firm, PLLC Bancroft, was one of the two dozen firms that helped sponsor the Federalist Society (Federalist Society), a long-standing group dedicated to upholding conservative legal principles.
Another firm that sponsored dinner, Jones Day, represents one of the trade associations that challenged the law, the National Federation of Independent Business.
Another promoter has been the pharmaceutical giant Pfizer Inc, which presents a huge financial challenge in the outcome of the dispute.
The dinner was held at a Washington hotel a few hours after the court conference on the case.
There was, among others, Mitch McConnell, top Republican of the Senate and declared opponent of the health law.
Featured guests at dinner? Scalia and Thomas.
This is not new: the two judges have been present at the events of the Federalist Society for years.
And there's nothing that goes against the rules of ethics.
In fact, judges are exempted from a code of conduct that governs the actions of federal judges.
If they were, they would no doubt argue by the Canon 4C code, which states: "A judge can attend fund-raising of organizations related to law and others although the judge may not be an intervenor, a guest of honor, or appearing on the program of such an event."
Nevertheless, the mere presence of Scalia and Thomas in the vicinity of two of the law firms in the case, as well as a company with a massive financial interest, was enough to alarm government ethics activists.
“This violation of ethics and superb indifference to the code contradicts the statements of several judges that the Court complies with the same rules applicable to all other federal judges,” said Bob Edgar, president of Common Cause.
"Judges were drinking and dinning and at a gala fundraiser with lawyers who have pending cases in court."
Their presence and participation in the fundraising of this event dismantles any claims of impartiality, and is unacceptable.
Scalia and Thomas have shown little regard for critics claiming that they too easily mix court affairs with the agenda of groups such as the Federalist Society.
And Thomas' wife, Ginni, is a high-profile conservative activist.
Moreover, the Conservatives claim that it is Judge Elena Kagan who has an ethical problem, not Scalia and Thomas.
Kagan served as Solicitor General of the Obama administration when the first oppositions to the law were brought to the level of the court of first instance.
Her critics lobbied for Kagan to recuse to hear the case, saying she had invested too much in defending the law to be impartial.
Kagan gave no indication that she would.
Boeing records record US$18 billion order for airliners
The airline Emirates orders 50 Boeing 777, two-corrider airliners, with the possibility to buy 20 more.
Boeing also sells six Boeing Dreamliners to Oman Air, an estimated market of more than one billion dollars.
There's nothing extraordinary about a company receiving an order contemplating 50 of its products.
On the other hand, it is quite a matter when the company in question is the aircraft manufacturer Boeing and the amount of these 50 aircraft reaches the record sum of 18 billion dollars.
Just as unbelievable is from whom Boeing received this order: the Middle East, a growing golden mine of future aircraft orders.
In forecasts released Monday, the Chicago airline estimates that airlines in the Middle East will need in the next 20 years 2,520 aircraft with a total value of 450 billion dollars.
Over the weekend, Boeing briefed on the order of the most expensive commercial aircraft in its history - 50 Boeing 777 two-corrider aircraft.
It was the Dubai-based Emirates airline that placed this pharaonic order.
Boeing announced at the 2011 Dubai Aviation Show that the airline is also likely to purchase an additional 20 aircraft, which would raise the total amount of the order to US$26 billion.
On the other hand, Boeing said on Monday that Oman Air ordered six Boeing 787-8s, dubbed Dreamliners, a model that flatters significant fuel savings and passenger amenities.
The amount of these six devices amounts to more than one billion dollars.
"It's clearly very positive for Boeing," said Neal Dihora, an analyst at the Morningstar.
"These customers in particular have immense value."
In the Middle East, oil-producing countries own many of their airlines.
So they not only have the means to pay these planes, but an increase in the price of fuel - usually detrimental to airlines because it represents their most important costs - would not be so harmful to them since the country would make money with rising oil prices.
This is an important point because these companies will be less likely than others to cancel or defer their aircraft orders when the price of oil rises, Dihora reported.
"They're having natural risk coverage," he said.
Emirates’ order reinforces Boeing’s dominance in the wide-body aircraft market and helps curb the Airbus competitor’s encroaching attempt with its A350 aircraft.
Boeing's victory at the aeronautics show came four months after Airbus' announcement to postpone the debut of the largest version of the A350, which competes directly with the Boeing 777-300ER.
Airbus is postponing its debut to increase its surge following the demand of its customers to increase its charging capacity and autonomy.
Boeing’s shares rose 1.5 percent to US$67.94.
The sister of the obviously defeated Mexican president in the Michoacán elections
Luisa Maria Calderon states that drug traffickers have helped his opponent Fausto Vallejo of the PRI in the race for governor.
President Felipe Calderon's sister appears to have lost her attempt to take over the office of Michoacán's governor in violent elections, and she said Monday that drug traffickers favored one of her opponents.
Initial results indicated that Fausto Vallejo of the Institutional Revolutionary Party, or PRI, was at the top of the race as governor of this western state.
"The intervention of organized crime throughout the voting process and in particular yesterday is an alarming fact, not only for the Michoacán but for the entire country," Luisa Maria Calderon told the radio during an interview the Monday following the elections.
"They threatened our candidates, our electoral agents... They seized the polls, installed roadblocks ... and forced people to vote" for the PRI.
PRI ruled Mexico for seventy years until he lost the presidency in 2000.
But he organizes his return, and a victory in the Michoacán is an important step in this effort.
The PRI hopes to win the presidential election in July.
Vallejo seems to have stood out with only a few Calderon, who was at the top of the polls before the day of the elections.
Calderon, candidate of his brother’s conservative party, the National Action Party, or PAN, refused to recognize Vallejo as the winner.
Silvano Aureoles, left-wing candidate of the Democratic Revolution Party, or PRD, who currently holds the positions of governor, came third, a painful defeat before the presidential elections of 2012 for this divided and unhappy left.
Aureoles also refused to acknowledge the first results.
Vallejo, former mayor of Morelia, the state capital, denied any connection to drug traffickers and strongly advised the other candidates to accept the results.
Michoacán has long been dominated by drug cartels specializing in the trafficking of marihuana, heroin and methamphetamine.
This is President Calderon's state-of- origin, and he chose the Michoacán to launch a military offensive against traffickers in December 2006.
However, violence persisted.
A week before the elections, a PAN mayor was murdered while campaigning for Luisa Maria Calderon, and many candidates gave up the campaign locally out of fear.
However, for the PRI, as corrupt as the Michoacán elections look, a victory will help the party in its momentum.
The man we’re waiting to represent the PRI in the presidential race, Enrique Pena Nieto, congratulated Vallejo from Washington and said, “I think this victory is very encouraging ahead of next year.”
ASDB strongly urges Asia to help the eurozone
The Asian Development Bank has asked India and China to prepare to help the eurozone emerge from the sovereign debt crisis to avoid a long-term degrowth that would prevent the development of the Asian economy.
Rajat Nag, director-general of the Manila-based ASDB, said the world's two fastest-growing economic powers must "do everything in their power" to accelerate the recovery of the monetary zone through the International Monetary Fund or direct bilateral agreements.
He advised BRICS (Brazil, Russia, India, China) to look at Europe's difficulties with an "indifferent eye" and noted that Asian financial support alongside Europe's leaders and resources would avoid a long-term collapse of the global economy.
"We're all together in this crisis."
“Therefore anyone who can help Europe out of the crisis is helpful,” he told the Financial Times during an interview during the World Economic Forum in Mumbai.
"Asia can be protected to a certain extent, but it is not safe."
"So if China and India can help, let nothing stop them."
In recent months, the eurozone has been seriously shaken by a sovereign debt crisis.
Recently, the cost of Italian borrowing skyrocketed dangerously and the Prime Ministers of Italy and Greece resigned.
Europe’s thriving sovereign debt crisis has sparked fears that the global economy will return to recession, and has sparked a call from key emerging economies to come together to help the eurozone find a solution.
Nag foreshadowed that the IMF would deliver all Asian aid, but he also said that this bilateral assistance - such as buying the bonds of the U.S. financial rescue fund, the European Financial Stability Fund - provided greater negotiating power to Europe's Asian partners.
Anand Sharma, India's Minister of Trade and Industry, said that "India will do everything it can" to help the eurozone as its own economy now suffers from a decline in exports and a depletion of foreign capital inflows.
"Nobody wants the eurozone to remain unstable and restless," he said.
"We have monumental challenges and we need to maintain a high level of growth."
"It's not a choice, it's an imperative, because where would we find work for the tens of millions of our young men and women?"
Others argue that developing economies such as India should not help the wealthy Europeans as they face their own significant economic challenges.
Ashutosh Varshney, a professor at Brown University in the United States, said that it would be politically very difficult to get Europe to accept aid to the 1.2 billion Indians, of whom at least 80,000 live with US$2 a day, or even less.
“One day, they will discover that the Greeks are retiring at the age of 50 and that they are going on holiday in the sun and they will have trouble swallowing it,” he said.
Lee Howell, president of the World Economic Forum, also questioned why India’s reserves should be used to keep many well-paid Greek civil servants in poorly managed jobs that generate losses, such as in the rail sector.
Nag said that the eurozone crisis is likely to cause "significant repercussions" across Asia.
ASDB's forecast for economic growth of 7.5% in Asia in 2011/2012 is now facing "loss risks" due to the European threat.
He noted that vulnerable emerging markets needed to “develope “contingency plans” to protect themselves from an economic reversal and significant capital flights.
Severe Pentagon Warnings on Potential Defense Reductions
Defense Secretary Leon Panetta raised pressure on Congress on Monday, warning that impending automatic budget cuts would undermine national security. He unleashed a financial-chain reaction from the Pentagon’s corridors to the battlefields of Afghanistan, including civilian gatherings.
The Pentagon is already digesting US$450 billion in cuts for the next decade, but now fears that an additional US$600 or more cuts are imminent if Congress fails to reach an expense deal.
“The impacts of these reductions would be devastating to the department,” Panetta said in a letter to Senators John McCain, R-Arizona, and Lindsey Graham, R-South Carolina.
He said that the failure of Congress to reach a resulting budgetary agreement in so-called sequestration, would lead to 23% reductions as well as the freezing of many new projects.
"Such a significant reduction, thus indiscriminately applied, would make most of our ships and construction projects unenforceable - you cannot purchase three-quarters of a ship or vessel - and seriously undermine other modernization efforts," Panetta wrote.
“We would also be forced to separate from a number of our civilian staff and, because the reduction would be imposed so quickly, we would almost certainly have to send civilians on leave without pay in order to achieve the goal.”
"These changes would undermine the faith of those who support our military and seriously damage our preparedness."
The reductions would eventually affect the fighting troops, continues Panetta.
“Although war-time financing of overseas contingency accounts is not directly affected by sequestration, war efforts would be negatively affected by severe disruptions in core budgets,” Panetta warned.
"Contracting personnel would be reduced, resulting in delays in contracts and supervision of contracts that support the war."
"Staff managing salaries would be reduced, resulting in late payments to war providers, and legal and political support would be disrupted."
The two senators had written to Panetta 10 days earlier, asking for details about the potential impact of automatic cuts on the Ministry of Defence.
“The consequence of sequestration on the Department of Defense would have triggered a rapid decline by the United States as undisputed global leader of military power.”
“We are fiercely opposed to this draconian action,” the senators said in a joint statement Monday afternoon during the publication of Panetta’s letter.
"It's not a consequence that we can live with, and it's certainly not what we should impose on ourselves."
“Sequestration is a threat to U.S. national security interests, and it shouldn’t have to happen.”
Whether Panetta’s letter and recent senators’ warnings increase the pressure for a budget compromise or a call to exempt the Pentagon from reductions remains to be seen.
Panetta has been increasingly virulent about possible cuts, even though he himself came to the Pentagon’s top position with years of budget expertise in Congress and the White House and knowing he was faced with difficult choices.
At a press conference last week, the Secretary of Defense painted a dark picture of what might happen — a soldier with a shell, but no nucleus.
"It's a ship without sailors."
"It's a brigade without bullets."
"It's a fighter jet without enough trained pilots."
"It's a paper tiger, an army of barracks, buildings and bombs that don't have enough trained soldiers capable of accomplishing the mission," Panetta said during her preliminary remarks at the Pentagon.
"It's a depressed force, ill-prepared and unable to deal with potential opponents."
"De facto, she's inviting aggression."
In an addendum to his letters to McCain and Graham, Panetta specifically stated how new reductions could "generate significant operational risks: crisis response times, conflicts and disasters; severe limits to our ability to deploy and engage in the world; and unacceptable assumed risk in future combat operations."
And Panetta said that some of the biggest defense projects could move on, including those already tested and some just in the early stages of planning.
This list included the Fighter F35 Joint Strike, a planned new bomber, the next-generation ballistic submarine, the new coastal combat ship and the new field vehicle, which would replace the army and navy’s Humvee.
Putting an end to the development and testing of the F35 could generate US$80 billion in savings over 10 years, but its supporters say this is an essential step in improving and responding to potential threats from China and other rivals.
How to spot a lie
A look at recent headlines indicates how ubiquitous deception and lying are in everyday life.
Republican presidential candidate Herman Cain is busy exchanging allegations of sexual harassment with several women; each side accusing the other of lying.
Penn State administrators have been charged with perjury for allegedly covering reports that a retired football coach sexually assaulted boys.
Last week French President Nicolas Sarkozy was taken by an open microphone telling U.S. President Barack Obama that Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu was a liar.
Lying has destroyed careers and convulsed countries.
New York congressman and internet celebrity Anthony Weiner, passed for an idiot by quickly issuing denials contradicted by irrefutable evidence.
Former presidential candidate John Edwards has been accused of violations of campaign funding related to the coverage of an extramarital affair.
And then, none of those who experienced the story will ever forget the media circus that President Bill Clinton unleashed during his second term about his sexual involvement with Monica Lewinsky.
Cheating stories in the school and university tests are legion.
There have been cases where teachers have given the test answers to students in order to make themselves clear about their performance evaluations.
Mentors who should teach otherwise send the message that lies and cheating are acceptable.
How much decepties do we encounter?
Over a given day, studies show that you can hear from 10 to 200 lies.
In fact, many of these are pious lies.
Another study has shown that strangers lie three times in the first 10 minutes of their meeting.
Detecting or spotting lies is an essential competence that everyone must acquire, both for personal and professional reasons.
Far from being a board game similar to, say, charades, where the objective is to exclaim: "I've got you", the detection of deception is a serious branch of knowledge based on scientific data collected over the past six decades at prestigious universities that have conducted an in-depth research of such projects, especially in psychology and physiology.
One of the results of the research is that the oldest myths about lying have been debunked.
Liars look you in the eyes.
They don't stutter, blush, or agitate.
Don’t conclude that liars are difficult to detect and difficult to unmask.
A qualified lie detector can uncover the truth by looking at the structure of the statement, facial micro-expressions, question formation, and response time.
I’ve spent several years examining scientific findings in the vast body and always emerging knowledge about deception, and it’s clear that detecting deception is a modern skill that’s easy to learn and useful for navigating our complex world – especially if your professional responsibilities include hiring, interviewing, negotiation, or management.
Good liars are skilled at reading others well, putting them at ease, managing their own emotions and intuitively detecting how others perceive them.
We know from research that extroverts lie more than introverts, that men say more lies "centered on them" while women say more lies "centered on others" - usually to protect someone's feelings - that married people lie less often to their partners than unmarried people (but the lies they tell tend to be huge).
We also know that if you’re perceived as an unbeliever, others will feel less guilty about lying to you.
How can you say if someone is lying?
First of all, observe the normal behavior of your subject.
This is known as "establishing benchmark data".
It helps to provide a reference point for measuring subsequent changes.
Observe the posture of your subject, laughter, vocal quality.
You need to know if someone normally stirs their feet permanently so you don't launch unfair charges when you see them stiring their feet in the middle of the meeting.
Then look for clues of deceptive verbal and non-verbal behaviors.
Think of these clues as red flags, not evidence of deception.
Deceptive people can immobilize the upper body when trying to remember their history, they can point their feet at the door, lean towards an exit, change posture significantly, or present “post-interview” relief – an exaggerated sigh of relief, a change in posture when all the difficult questions have passed.
The interrogators often falsely indicate that the interview has ended just to seek to trigger the post-interview reaction.
Also, pay attention to the vocabulary of your subject.
The famous Scott Peterson skidded using the past while pretendinging that his murdered wife was alive, triggering a national search to find her.
Deceptive individuals can also use distanted language: "I didn't have sex with this woman... Miss Lewinsky" or fully repeat a difficult question.
The most common verbal indicators are subtle.
Someone can use a lot of "language of wood" during his answer to a difficult question: "Well... to tell the truth... if I remember well... to my knowledge."
This renders the perceptive rather than factual response and is often a red flag.
There is no magic formula for the detection of lies, but the development of skills to recognize deception is possible.
These skills will improve anyone's chances of avoiding victimization by scammers in their professional and personal lives.
The controversial pipeline plan will be diverted after U.S. threat of late approval
A few days after the Obama administration threatened to delay the approval of a pipeline project between Canada and the Gulf of Mexico — arousing the anger of unions while soothing environmentalists — the company seeking to build the pipeline says it is ready to redirect the project to get it back on track.
TransCanada said Monday night that it will move the projected pipeline out of the ecologically sensitive area of Nebraska dunes, and is confident that the project will still win its approval.
The company announced the decision at a press conference at the Nebraska Capitol.
TransCanada official Alex Pourbax says the company remains confident that it will eventually get approval from a pipeline, but with a different route.
This follows the State Department's announcement last week that it would delay its decision to grant a federal permit for the project until it receives new studies of potential routes to avoid areas of Nebraska dunes and Ogallala aquifer, vast groundwater sources.
“It’s a real path forward,” said Lee Terry, a Republican from Nebraska on Fox News.
The new environmental review commissioned by the State Department would likely have postponed any decision until 2013, after next year’s elections, although officials deny policy involvement in the decision.
Obama, in a written statement last week, described the decision as the State Department’s decision, and expressed his support.
The 2,735-kilometer pipeline would extend from Canada to the Gulf of Mexico.
It would transport 700,000 barrels a day from the province of Alberta to refineries in Texas.
To do so, it would have to pass through six states.
He is hated by environmentalists, but loved by working groups, who bet on the estimated 20 000 jobs related to the pipeline.
But Nebraska lawmakers opposed to the plame are pressuring that a law to forcibly distance from the Sand Hills area and Ogallala aquifer, a major source of drinking water and irrigation.
State Department's ongoing environmental impact study found that the project would only pose limited adverse environmental impacts, but the energy industry stated that the Ministry's Inspector General ordered a separate investigation from the review process, centered on two issues.
One is that a lobbyist hired by TransCanada, Paul Elliott, who was a campaign adviser to Hillary Clinton in 2008, would represent a conflict of interest in the program.
The other is whether a company that was hired to conduct the initial study was an inappropriate choice because of its relationship with TransCanada.
Patti LaBelle sued for allegedly causing a young child to vomit in fear
A New York family claims that diva Disco Patti LaBelle thrown into an unleashing of violence in the lobby of their Manhattan building, terrified their little girl to the point where she vomited for fear.
Lady Marmalade's singer's tirade frightened 18-month-old Geneviève Monk, so much so that she underwent "personality changes, sleep disturbances" and "increased fear of strangers," her family said during the Manhattan Supreme Court trial.
It was November 10 last year as stage technician Kevin Monk, his kindergartening wife, Roseanna, and Geneviève were preparing for family travel.
As Kevin Monk went to get the car, Roseanna Monk walked down into the lobby of their Riverside building with their luggage, carrying Geneviève in her arms.
Roseanna Monk said she stopped by the door and laid her daughter for a while.
"Someone came behind me and said, 'Do you know what your daughter is doing?" I said "yes" and I went looking for her," she said.
LaBelle angrily admonished the pregnant woman.
"She told me in an aggressive tone: 'You shouldn't leave your daughter by the door,' says Roseanna Monk.
“I said, ‘What you’re saying or thinking doesn’t interest me.’
She became enraged and began to use swear words - the word "" and the word "" with a loud voice...
She had a bottle of water and began to sprinkle water from the bottle.
She said that Geneviève became hysterical.
"I told this woman, 'Look what you did to my daughter,' said the child's mother.
LaBelle threw herself at the mother - who still held Geneviève in her arms - and had to be restrained by her entourage who dragged her to a car waiting for them, says Roseanna Monk.
The little girl was crying so hard that she started vomiting, she adds.
Later, the family sent a note requesting an apology from LaBelle, but received no response.
LaBelle's lawyer and representatives did not return our calls to comment.
The lawsuit claims unspecified damages, but the family’s lawyer, Sam Davis, said he took the case free of charge and that the family plans to donate all the money received to child cancer charity.
“The goal is to hold Patti LaBelle responsible for his conduct,” Davis said.
"This kind of behavior is totally unacceptable, especially towards a kindergarten teacher carrying an 18-month-old child."
Climate adversity has slowed the economy
During the first year of management of Mario López Valdez, in Sinaloa unemployment and informality increased, foreign investment fell and the economy contracted
2011 is a year that will be remembered in the economic history of Sinaloa, it is the first year of Government of Mario López Valdez, self-called change, which has generated great expectations with regard to advances of the economy, but given the climate adversity at the beginning of the year, the increase does not observe, in exchange there is a deterioration.
Loss temperatures in February, apart from causing trillion losses for the agricultural sector, have limited the possibilities of the state economy for growth, have caused a contraction of economic activity in general by 3.6 percent in the first half of the year, justified mainly by the historic 31.16 percent decline in agriculture, which has affected the dynamism of other economic sectors.
According to the Mexican Council for Sustainable Rural Development, there has been damage of 582 million 835 cultivated hectares, of which 78.03 per cent have suffered total damage and the rest of 21.07 per cent have suffered partial damage, the main affected crops have been vegetables, maize and chick peas.
The decline in economic activity generated, until November, a constant increase in unemployment in the state, according to the National Institute of Statistics and Geography the rate of state unemployment during the first three quarters of the year there was an accelerated increase, because from January to March 55 thousand 0,053 sinaloens were unemployed, with a rate of 4.53 per cent of the Active Economic Population, in the second quarter
Less liquidity
Unemployment in Sinaloa during the year is multifactorial, according to economist Gerardo López Cervantes, director of the UAS Faculty of Economic and Social Sciences, the increase in unemployment can be explained indirectly according to the actions carried out by public policy.
“What’s observed is an increase in unemployment that is due to a variety of factors, among which the loss of liquidity in the market due to a restriction of resources by legislation approved in Congress to combat money laundering, because for people who make dollars in an illicit way, it’s not easy for them to work to earn them, to invest them and use them in something,” he said.
Detained migration
López Cervantes reported that rising unemployment is also due to a demographic factor.
“The number of people abandoning the state to go to work abroad in the U.S. has been restricted, primarily because of the difficulties of entry and secondly because by entering they already have a lot of difficulties in finding work, these people remain here and ask for sources of work, which means that unemployment rises,” he said.
Lack of Seriousness
The analysis of the Sinalian labour market worsens by considering the high level of seriousness that predominates between working conditions, according to INEGI, 60.38 percent of the population employed in this state works there without contributing as a worker to any social insurance institution.
Apart from unemployment and the lack of seriousness, another reason for the state's lag are low wages, the entity has the lower wages of the Country, according to the registers of the Mexican Institute of Social Insurance and the Secretariat of Labour and Social Forecasting.
The average monthly incomes of Sinalian workers were, until September, 5 thousand 352 pesos, much lower than the national average, which stands at 7 thousand 375 pesos.
EDI contraction
Apart from the decline in economic activity and the increase in unemployment, in 2011 there was the largest reduction in Foreign Direct Investment in the last 10 years, according to the Economic Secretariat from January to June the FDI of the State reached 630 thousand 697 dollars, which represents a decrease of 87.74 percent compared to 5 million 143 thousand 312 dollars recorded in the same period of the previous year.
In turn, the Economic Development Secretariat of the Government of the State accounts for a total investment of 17.280 trillion pesos per year, registered mainly by companies in the tertiary sector.
Debt
Given the scenario of economic contraction, the Government of the State requested in July the public congress the approval of a credit of 3 trillion pesos, intended for investment in infrastructure projects in the 18 municipalities, with the objective of reactivating the economy, the approval of the state congress was 2,600 trillion pesos and until November there has been no benefit of debt.
Reactions
For representatives of the management sector, Mario López Valdés’ first year of government demonstrates a lack of Government management capacity.
Miguel Loredo López, president of the National Chamber of Transformation Industry, said that in 2011 there was no harmonization of the wills of the different sectors, hereby including the State Government.
"The performance of Sinaloa's economy during this year was really very poor, we have to work hard to recover the ground we lost this year, in 2012 we have to double our efforts, we had to align everyone's effort and especially the already synchronized progress of this new government team, let's hope that next year is much better," he said.
Slower economic
Loredo López said that events like the frosties at the beginning of the year have retained the state’s economy and restricted its yield and growth.
“The low economic flow is produced from several events, between which frosties, one of the main effects has been a slow economy that does not turn fast enough to generate an economic dynamism that makes it possible to have an acceptable level of flows in lawsuits, products and services and therefore, at the end of the year we close a lot below the expectations we had,” he said.
Redefining the vocation
For Teodoro Salazar Uriarte, president of the Patronal Confederation of the Mexican Republic of Sinaloa said that we want to channel the state towards productive activities of greater dynamism.
“In general terms we have observed that we have worked, we believe that it is necessary to develop a long-term program that redefines the vocation and economic direction of the state, because it is important that we detonate the full potential of Sinaloa’s material and human resources,” he said.
A lot to do
The President of the Coparmex stressed the importance of establishing a vision for the future that better channels the Government's efforts.
“In general terms, I would say that the Government has made efforts to achieve good results in different areas, in terms of economic development there are a few advances, but it is important to develop a long-term programme,” he said.
He noted that the current administration has shown advances in public policy management.
"There are elements that allow us to say that the Government is more inclusive, what I can say is that we have worked hard and the Government is aware of the fact that there is still a lot to do," he said.
Convicted for 8 kilos of marihuana
A person resident in Novolato was sentenced to five years in prison and a 100-day fine for possession of eight kilos of marihuana.
The Public Prosecutor General of the Republic has informed that the convicted petty trafficker is Omar Alexis Valenzuela Sandoval, for crime against health, in his variant possession of marihuana intended for sale.
Pursuant to file AP/SIN/CLN/687/2010/M-I, Valenzuela Sandoval was arrested by agents of the Mexican Army on 7 June 2010 during a surveillance operation in Cipriano Valdez Street, in front of the number 80 in El Castillo, Navolato.
The smuggler was driving in a white pick-up-type pick-up van in which he was carrying a black-colored bag in the vehicle’s luggage rack and on the co-pilot’s square a small backpack where the military found eight kilograms and 23 grams of marihuana.
For this reason, confiscated elements have been made available to the Second Judge of the District and the convict is still in the Centre for the Execution of Legal Consequences of the Offences.
Debts to contributions to books
The works of a social character contemplated in programs such as Hábitat, Rescate de Espacios Públicos and 3x1 para migrants may not be finished where to record serious delays due to the lack of economic resources, said Miguel Ángel López Miranda.
The Director of Municipal Social Development has indicated that, even so far while construction programmes have progressed, they are in a situation of risk, because the State Government has failed to comply with its obligations to offer contributions that exceed 11 million pesos in the three programmes.
He noted that, in total, the investment of these three programmes is approximately 50 million pesos, from which the State must provide the 25 per cent and so far only the Federation and the Municipe have provided resources for the works, therefore there is the possibility of a delay in their finalization.
"They have to provide a contribution of 12 million and despite the fact that the year is about to end they haven't given us anything, even though since July they had to give us part of it," he says, "and we, in order not to record delays, we have started the books and we've advanced a lot, but we need the money."
He mentioned that last year, during the administration of Jesús Aguilar Padilla, the State had not paid the corresponding contributions, therefore at the beginning of the Municipe administration had to apply for an appropriation to cover the 7 million that the State administration had not provided on that occasion.
"We had already suffered this in the previous sex period because the state had not had the economic capacity to participate and the programs continue to work and this year we, in order to have access to these programs, we had to get the resources to cover what the state had not paid," he said.
In spite of the fact that there is a commitment on the part of the state authorities to provide these resources, he stated that they had made the request to the Secretary of Administration and Finance, Armando Villarreal, to activate the managements so that the corresponding percentages arrive as soon as possible.
“They have to provide almost 12 million and despite the fact that the year is about to end, they haven’t given us anything.”
The Court validates "analogy cut-off" in Mexico in 2015
The substitution of analog signals by digital is one of the most important exchanges that the world has suffered in recent years
The Supreme Court of Justice of the Nation (SCJN) rejected the constitutional controversies that the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies formulated against the presidential decree that advances the so-called "analogy cut" from 2021 to 2015, because the eight votes necessary to invalidate it have not been gathered.
A minority of four ministers, including the President of the Court, Jauan Silva Meza, considered that President Felipe Calderón did not exceed his responsibilities when he issued the decree which details the actions to be taken to concretize the transition to terrestrial digital television.
With this result, the constitutional controversy promoted by the Senate of the Republic, has been rejected for not having reached the qualified majority of eight votes for the Court to decide on the constitutionality or non-constitutionality of the decree, and this continues to be in force.
The draft sentence, developed by Minister Olga Sánchez Cordero, proposed the declaration of the decree as unconstitutional, due to the fact that the regulation on radio and television is an assignment reserved by law to the Federal Telecommunications Commission (Cofetel), in contravention of the principles of hierarchical subordination and reservation of law.
In favor of the project voted only Zaldívar, Jorge Mario Pardo, Margarita Luna and Sánchez Cordero himself.
With this argument, both the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies promoted two constitutional controversies to challenge the decree and by admitting complaints, Minister Sánchez Cordero suspended the application of the decree and all the actions planned by it for the digital transition.
The plenary session of the Ministers also resolved the complaint made by the Chamber of Deputies, without discussing the project for which Minister Sánchez Cordero is also responsible.
In his speech, the minister president said that he did not agree with the arguments of the project, in the sense that the Federal Executive cannot structure through general standards the measures so that the public administration is prepared.
"I also only consider that if it did so would affect the autonomy of the regulatory body," and he recalled that public policy planning in strategic areas such as the telecommunications sector is the responsibility of the Federal Executive, whose constitutional mandate is the management of the state.
The substitution of analog signals by digital signals is one of the most important changes that the world has suffered in recent years and this translates into a better exploitation of the radio spectrum, better signal quality and more channels.
Adjourning the change, he argued, would adjourn the benefits of digitalization which implies a decision in the public interest that not only has a technical dimension, "because it also includes economic, social, political and national security issues."
These issues, he stressed, "require the operation of an entire system establishes, in accordance with the principle of economic direction of the state, encompasses a group of responsibilities that the Constitution grants to the faculty of the State Executive."
He recalled that a large number of countries from everyone have already finished the digital transition and they have done so through the Executive, despite the fact that they have highly specialized technical bodies.
It cannot be concluded that the Executive, by issuing the contested decree, has invaded the sphere of competence of the Congression in a form, because it ensures in the administrative sphere compliance with the laws issued by the Legislative, he assured.
One analyzes the possibility of a "vote-by-vote" statement in Michoacán
According to the data highlighted by PREP, the difference between the first and second places in the governor's election is only 27 per cent, even without counting the votes corresponding to the 879 of the 6 thousand 74 total minutes.
The possibility of the "vote-by-vote" countdown is a reality with regard to this year's electoral trial in Michoacán, where according to the data highlighted by the Preliminary Electoral Results (PREP) program the difference between the first and second places in the governor's election is only 27 percent, even without counting the votes corresponding to the 879 of the 6 thousand 74 total minutes.
In this sense, the President of the General Council of the Electoral Institute of Michoacán (IEM), María de los Ángeles Llanderal Zaragoza, recalled that a few days ago, the past 9 November, the electoral body approved a series of guidelines to harmonize local standards with the federal reform in electoral matters.
It will be recalled that Michoacán did not approve either its Political Constitution or State Electoral Code or its Law of Electoral Justice, at the same time as the State Congress approved the reforms shortly before ending the legal deadline for this electoral trial to enter into force and Governor Leonel Godoy Rangel omitted its publication within the time limits provided for by the Law of the Official Gazette.
On the same November 9, the Supreme Court of Justice of the Nation (SCJN) resolved the constitutional controversy promoted by the local legislature and described as "invalid and illegal" the vote of the state's proxy for constitutional reforms, because it does not have the right to "sanction or challenge them".
Interviewed on the possibility of the "vote by vote", María de los Ángeles Llanderal said that thanks to the approval of the corresponding guidelines by the IEM General Council it will be possible to carry out partial and total votes by the local electoral body.
Thus, the partial counting of votes, of one or more electoral packets, it may be that: the results of the minutes do not coincide; there are no minutes of voting and counting in the file of the urn is not found in the president of it; or there are obvious errors or alterations of the minutes.
In addition, the total counting of votes received in all polls of the election may be achieved when there is an indication that the difference between the assumed candidate winner of the respective election and the one who obtained the second place in the election is equal to or below a percentage point; provided that at the beginning of the session there is an express petition from the representative of the party and/or coalition that presented the second of the reported candidates.
The total counting of the vote can also be carried out if at the end of the joint account it results that the difference between the supposed winner candidate and that of the second place is equal to or less than a percentage point, provided that there is the express petition of the representative of the political party from which the candidate came out second, in which the electoral packets which have been the subject of the partial count will be excluded.
It should be noted that this is the situation of Morelia's election, where according to the PREP, the difference between first and second place is only 0.16 percent, even without counting the votes of 129 of the 923 total minutes.
His last will
His ashes will be spread in Zapalinamé’s sierra, the family confirms.
The remains of pilot Felipe Bacio Cortés will arrive in Saltillo, his hometown, this Sunday towards the night, and Monday people will present their tributes and there will be a requiem address.
According to his family, the last will of the lieutenant-colonel was that his ashes should be poured into the Sierra de Zapalinamé.
After having received the tributes next to the other people died in the accident, the family of Bacio Cortés returned to the capital of Coahuila on the edge of an aircraft of the Mexican Air Forces, the closest attended the ceremony in Campo Marte.
At noon the ashes of the Lieutenant-Colonel arrived at the vigiling chapel of Nazario Ortiz Garza Boulevard, where family and friends went.
At 17:30 a.m., he was presented with tributes to the Saltillo Technological Institute organized by his former technology bachelor's colleagues and the American football team of which Felipe Bacio was part when he prepared for the baccalaureate.
"He wanted to go back to Saltillo and that his ashes would be poured into Zapaliname's Sierra, he always said that `'has his family and friends," said Sandra Bacio Cortés, the pilot's sister.
For her, the lieutenant colonel had realized all his dreams, she considers that he was at the fullness of his life, "he was successful, he was a father and son, extraordinary, an affectionate brother and cared a lot for his family."
Felipe Bacio Cortés abandoned the town of Saltillo very young to register at the Air College in Zapopán, Jalisco.
Some couldn't believe it, because they said he had even a truck pain; but he always followed his dream of becoming a pilot.
His nephew Jorge Alberto Dávila Bacio remembers that he was a very good person, "when he was in Saltillo the whole family wanted to salute him, as was the occasion on which he brought Martha Sahagún to Saltillo".
The young man even remembers the fact that when he was 7 he traveled to Zapopán for the graduation of his parent, for this he knows that his uncle is dying by doing what he loved the most in life: flying an aircraft.
His last farewell
With honors we said goodbye to the Lieutenant Colonel of the Mexican Air Force, Felipe Bacio Cortés, a homage who had at head the governor Jorge Torres López, presented by the people and the Government of the State, the Mexican Army, through the Sixth Military Zone, the Mexican Air Force, directors and students of the Technological Institute of Salt
Bacio Cortés died recently when the helicopter he was flying crashed, next to the Secretary of the Government, José Francisco Blake Mora and other six persons, all officials of this service.
The remains of pilot Bacio Cortés born in Saltillo arrived (incinerated) at 18:05 a.m
Three abandoned corpses, located in Cadereyta NL property
A few hours before being found, the military authority had presented to the media to eleven people who had been arrested in the same municipality for offences relating to organized crimes.
Three men were found murdered with fire in different parts of the body and with the coup de grace, in an abandoned property in the municipality of Cadereyta Jiménez, Nuevo León.
The discovery took place at kilometer 1.5 drive from Cadereyta to Santiago, in the community known as El Castillo.
Some people who have passed through this place observed that in the property, whose fence has beige color and coffee the gater was widely opened.
Looking inside, at approximately three meters, they located the corpses of three people, which is why they immediately informed the corresponding authorities.
One of the individuals wore a yellow T-shirt with dark jeans, without shoes; at his side there was another with a white T-shirt, with straps.
At one metre away there was the body of another individual with a red t-shirt and jeans of blue color.
The three were lying on their stomachs.
According to data obtained in situ, the three people had been murdered in another location, because should not be found in situ casings.
In the fence of the property there were legends addressed by an organized delinquency group towards the other, apart from a few bullet holes in it and in the gate.
11 captured in Cadereyta
The Secretary of National Defence made the presentation of 11 people, between which a minor, who formed part of a criminal group that operated mainly in Cadereyta's municipe and who are considered to have a relationship with sequestations, homicides, "halconeo" (surveillance) and clandestine burials.
According to the information published, in a first event on 11 November in the colony of Los Alveros eight people were arrested and one who had been deprived of their liberty was able to be rescued.
They were confiscated three buildings and a cell phone; they claimed that they were part of the criminal group called "Los Zetas".
In another event that took place on November 12 at 6:00 a.p.m., military personnel made a surveillance course in Rancho Viejo, Cadereyta, by detecting a car without a license plate with two subjects on board.
By stopping them and carrying out an inspection, the military found a long weapon, a magazine and 18 cartridges.
Later and after these statements, on November 13 at 14:00 hours one of his accomplices was arrested in La Frauda, when he tried to escape by seeing the soldiers.
They are credited with clandestine burials in the municipalities of General Terán, China and Cadereyta.
Italy's new government will be formed on Wednesday
The Prime Minister-designate, Mario Monti, will meet tomorrow with the President of Italy to present the new government that will face a crisis that has put this country on the brink of economic disaster
Prime Minister Mario Monti will meet Wednesday with the president of Italy to present the new government that will face a crisis that has put Italy on the verge of economic disaster and jeopardized the entire Eurozone.
A statement from the presidential palace announced that Monti, appointed Sunday, would meet Wednesday with President Giorgio Napolitano to confirm that he can form the Government.
It is expected that he will present a cabinet composed in his majorities by technocrats, even if it is not clear when the new executive assumes his position.
Monti said he was going to present to the president the outcome of his conversations with political parties in his attempt to form a government.
"I want to confirm my absolute confidence in our country's ability to overcome this difficult phase," said the former European Commissioner for Journalists.
Monti did not explicitly state that he could form a government, but the tone of his comments indicated that the obstacles have been overcome.
"The framework is now well traced," he added.
Monti has completed the process of forming a government in less than three days, in a shorter time than normally, while Italy struggles a political and financial crisis that has pushed the costs of its debt to levels impossible to sustain.
The new administration headed by former European Commissioner Monti must approve a tough austerity package demanded by European leaders to recover the confidence in Italy that had been affected.
Highlighting pressure on Monti to hurry, the instability of the markets has placed the yield on Italy’s 10-year BTP bonds above 7 percent, the level in which Greece and Ireland had to be saved.
Emma Marcegaglia, leader of Italy’s Confindustria employers, told the media after her meeting with Monti: “We said we would support this government a lot.”
We believe that this Government is the last opportunity for Italy to emerge from this emergency situation.
Monti’s opportunities for success have been greatly stimulated by support from Silvio Berlusconi’s PDL party, to which the crisis has forced to resign last Saturday.
“We believe that Professor Monti’s efforts will have a good result,” Angelino Alfano, secretary of the centre-right party, told reporters.
PDL's support, the largest Italian party, is significant because until now many of its members had opposed the Technocratic Majority Government that Monti forms.
Parliamentary support
Monti’s new government must have strong parliamentary support to implement what will likely be unpopular austerity reforms.
Any blocking or delay in its efforts could provoke a new and devastating attack from the financial markets.
The Italian association of foreign benches added to the pressure by observing that a Monti bankruptcy would be a disaster.
Monti on Monday began its consultations with political parties, trade unions and employers’ groups and juvenile and women’s organizations.
He'll end his meeting tour Tuesday at night.
Monti was named Sunday by Napolitano, who empowered an extremely rapid transition in response to the crisis.
Following a brief respite at the end of last week, when it became clear that Berlusconi was going to resign, Italian debt costs have now returned to critical levels between uncertainty about the success of the new Prime Minister.
Saving Italy, with a public debt of 1.9 trillion euros, it would be too much for the current financial defenses of Eurozone.
Monti said that his Government should persist until future elections, scheduled for 2013, despite widespread predictions that politicians will only give it time to apply the reforms before advancing the elections.
Monti said he’d like to include politicians in his cabinet, but the big parties insist that it should only be trained by scholars, an indication of their objections to a process forced by financial pressure.
Political sources have pointed out that reciprocal mistrusts and disagreements between parties complicate the attempt to include political figures.
The experience of the Spaniards is superior to that of the Costa Ricans
Of the 22 footballers called from Spain, ten participated in more than 50 international games.
From Costa Rica, only one of the 22 summons participated in 50 meetings.
An enormous contrast to that of Costa Rica, if we compare the 22 summoned by the two countries for today's friendly, at 15:05 hours, at the National Stadium of San José.
While the national and European champions, the number one in FIFA's monthly rankings, have a wide-ranging register of inter-selectional Class A games, the Habs only have defender Michael Umana as the only one to have all 50 international matches.
This figure was reached last Friday in the match against Panama (0-2) on Rommel Fernández.
The Spanish footballers who play in the All-Star League and in powerful clubs of the Premier League of England, maintain an intense year's activities in national league and cup championships and disputes in European cups like the Champions League.
To all this we must add the experience with Furia Espanola during the complicated qualifiers of the European Cup and the World Football.
Costa Rican footballers, on the contrary, have international experience limited to an area, such as Concacaf, which is considered at a lower level in FIFA.
We see that the bigger record of the Spanish Selection is that of goalkeeper Íker Casillas, who earlier Saturday against England (defense 0-1 in London) matched the record of former goalkeeper Andoni Zubizarreta, with 126 international games.
Casillas will play today against the Habs and they will impose a new record for Spain, of 120 international games.
Xavi Hernández, cerebral flywheel of Barcelona, has 106.
Casillas and Xavi were even recognized the previous week by UEFA alongside the other centenaries of the Iberian team, Zubizarreta mentioned above with 126 and Schalke's centre-forward, Raúl González, with 102.
Loan of the centennial figure is Carles Puyol, with 97 games.
It is followed by Xabi Alonso, with 91; Fernando Torres, with 90; David Villa and Sergio Ramos, both with 81; Andrés Iniesta, with 62; Cesc Fábregas, with 61 and David Silva, with 53.
From the Costa Rican side, and near Michael Umana are left-back Júnior Díaz, with 48 appearances and scorer Bryan Ruiz, with 45.
After that, only five passed all 30 games: Keilor Navas with 31, Randall Azofeifa with 32, Michael Barrantes with 31, Carlos Hernández with 36 and José Luis López with 35.
Less less.
With more than 30 presentations in Spain are Gerard Piqué (37), Sergio Busquets (36), Santiago Santi Cazorla (32), Álvaro Arbeloa (31) and Raúl Albiol (31).
On the national side, with less than 30 appearances, Roy Miller appears with 26, Winston Parks with 26, Gabriel Badilla with 25 and Roy Myrie with 23.
The rest doesn't even get to 20.
While Nacho Moreal only has four games and Jordi Alba twice, in Costa Rica Víctor Bolívar has only one and Ólman Vargas and Edder Nelson do not begin yet.
Spain's historic scorer is David Villa, with 50 flies, followed by Fernando Torres with 27.
For Costa Rica, Bryan Ruiz is the one who records the most, with nine, two more than Carlos Hernández and three more than Roy Myrie and Parks.
Quinteto has included the country in its turn to celebrate two decades of trajectory
""Going to Costa Rica is a dream that has come true"
In the exclusive interview with Viva, bassist Jeff Ament of Pearl Jam said the band is ready to offer one of its best concerts next Sunday on the National Stadium.
They have waited more than 20 years, but the group assures that for them it will also be a dream come true to perform in this country.
It is that bassist and founding member Jeff Ament assured, speaking exclusively with Viva, of Brazil, last week.
The band is in the South American wing of its tour of celebration of two decades of work and Sunday 20 will make its only stopover in Central America.
That day will show up on the National Stadium, starting at 19:30 hours, next to the Los Angeles band The X and Las Robertas nationals.
This is an excerpt from the conversation, whose complete transcription can be found in La Nación on the Internet.
For its Costa Rican followers, the fact that Pearl Jam will perform in Costa Rica is a dream come true. Are you aware of what your fans are experiencing?
Going to Costa Rica is a dream that has come true for us too.
This one is our second time in South and Central America and so far it has been grandiose.
I spent in Costa Rica only once the holidays; it's such a beautiful country and I think we'll be able to spend a few days there.
So it makes us excited to bring our music there.
I ask you, because there is a kind of devotion to the band, the fans live with intensity on previous days.
What is your message to your strongest followers?
Thank you for staying beside us all these years.
It took 21 years for us to arrive in Costa Rica; thank you for waiting so long and we are sorry for the fact that so much time had to pass until this concert.
A few people already say that his concert is the biggest show of the year.
Is this adding a bit of pressure to your visit.
Some of the biggest shows we’ve been offered in the past 21 years have taken place in the past two weeks.
We offered two gigantic shows in Sao Paolo and we hope it put us in shape for this concert in Costa Rica.
We are very looking forward to performing there.
We love this country, we love surfing and the jungle; for both things, Costa Rica is one of the best countries in the world.
How did you discover Costa Rica and its waves for surfing?
We have a lot of surfer friends and I have a very good friend who lives in Tamarindo and the waves are amazing there.
My friends were always going surfing in Costa Rica and Nicaragua, next to Mexico, are great places to surf; that’s how we’ve learned it, through people who love surfing.
Seeing the tour, you offered huge shows, with a lot of songs.
What have you planned for your presentation in Costa Rica?
As we have never behaved there, we have no restrictions on what we can do.
We will try to make the best show possible, with a wide variety of songs; we will try to perform two songs from each album and offer our fans a good and diverse repertoire.
You never rehearse the repertoire.
What does this owe itself to?
“I think it’s partially because we have nine records and we can perform more than 120 songs and everyone likes different songs, that’s why it’s very difficult to choose which songs to perform each evening.
When we have two or three songs in the repertoire that we haven’t performed for a very long time, it helps us stay focused and surprises us because it reminds us of why we loved these songs and we may have forgotten.
This makes the show more interesting for us and therefore I think it also becomes more interesting for people.
With this tour, you are celebrating 20 years of trajectory.
Looking back, is it hard to believe everything you’ve achieved?
Yeah, absolutely.
In the first place, I don’t think any of us had imagined that we were going to form a big rock band, and the fact that we did it for more than 20 years makes it look like a dream.
We continue to share our blessings and being able to continue to make music together makes us very emotional.
We love each other and we love creating music.
We're lucky to go to places in the world we've always wanted to go to.
Things couldn't get any better for us.
What does it mean for you to have been a fundamental element in the creation of grunge?
It has always been an honour for us to form part of this group of major groups.
We're very friends with the people of Soundgarden, Mudhoney and Alice in Chains.
It's surprising when a movement emerges from a group of friends and is uncommonal.
From time to time, a group emerges from a city, but it is much stranger when five or ten groups leave the same town at the same time.
We are proud and it's an honour for us to be part of the Seattle group.
We're still playing with some of them from time to time.
Last year we participated in a festival with Alice in Chains and we went to Canada with Mudhoney, we saw in Soundgarden on their tour, so we're lucky to still be friends with all of them and it's an honour for us to be part of this Seattle sound that arose.
It's also very strange that a group like yours stays united and with the same members for so much time.
Yes, four of us have been together for 21 years and Matt (Cameron) joined us 13 years ago, and only this is much longer that many bands normally last.
In the first place, I believe that we are all lucky to still be alive and, secondly, we are all concerned for others.
We are all close friends and thanks to this there are musical boulevards that give everyone the freedom to experiment.
We have confidence in each other’s music and it couldn’t get any better.
Your relationship with the word celebrity has not been easy; however, thanks to your success, you were able to do what you want.
How do you find balance?
As we have growned, it has been easier for us to find balance.
We make sure that we don't spend too much time on tour because it's easy to fall into this game of great multitudes and people who love your music.
If we don't forget that people are there for music, it helps us not to forget that we have to respect the music and what brought it, without putting ourselves in mind the fact that we are famous and a rock star, which does not really interest any of us.
How do you remember your other project, Mother Love Bone?
I think we wrote some good songs and at first it was great.
We’ve been a band for almost three years and we didn’t release a record before Andy (Andrew Woods) died.
There has been a lot of frustrations for the fact that she lasted so little and the most important is the fact that we are still very friends with Bruce (Fairweather) and Greg (Gilmore) who started in the group with us and when I listen to a song where I see a photo I think of the incredible person that Andy was and how he made me laugh, it was always a pleasure to work, laugh
He was a surprising human and we miss him.
The national group Las Robertas will open the show in Costa Rica.
It is true that you know his work, especially Eddie Vedder and for that you have chosen her for the opening?
Yes, the production sent us videos with different proposals and this one was the band that we liked the most, so we can’t wait to see what Las Robertas can offer live and it must be very distracting.
For what do you always change the lyrics of Yello Ledbetter live?
This is a question for Ed (Eddie Vedder), so I don’t know.
He's singing her and I think he's doing it with the first words he imagines.
As a bassist, what are his influences?
There's so many...
Among the premieres I remember are Geezer Butler, John Entwistle, C. J. Ramone, John Doe, Paul McCartney, Chuck Dukowski...
There's so many influences that I could talk all afternoon about the bassists I love.
How did Pearl Jam to keep the grunge rising despite the fact that it is no longer fashionable?
I think we all feel a little disconnected from the word grunge.
When I think about it, it reminds me of the bands I just mentioned, like Mudhoney, Soundgarden, Nirvana and all those that arose at the same time and, if it’s relevant now, I think it’s wonderful, because it’s music that’s worth listening and bands like Mudhoney are now making the best music than ever.
At first, his music had more obscure content and now it has become more positive.
What's going on with this change?
It's interesting, because I think there are songs in the new album that look lighter, but there are also songs, such as The End or Just Breathe qualified as very obscure songs, even if they can sound a little positive.
With both, when Ed played them for me, I cried when I listened to them and these are deeply obscure songs.
I think that if you grow up and there’s trauma in your life and you lose to a relative or friend, these things are starting to influence your art and music and those songs, no doubt, represent that to me.
I also think that we can also be happy and even like that we can make obscure music.
Peru:
Fujimori Re-hospited for Medical Examinations
Former President Alberto Fujimori was hospitalized Tuesday in a public hospital to be evaluated for the loss of muscle strength that he presents in the magpies, his family doctor informed.
Fujimori, 72, who is serving a 25-year prison sentence for human rights violations committed during his government (1990-2000) suffers from a variety of conditions, herein’s language cancer for which he has been operated four times and is under control.
Delegate Alejandro Aguinaga, his personal physician, told Canal N television that Fujimori will be evaluated for three days at the National Institute of Neoplasic Diseases to determine the reason for pain and the loss of strength in its lower limbs.
Aguinaga commented that in his opinion Fujimori would be a candidate to obtain the benefit of the remission of the sentence for humanitarian reasons, no doubt he said that he does not want it because he feels innocent.
He had surgery four times with tongue cancer, he has weight weight loss of 18 pounds, and other pathologies, such as severe erosive gastritis, pancreatic cyst, kidney stones, high blood pressure, and circulation problems in the feet, the doctor enumerated.
Fujimori has been purgiving segregation since September 2007 in the National Directorate of Special Police Operations.
In April 2009 he was sentenced to 25 years in prison for the death of 25 people because of an army annihilation squadron that operated clandestinely during the early years of his government.
Mujica travels to Mexico to strengthen political ties
An official procession, headed by Uruguayan President José Mujica and made up of various ministers and bosses, will initiate this Tuesday a visit to Mexico to expand trade between the two nations and address political themes of the region.
Mujica will meet next Wednesday with his Mexican counterpart, Felipe Calderón, the highest point of a lengthy agenda of meetings with the political, commercial authorities and a meeting with the community of Uruguayan residents in Mexico, according to the agenda disseminated by the Uruguayan Presidency.
The meeting between the two presidents in the city of Guadalajara, will be "conducive for the exchange of information in political affairs between the two nations", according to the information published by the Presidency of the South American country on its web page.
According to local media, Mujica seeks to convince Calderón to support Uruguay in the incident with France which took place when the French representative, Nicolas Sarkozy, included the South American country on the list of tax havens, in the G20 meeting in the French city of Cannes.
In his speech, Sarkozy threatened to exclude from the international community the countries that continue to be tax havens, a declaration that provoked the rejection of Uruguay and the call for consultations of his ambassador of this European country.
Mexico has been presiding over the G20 since November, a group made up of the world’s stronger emerging and developed countries, and has been in effect with the South American country, since January this year, a convention to exchange tax information to avoid double taxation.
The Uruguayan government has tried in recent years to dispatch the implementation of tax exchange agreements to get out of the "Gri list" of the Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD), integrated by countries that do not implement all international standards of cooperation in tax matters.
In terms of trade, Mexico and Uruguay have in force since 2004 a Free Trade Treaty (TLC) which has increased the exchange of goods between the two nations.
Moustached plumber switches to three dimensions with "Super Mario 3D Land"
Super Mario struggled, tireless, for 25 years to save Princess Peach from the clutches of the evil Bowser, but he had never done it in three dimensions: in "Super Mario 3D Land" he will fight for the love of young people in stereoscopic technology.
The title, which arrives this month at everyone's stores, was designed from the beginning to exploit the features of the Nintendo 3DS mobile system, which allows to play in three dimensions without using glasses.
"Mario is the character who has to say how stereoscopy should work without glasses," said Argentina's specialist press executive Omar Álvarez during the presentation of the video game in Madrid.
Álvarez indicated that "Super Mario 3D Land" is "Nintendo's first video game originally designed for this support", because so far successes like "Star Fox 64 3D" or "Zelda: Ocarine of time 3D" were adaptations of existing titles.
The Nintendo spokesman argued that, despite being in 3D, "Super Mario 3D Land" is an "accessible" video game that allows a quick and smooth part.
⁁lvarez claimed that "Super Mario 3D Land" contains two games in one: the first part is "simple" to hang less skillful users.
Once overcome, the most experts will be able to do a "second round" at game levels, which this time are more difficult.
In addition, users will be able to choose in real time between an "aggressive D" or one that only affects the depth of the action, but it would be necessary to play in three dimensions to be able to overcome the different challenges and "do not succumb to optical illusions".
This foray of Super Mario into the three dimensions also presupposes the return of the character to a mobile console, a fact that has not happened since 2005.
In these Nintendo video games winks at fans of the saga and repeats the success formula of previous games, which consists in the fact that the moustached plumber must go through peril-mined platforms to liberate Princess Peach.
Even if I find novelties in movements, environments, costumes and enemies and also in Luigi "playable" cases.
Colom gives his approval to Portillo's extradition to the United States
President Álvaro Colom announced last Tuesday that he had ordered the extradition of former Governor Alfonso Portillo to respond to the United States for the U.S.-dollar money laundering conspiracy.
Thus ends the trial so that Guatemala responds to the request of the country of the north which called for Portillo to judge it.
In a press conference, Colom affirmed that his decision obeys the independence of the judiciary and executive, taking into account the fact that a court of first instance had already ordered his extradition.
He claimed that during his mandate "he did not help" in any judicial resolution and had given rise to all the extraditions he had experienced.
Apart from Portillo, Sergio Ruano Marroquín will also be extradited for murder and serious injuries, and Edgar Estrada Morales and Víctor Estrada Paredes will be extradited for drug trafficking.
What Colom has done presupposes an executive approach after having exhausted all the judicial and constitutional bodies to judge the former mandatary of the interval 2004-2004.
According to the resolutions of the Fifth Criminal Tribunal and the Court of Constitutionality (CC), Portillo should be extradited after resolving his legal situation in Guatemala and having guaranteed his human rights.
Portillo was absolute for embezzlement of Q120 million funds to the Ministry of Defense when he was president, which was the trial that preceded extradition, but the Public Prosecutor's Office appealed and reactivated the case.
Trinijove has helped 6,000 young people at risk
The entity was born to prevent the marginalization of untrained young people
The assessment of the first 25 years of life of the social support entity Trinijove is important: 6,000 young people at risk of exclusion have received help to overcome a difficult situation during this quarter of a century.
Added to this is the long centenary of workplaces currently obtained by this social group.
The anniversary served yesterday to take stock in an act celebrated in a former industrial complex of Fabra i Coats that more than 300 people attended.
Trinijove was born in the Trinitat Vella neighborhood in 1986 with the goal of helping people with little training, without work, with substance abuse problems or marginalized.
Yesterday's celebration in Sant Andreu concentrated a large number of leaders of entities and institutions who in these 25 years have collaborated with the association.
The various political representation, in the middle of the electoral season, has shown the unitary character of the work done by Trinijove.
Next to the president of the Catalan Regional Government (Generalitat), Artur Mas and his predecessor Jordi Pujol, attended the act the former minister of socialist education, Ernest Maragall and the former deputy mayor to the mayor and current eco-socialist municipal councillor Ricard Goma.
The current Minister of Social and Family Well-Being Josep Lluís Cleries and La Caixa’s Director General of Charity Jaume Lanaspa were also assisted in Fabra i Coats.
Mas claimed that Trinijove was born in a very difficult time and the current one is too, but he added that the work done so far "is a good basis".
The president gave Trinijove as an example to know how to combat bad circumstances and to give hope to people in difficulty.
“The crisis is not something new for the entity and for the work it carries out,” he said.
Property invaded by squatters for micro-feasts, evacuated
Invaders had adapted 11 of the building's 13 apartments in Balmes 51
The 12 occupants abandoned the property at noon yesterday without any resistance
Everything seems to indicate that the nightmare that the residents of No. 51 of Rue Balmes were experiencing has come to an end.
Twelve people voluntarily abandoned at noon yesterday the building they occupied illegally and where they organized micro-feasts that despaired the neighbors, by judicial order and without the need for police intervention.
At the end of the operation, the door lock has been changed to prevent the situation from recurring.
"Starting today there will be access control," said the administrator.
The squatters, which had invaded 11 of the building's 13 apartments - the rest were inhabited - took away the electrical installation and part of that of the water, except to paint the walls and leave residues of all kinds, especially boxes of alcoholic beverages.
The first three apartments were the mass festival scene, which lasted up to two days and despaired tenants.
Next to the building there is a discotheque, Balmes 51, which has also been affected.
Its owner, Maria Pantinat explained for Europa Press that as soon as the edifice occupied his business has reduced by 80%.
The owner of the discotheque evoked that the invaders of the property gave her unfair competence, because they sold the glasses for three euros, while in the discotheque they were worth 10.
In addition, the owner of the leisure room had had to respond to the complaints of the neighbors for the noise, while, according to it, the problems were not caused by the customers of the discotheque, but by the inhabitants of the occupied house and especially the participants of the parties organized there, which were more frequent and intense in recent weeks, according to the irritated neighbors of the area.
The head of the discotheque explained that the problems were so great that it even resulted in an agreement with the owner of the building and it's been nine months since he "forgives" the rent.
The Guardia Urbana (municipal police) has also confirmed that in recent weeks they have received more complaints from neighbors for this situation.
Alberto Fernández Díaz, the president of the town hall's municipal group of the town hall, last weeded "the immediate evacuation of the building to end with the problems of living in common and citizenship that it caused to residents and the degradation of the area".
The popular leader explained that before the summer only one floor of the building was occupied, but in recent months the squatters had occupied all floors of the building and almost all the apartments and that in some apartments there were flights too.
It is in this sense that he added that "we must end with impunity and be relentless against the anti-social behavior generated in the property".
Also yesterday, the Mossos (the local Catalan police) evacuated a building in Nou Barris.
At 15.30 o'clock, the police moved to No. 35 of Rue Mont-ral, having received the call of a neighbor who had observed that four people were accessing the building.
All four were arrested immediately.
Park or mountain?
The architects coincide with the desirability of convening beyond polemic over fees
The municipal competition on the 16 doors again opens the debate on the role of Collserola in Barcelona
Around the middle of the '80's, in the middle of the climax of the existing Metropolitan Corporation of Barcelona (CMB), this Administration printed promotional posters of Collserola.
The brochure defined the mountain as the great park of metropolitan Barcelona.
In addition, it also contained a comparison, in numbers, with the Central Park of New York.
With the death of the CMB, in 1987, the park became again, in the imagination of many people, the upper limit of Barcelona, the Catalan capital being the only one that does not focus on the north-south axis, if not high (mountain) - bottom (sea).
Collserola's qualification as a natural park is to the advantage of this idea.
The reference was no longer Central Park but Yellowstone, with wild boars in the role of the Yogi bear.
This was the situation until, in September, the town hall convened a mega-competition composed of 16 competitions for all access doors to Collserola.
A door goes back to the idea of a park, without a doubt.
It's like that, or someone wants to put doors in the field.
The question appears by itself: Collserola: park or mountain?
The competition, both with regard to its organization as well as with regard to reflection on the city, is a topic of conversation for all the architects of Barcelona.
It’s almost a recurring theme that two members of this older’s caste talk about when they recognize themselves in an elevator.
And this is precisely one of the few opinions on which coincide three architects of Barcelona, university professors, who presented themselves there.
Miguel Roldán, Daniel Modol and Marta Bayona: the contest is timely.
For Roldán, after approaching the coastal front "it is necessary to" debate on "the mountain front" because the discourse of Ildefons Cerda, that of green blocks has not been completed.
"You have to start from the base that Barcelona's more extensive park is the beach.
Among other things for its great accessibility.
Collserola is more complex", gave the verdict the architect for whom the title of "portes de Collserola" contains a "lexicon trap": we must not think of them "like access to a park, but the delimitation of certain areas in which it is necessary to act".
Modol, like Roldán and Bayona with experience in the urban planning world, describes the proposal as an "intellectual challenge" with "some perils".
From the perils that derive from "the uncertainty of the projection of the public space" after the experience lived in the '80's and which had in the area of the Forum "his swan song".
The first fear is the own delimitation of the boundaries on which we can build.
"Limiting something already means establishing the next step", that is to say, its overcoming.
"We have to think of new urban planning instruments, because the current ones [with reference to the General Metropolitan Plan (GGP) ] can cause authentic disasters."
By sticking more to the question, Modol opines that, by simplifying, the slope of the El Valles de Collserola region "can be understood as a park, that of Barcelona, no".
Modol recalls that there is already a work done by the Patronate of Collserola who "must not be lost", thanking for the opportunity that the town hall offers to his corporation to intervene in this project "from the first moment".
Moreover, he understands that the proximity of the mountain with the city is the safe-conduct that architects allow to enter the debate, but "doubt" if this technical profile must lead the multidisciplinary teams ("all a success", he says) that we had to create: "We architects, should be only an instrument".
Bayona insinuates that the answer to the question that gives the title of this piece is complex, because the basis of the competition is missing a global vision of Collserola: "We convened 16 cross-cutting competitions - he said - in the mountain-sea direction, but there is not a longitudinal reading that sees and understands the area as a whole".
This means, at least in this case, that he opted to participate in the 16 contests.
In another way, a project so that one of the doors can shock in the program and definition with that of the architect of the next door.
In its opinion, yes, the town hall should ensure that the band immediately higher than that of Ronda de Dalt should provide, in all its extension, an area of equipment that carries out a smooth transition between the city and the green.
Casino forgives Jonás Larrazábal and it will be released
After 72 days of provisional arrest, Casino Red’s legal power ground granted forgiveness to the mayor of Monterrey’s brother for the lawsuit initiated against him for alleged blackmail
After passing 72, Manuel Jonás Larrazábal will be released after the foundation of the legal power of Casino Red grants him the pardon before the Second Criminal Court of the District, which follows a trial for blackmail against the brother of the mayor of Monterrey.
Following the Casino Royale attack, the owner of the Casino Red, Sergio Gil García, published a series of photos and videos in which allegedly Manuel Jonás demanded the payment of an economic quote so that the mayor of Monterrey allowed the clandestine functioning of the gambling house.
Following public denunciation, the Public Prosecutor’s Office opened a criminal trial against Mayor PANiste’s brother and on 2 September he was arrested and temporarily arrested for 30 days.
After four hours in front of the Agent of the Public Prosecutor's Office 3 of Patrimonial Offences, it was transferred to the Detention Center 1, located in the Old Neighborhood of the state capital.
He remained there for the next 49 days when he was given a second order of detention after the Public Prosecutor's Office dismissed the offences of corruption and organized delinquency.
On October 19 he was transferred to Cadereyta's penal center presumingly for blackmailing the owners of Casino Red with a million-and-a-half pesos; but he spent only a few hours at the medium-security detention center as there were death threats against him.
The authorities allowed him to continue the trial enclosed in the municipal cells of San Nicolás de los Garza.
On October 25 an order of capture was issued by the Clerk of the Second District Criminal Court, Jorge Yánez, who said that after almost two months of investigation there was sufficient confirmed evidence to determine Jonás' guilt, such as that relating to the collection of 1.5 million pesos of the representatives of the gambling house in exchange to allow its operation.
This Monday, Casino Red’s legal boss, Víctor Aldo García Gómez, came before Judge José Luis Pecina to ask for forgiveness for the mayor of the region’s brother; therefore he could recover his freedom in the following hours.
During legal formalities, the Public Prosecutor's Office officer attached to the Tribunal, Ramiro Arias, questioned García Gómez if the damage had been recovered and which associates of the gambling house had been the one who had granted forgiveness, but the lawyer tried not to communicate the name.
Under file 197/2011 the legal pardon is granted to Jonás Larrazábal and at a later no claim can be filed in respect of the lawsuit against him; he will not be asked for any compensation for the damage and no punishment either.
After the hearing of the foundation of the legal power of the gambling house, at 15:50 hours the immediate freedom of Larrazabal Bretón was ordered.
The clerk of the Tribunal, Jorge Yánez, traveled to the prison of San Nicolás de los Garza municiple to communicate to Jonás that he had been legally forgiven and his file would be closed.
He will also communicate to him the immediate order of freedom issued by the judge.
Secretary Dionisio Pérez Jácome gives the details of the air accident in which the Secretary of the Government, Francisco Blake and seven people lost his life
The Communications and Transport Secretariat (SCT) has informed that the results highlighted on the accident in which the Secretary of Government, Francisco Blake Mora and others lost his life, indicate that the helicopter shocked directly against the ground in complete structural integrity, without the pilot being able to try a forced landing.
During a press conference, Dionisio Pérez Jácome, Secretary of Communications and Transport, mentioned that prior to the take-off of the aircraft, the pilot found that climate conditions were adequate to fly.
"Elements that seem to indicate that the unit had various contacts with the ground, the blades had contact with the trees at 25 meters where the unit lost the fuselage."
He also informed that the unit was designed for 19 people, so it met the requirements to transport personnel.
Moreover, he said that the investigations carried out in the impact zone do not relate the appearance of parts on the ground.
"In a search it has been reported that there are no parts or components of the aircraft in another location, the impact presented itself structurally.
"The helicopter had been designed for 19 passengers and satisfied the requirements to transport them.
The soil had a slip of seven to 30 degrees inclination.
For the moment, the secretary has assured that a priority is to have the results as quickly as possible; but the investigation can last a few months, it can even last one year.
In turn, Gilberto López Meyer, Managing Director of Aéroports and Auxiliary Services assured that the moment of the impact, the helicopter of the Presidential Maggiore State was operating in cruising speed and this happened on an ascending soft ground.
“This information supports the hypothesis of a normal functioning cruiser at the time of impact,” he says.
During the press conference, SCT holder Pérez Jácome listed six important elements in the investigation:
Visual theft:
According to aeronautical regulations, the flight has been carried out according to the rules of the visual flight, which implies that the crew must have visibility during the flight and on the ground.
Weather Conditions:
Reports in AICM mentioned adequate take-off conditions, and as the aircraft progressed towards the boundary zone between DF and the State of Mexico there were layers of low-heating cloudiness.
Selected Routes:
It is likely that because of the conditions of cloudiness the crew sought an area of lower height and more visibility towards the Cuernavaca valley.
Locating the impact.
The coordinates of the impact and the last recording of the aircraft on the radar coincide, probably prior to impact there was no loss of control or alteration of the flight path.
Dispersion of the remains of the aircraft in a reduced zone:
The information available so far makes it possible to assume that the impact took place on a straight and lateral trajectory.
The model for dispersing the remains of the aircraft makes it possible to assume that the helicopter has collided against the ground under structural integrity conditions.
Traces of fire or explosion have not been detected.
In the location of the disaster there is always no trace in the remains of the aircraft of any type of explosion or fire.
The latest results of the investigation indicate that the aircraft had had multiple contacts with the ground.
During the fieldwork, parts of the aircraft were found at a height of 9 thousand 200 feet above the mean sea level, the soil had a slope that has been going from seven degrees to 30 degrees inclination.
The photographs show the sequence in which he may have disarticulated along his journey.
The information provides elements that appear to indicate that the helicopter may have had multiple impacts on the ground.
Following the careful search in an area larger than the encircled area, around the accident, dispersed parts of the aircraft were not found, which supports the presumption that the impact occurred under structural integrity conditions.
MSS Announces Protocol to Find the Obesity Gene
Social Insurance conducts an investigation to discover the obesity gene in the case of children and youth; this is supported by IPN specialists
The Mexican Institute of Social Insurance (IMSS) has announced that it is leading an investigation to discover the obesity gene in the case of children and youth, known as “Genetics of Obesity of Children and Adolescents” which will allow to have the markers associated with overweight.
In a statement, the head of the Biochemistry Medical Investigation Unit of the 20th Century National Medical Center Specialties Hospital, Miguel Cruz López, explained that a multidisciplinary team of doctors, nurses, nutritionists and social workers had been formed.
This team is supported by specialists and postgraduate students of the National Institute of Public Health (INSP) and the Center for Investigation and Advanced Studies (Cinvestav) of the National Polytechnic Institute (IPN).
To the investigation it is invited to participate the population entitled or not, to present themselves to the participating sports units of the IMSS in Valle de México, where the evidence is carried out to minors every Saturday from 8:00 hours.
He pointed out that the protocol began in the Cuauhtémoc Sports Unit (Estado de México Poniente) and that from October 8, the activity takes place in the Sports Unity of Nezahualcóyolt (Estado de México Oriente).
Having achieved the goal of raising 500 or 600 minors, the study will continue in the Independencia Sports Unit (south of Distrito Federal) and finally in the Morelos Sports Unit (north of Distrito Federal).
He explained that the objective of this investigation and that it is to know the components and importance of genetics with regard to the development of obesity and to identify risk factors in Mexican children and youth to prevent the onset of chronic degenerative diseases.
As World Diabetes Day will be celebrated on 14 November, he mentioned that the objective is to reunite three thousand children and youth between six and 14 years old, with the consent and under the supervision of their parents, for the application of the specific questionnaires relating to food.
They will also be asked about their physical activity and their history of familial heredity of their diseases, to avoid risk factors that trigger conditions such as diabetes or kidney failure.
He detailed that Saturdays anthropometric measurements (weight, height, waist circumference), blood pressure, determination of levels of glucose, triglycerides, cholesterol and insulin and genetics will be applied to all participants (seeks to identify people associated with childhood obesity).
Cruz López reported that this investigation is the result of the concern of health professionals for the alarming weight gain in the case of minors.
"The 1999 and 2006 health surveys reported that in only six years there has been an increase, in the case of boys, of 77 per cent in obesity and in the case of 47 per cent girls; in terms of overweight there has also been a dramatic increase," said the IMSS specialist.
He indicated that the rationale for the search for markers is based on the fact that the country’s population is different from others by its genetic history.
He informed that, on average, Mexicans have a 65 percent Indo-American heredity, a 30 per cent European and a five percent African and this condition is reflected in people associated with diabetes and overweight.
He explained that, in general, the genetics of obesity translates into the inability to burn the excess energy that is consumed (calories), which is deposited in the form of fat.
The researcher explained that the direct benefit for participants is the integral evaluation of children, which makes it possible to detect whether they have a degree of overweight or obesity.
Ila even says that the presence of dark-colored folds (acanthosis) on the neck and forearm indicates the likelihood of suffering from metabolic and even pre-diabete alterations.
The information is managed confidentially, physicians give it in writing to parents, to whom each of the measures and changes in the lifestyle they need to make to prevent diseases is explained.
Cruz López explained that there are three main components that cause overweight, obesity and other chronic conditions: sedentarism, which is the lack of daily physical activity, stay most of the day sitting in front of TV and use means of transportation instead of going on foot on short distances.
Another cause is high calorie intake, i.e. the greatest consumption of sugary beverages, carbohydrates (tamals - meat pâté with corn flour wrapped in corn leaves -, bread, pizza, hamburgers, etc.) with a high content of fats and proteins and the genetics analyzed in this protocol.
UNAM's asphalt would prevent blackcurrants
Rafael Herrera, researcher at the UNAM Faculty of Chemistry, develops asphalt modified with polymers to produce a mixture with better yield, regardless of environmental and temperature conditions
If the reports that the pavement were resolved, it would no longer be necessary to repair about 200 thousand blackcurrants each raining season in the Metropolitan Area of Valle de México, warns Rafael Herrera Nájera, researcher of the Faculty of Chemistry (FQ) of the UNAM, who assured that if the process of construction and maintenance of the bituminous pavement is adequate, the blackcurrants could be reduced or even eliminated.
During his conference A Technological Opinion on Blackcurrants, organized as part of the International Year of Chemistry, he announced that the laboratory for which he is responsible develops modified asphalts with polymers to produce a mixture having characteristics similar to those of asphalt, but with greater mechanical strength, both at high and low temperatures.
Herrera Nájera considered that this type of modified asphalt could be used in places where the climate is extremely hot, such as some cities in the north of the country, to improve the yield, and consequently the formation of blackcurrants.
He explained that asphalt, the heavier part of oil, is a set of hydrocarbon molecules, molecules with a heavier molecular weight, called asphaltenes, and molecules with low molecular weight, called malthenes, which together give the pavement its viscoelastic behavior characteristic.
The viscoelastic behavior of asphalt consists in the fact that at high temperatures, of almost 180 degrees, behaves like a liquid, at 120 degrees C its viscosity is very high and at 50 degrees C it has an almost solid state, while at very low temperatures, the asphalt becomes brittle, he added.
This means that in the streets the resistance of the asphalt is not uniform and changes during the day according to environmental and temperature conditions, warns the specialist in chemical engineering.
The FQ researcher observed that the streets of Ciudad de México in their majority were covered by a flexible pavement, composed of six layers of materials, the last three of which involve asphalt.
Between the more superficial layers is the layer of asphalt, formed by small stones bathed in asphalt and later compacted.
Each of the small stones that forms the layer, he pointed out, was bathed an asphalt.
When the vehicles pass there by generating forces, it allows the forces to propagate very well in the pavement.
If we had modified asphalt, they would spread better, stubborn the chemical ingenier.
On the layer of asphalt, he says, one puts a last layer called rolling layer, made of thin stone, i.e., sand which is also bathed in asphalt.
Since this one is the layer in contact with the wheels of the vehicles, it must have more mechanical strength than the asphalt layer and provide adhesion in the street.
Being the part of the pavement which receives and distributes the forces, the rolling layer also requires it to have an adequate thickness and the material from which it is made must be cared for.
The climate, especially the humidity and temperature variations, is also a very important factor to consider, especially the water, he says, because these significantly affect the yield of the asphalt layer.
When the soil wets, the water spreads inside the layer is it is difficult for it to come out, which softens all the layers of the pavement and consequently causes blackcurrant.
In the process of forming a blackcurrant, he says, the first one that spoils is the rolling film and only later is affected the asphalt layer, but the damage does not take place immediately, but it is a process that must be cured at the first signals with adequate maintenance programs.
The member of the National Research System explained that the load of vehicles is another important factor for the formation of blackcurrant and therefore the thickness of each layer of the pavement must always be linked to the load of vehicles and guarantee its satisfaction, to avoid the frequent formation of blackcurrants.
Rafael Herrera has warned that there is still much to investigate and work on asphalt, for example, bituminous emulsions, which in other countries already exist on the market and make it possible to repair blackcurrants easily and without having to use high temperatures.
What the candidate of Valle missed to be the new miss Colombia?
Analysis of the factors that caused Melina Ramírez Serna not to obtain the crown of Miss Colombia.
Valle receives her title number 14 princess.
Once again Valle del Cauca remained with the desire to win the crown of Miss Colombia.
This time Melina Ramírez Serna had to be satisfied with the fact of being on the podium of the winners, wearing the silver wreath and the band that accredits her as the new princess of Colombia.
With the result obtained last night in Cartagena, where Miss Atlántico climbed with the crown and the sceptre, the department already sums 14 titles of Princess and Melina is one to a legion of women of Valle del Cauca who found themselves close to the crown but have been proclaimed princesses, between which Miriam Ospina Benoit (1947), Clara Domínguez Borrero (1949),
Today the big question that a lot of thosks arise is:
Because the result represents the defeat of a great favorite that in each presentation demonstrated that she had it all to be the new Miss Colombia, because she had not been elected in vain Queen of the Police and Best Face of Vogue, titles obtained by queens Taliana Vargas or Natalia Navarro who won the crown of Miss Colombia.
The first factor considered key in the juror's decision to designate her First Princess is the fact that she did not convince with her answer to the question "?What was the book that marked you and why? asked by Martín Murillo Gómez, of the Carreta Literaria Leamos.
This is Valle’s candidate’s answer to this question:
"I read a lot, I believe quite in literature, I think it's a form of culturalization, definitively I believe that the book that marked my childhood is "The little prince", it's a rather deep book.
Now I’m reading “Crime and Punishment,” it’s a book of universal literature that we should all read; and now I’m reading Irene Nemirovski’s book, a Russian, a very beautiful book called “Blood Ardour.”
This answer did not please first of all because of the term "cultivating" and secondly in order not to explain concretely in what way each cited book had marked it.
But at the end of the Melina ceremony said that she felt quiet and happy with the result "the universe and wise, what should happen happens.
And for everyone, please read a little, "The little prince" is a great book"
Between the hypotheses launched there is the one according to which Valle was destroyed by favoritism.
A Bogota journalist insinuated that being the favorite, the ovation and the candidate who received all the preliminary prizes (Oster gastronomy challenge, the vote of the public for the best artisanal costume, and Queen Mother), "it awakened the jealousy of the other candidates, stressed the queen and that's why she didn't shine on the scene."
And this was noted in his first swimsuit presentation at Hilton Beach and his twicks on the eve of the coronation.
Even if at both parades she had seemed sure of herself, it was noted that she did not have the strength and energy of the representatives of Magdalena and Atlántico.
Ariel Osorio, journalist of the "Como en casa" program (As at home), of the RCN Canal said that the fact that Melina had been named princess "confirms the fact that the candidate who obtains most prizes during the competition does not receive the queen's wreath".
In addition, Valle avoided the press at the end, and that was a mistake.
Another hypothesis is that Valle has not managed to convince the jury about its interest in social initiatives, which is actually the essence of the National Beauty Competition.
That’s why two days before the election international juror Lucy Doughty said they were looking for a committed woman when it came to her country’s social necessities.
"Only beauty is not enough in a Miss Colombia, you have to look beyond the physical and focus on your social sensibility, that before starting the Competition are already involved in projects," she explained.
It is also speculated that Valle sinned because of his defects of his back and the fibrosis that appeared in his abdomen, but this is contradictory, because precisely the jury elected him as the "Natural Beauty" candidate with the healthier body, good diet and responsible physical work.
Queens preparer Jorge Hernán Orozco acknowledges that Melina didn't have the best body, that there was better, "but her fibrosis problem on the abdomen, which in reality for me hadn't been too notorious, left points for him on the last day."
In the meantime journalist Guido Hoyos of Cromos considers that the error of Valle's candidate was the surgical procedures she had undergone.
"The surgeries are good, but Valle didn't know how to do them.
Her body has never been her asset and she has lost because of this.”
For this prize, which the National Beauty Contest presents for the first time, after the swimsuit parade arose a cruel prediction that was fulfilled last night, it was said that it was a small gift that Raymundo gave to Melina not to give him the taste of the crown.
Reality or not, the truth is that Melina begins today a year of reign, not as a sovereign, if not as a princess, a position that gives her the opportunity to represent Colombia in one of the international contests by designation of the National Competition.
However, it will pass into history as one of the queens who in spite of her dazzling passage through Cartagena, did not obtain the crown.
Thus was the work of Cali's person who identified Alfonso Cano's corpse
A Cali dactyloscopist made history as the man who confirmed the death of "Alfonso Cano".
The work of these officials is crucial.
The past November 4 a Cali CTI dactyloscopist made history as the person who confirmed the identity of FARC's supreme leader: "Alfonso Cano", confronting his fingerprints.
Of the seven typists who work in the CTI's local offices, this week it was the "availability" tour of Eliazar González, a man from Puerto Tejada who has worked for 21 years in the field of judicial investigation.
At the beginning of his career, González worked in Chocó's municipalities, where many times he had to spend more than five days in the forest to get to the place where the corpses that had to be identified were located.
The year 2000 he was transferred to the capital of Valle and after following adequate courses, he specialized in dactyloscopy, which is the study of the skin of friction, i.e. the palms and feet.
During the week of the operation against alias Alfonso Cano it had been warned for Eliazar to take clothes with him for at least three days.
There was something very big waiting for him.
Friday at night he left for Cauca in a Palmira helicopter.
He knew he had to identify two corpses and it was suspected that one of them was that of the man who ran the oldest guerrillas in the world, so he was asked to take the card with Guillermo León Sáenz's ten fingerprints (his real name).
But when he arrived with the investigative team at the place of the corpse, he did not recognize him by his physical appearance, because it was very different from that he had seen in the media.
He didn't have a beard, he was dressed like a peasant in the area, without glasses and much leaner.
But fingerprints don’t lie.
Accompanied by a Galtonian magnifier, ink, a leaker and a lantern, Eliazar compared the fingerprints of the corpse with those of the card.
His eye didn't deceive him: it was "Alfonso Cano".
That same night, the man from Puerto Tejada was the official who confirmed to the Minister of Defense, Juan Carlos Pinzón that yes, it was the supreme leader of the FARC.
In Search of the Imprint
For a dactyloscopist of Sijín de Cali the recognition that President Juan Manuel Santos made to Eliazar González, in a press conference for the operation against "Cano", is very significant.
A lot of people don’t know our work.
Dactyloscopy is an exact science, with no margin of error, which can be crucial in solving crimes.
Apart from identifying the corpses, the typists support the investigations for crimes such as theft and homicide and confirm the identity of those captured.
When they are captured, many offenders present false identity cards or do not have documents.
We take their fingerprints and search for them in the database.
The researcher said that in one case of theft he had to remove part of the wall from a house, because in this place the thief had leaned his hand to bring out a safe full of jewelry and money.
"The walls are difficult because they are not adequate areas to make a fingerprint, but in this case we were able to get the fingerprint from the whole hand and identify the offender who had participated in different thefts," he recalls.
Footprints may also rule out or confirm suspects in homicide cases.
Recently a dead man was found, with bottles of drink around him.
As an interlude to the investigation, it has been determined that a parent may be a suspect of the crime and indeed an imprint on the bottle coincided.
Even if it doesn't guarantee that he's the assassin, it puts him at the crime scene.
A CTI dactyloscopist explained that "the fingerprints of objects with chemical or physical reagents are extracted."
When they appear, you use a sampler or band to remove them.
Subsequently, fingerprints are sent to Asif, the declinquent database, or to the National Civil Registry.
"The problem is that there are still a lot of undocumented Colombians," said the CTI expert.
He explained that it is not all what is shown in the CSI television programme.
As everything that shines is not gold, everything you touch doesn't necessarily become an imprint.
It can, however, be used if the necessary confrontation is achieved.
"Indignants" defy authorities in New York
Hundreds of protesters were jostling outside Zuccotti Square to camp there.
A large proportion of those acceded to the evacuation order and moved to a nearby park.
Integrals of the Occupe Wall Street movement defied New York authorities this Tuesday by trying to enter again, thanks to a judicial order, instead of Manhattan, from where they were evacuated at dawn and when they tried to occupy another park in the Great Apple.
Hundreds of protesters were jostling outside Zuccotti Square, where they wanted access thanks to a judicial order that allows the 'indignants' to camp again in this place, while the other part of the movement responded to the evacuation by occupying a park further north of the city, where there were new stops.
"Our idea is that if the court doesn't allow us to encamp again in the square, we're going to move to another place and we're going to continue the legal struggle to return to Zuccotti, and through sit-ins and peaceful demonstrations," said Mark Bray
Bray detailed that “indignant” response to the evacuation of their camp personally dictated by New York Mayor Michael Bloomberg came to the courts, where Judge Lucy Billings issued an order that prohibited the forced evacuation of the place and allowed protesters to stay in the camp.
In addition, the 'indignant' occupied a new park located at the confluence of the Canal Street and Sixth Avenue, very close to the entrance to the Holland Tunnel, which unites New York and New Jersey, owned by Trinity Church Parish.
At least an hour they managed to occupy the place, but after the police moved there and arrested a few protesters, even if Bray could not confirm the concrete figure, assuring Efe of the fact that he saw some journalists who had also been handcuffed.
The organization assures that there are centenaries of people who had settled in this place, where a delegation of religious leaders moved as a sign of support to the members of the movement and tried to intervene in favor of them in front of the police.
In the meantime, Zuccotti Square - which is an enclosure of private property - remains closed pending the celebration of a judicial hearing scheduled for this Tuesday during which we must analyze the legality of this morning's evacuation.
A few misplaced protesters previously carried and distributed copies of this judicial order at that time, and they even delivered it to the agents who were monitoring the square, which caused “some small clashes,” said Bray, who denounced the fact that some officials hit the protesters.
The judiciary prohibits authorities from evacuating members of Occupe Wall Street or enforcinging 'laws published after the start of the occupation or prohibiting demonstrators from accessing the park again with tents or other properties they previously used.'
Mayor Bloomberg told reporters that, because of this judicial order, the city had suspended the reopening of space to the public and protesters, to whom he however alerted that local laws do not allow them to settle with tents and sleeping bags.
The city councillor personally defended the evacuate carried out at dawn in Zuccotti Square, an operation during which 200 people were arrested and which was decided because the protesters were "violating the law".
Bloomberg explained that it had given the order to evacuate the square because it became "a place in which people did not come to protest, but to violate the laws and in some cases, to harm others", because "some stores had received threats" and residents were afraid "for their quality of life".
"The evacuation this morning at dawn has been repugnant and demonstrates that at bottom Bloomberg is more interested in defending the financial interests than those of American workers," added the movement's spokesman, who accused the mayor of "not respecting freedom of expression."
"Occupe Wall Street" officials have also said they don't think they're organizing "great actions" for Thursday in collaboration with community organizations and unions to commemorate the two months of protests, which began Sept. 17.
Pamela Anderson will be the Virgin Mary in a special Noel television program
Next to Michael Bublé and other famous Canadians, it will be released in the television program "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1
Canada's Pamela Anderson was Tim Allen's assistant in "Home Improvement", a rescuer in "Baywath", a bunny in "Playboy", the wife of rock musician Tommy Lee, and now the actress and model will be the Virgin Mary in a special Canadian television Christmas program.
The private CTV television channel announced today that Anderson, next to the "crooner" (baladin) Michael Bublé and other famous Canadians, will be released in the television program "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1 performing a very special Virgin Mary.
CTV today distributed images of the television program in which Anderson, also known for the extensive circulation on the Internet of a private pornographic video in which she appeared with her husband of that time, the drummer of "Mötley Crüe", Tommy Lee, appears dressed as the Virgin Mary by supporting in a crèche a doll that represents Jesus.
In the scene, Peters, a well-to-be-including Canadian Indian comedian who recently presented part of Charlie Sheen's "My Violent Torpedo of Truth," appears as Joseph.
In addition to Anderson and Peters, CTV's special Christmas program also features Canadian singer Bublé, who at the end of March married Argentina's Luisana Lopilato in Buenos Aires, and which is not far from controversies.
Recently, Bublé called “whore” American Kim Kardashian during one of his concerts in New York.
Japan was reborn after the earthquake
GDP rises one 1.5% thanks to domestic consumption and puts an end to the downward spiral in early March
Since March the Japanese economy has been paralyzed, convalescent again after the devastating earthquake that has become a nuclear crisis, the worst disaster in the Japanese country since the Second World War.
Initial estimates by the Government of Tokyo have calculated the US$300,00 million capital losses, a blow which, despite the difficult global context, appears to have eased.
After three consecutive quarters of falls, data from Gross Domestic Product between July and September were used to definitively turn the page: GDP raised one 1.5% compared to the previous quarter and one 6% in annualized rates.
That recorded in the second quarter - for the Japanese, the fiscal year begins in March 2012 - implies, in addition, the greatest rate of increase since the period from January to March 2010.
Recovery is partially due to the resumption of equipment acquisitions to restore areas devastated by the earthquake and subsequent tsunami.
In fact, companies increased their investments by 1.1%, compared than the 0.9% decrease in capital spending observed from January to March, the first decline after six months of uninterrupted increase.
However, the real stimulus of the Japanese economy is not based exclusively on the Keynesian impulse that invites to take advantage of the crisis to consolidate - or rebuild - the infrastructure, to use deficiencies to grow from scratch.
Japan has woken up mainly thanks to the evolution of domestic consumption, which currently represents the 60% of Japanese GDP.
This variable has raised a 1%, pushed by the increase in expenses on vehicles, travel and entertainment activities.
Stigma of yen
Tokyo returned to the domestic market when the yen – the Japanese currency – became one of the “refuge values” elected by investors fleeing the debt crisis in Eurozone and unrested United States, a privilege that weighs like a slab in the accounts of exporting companies.
With a stronger divide, the products are more expensive abroad, a serious setback for a country whose economic floating line is determined by the health of the major technology groups.
Toyota or Sony, for example, were responsible for their low results between July and September the yen’s contribution.
As markets continue their headquarters in peripheral EU countries, Yoshihiko Noda’s Government does not lower its guard.
"We must follow the risks that persist, such as the deterioration of certain foreign economies, the impact of Thailand's floods and the rapid appreciation of the yen," Secretary of State for Economic Policy Motohisa Furukawa said yesterday.
Indeed, the prospects aren’t encouraging at all.
The Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD) mentioned that the majority of member countries presented clearer signals of economic downturn in September.
The indicator that anticipates the inflection points of the global economy has highlighted that the main powers are held back.
Because it fell four decimal places up to 100.4 points, compared to 100.8 in August.
Eurozone has even been below the average, which is in level 100, falling eight decimal places up to 99.13 points.
Athes fuels doubts again
Papadimos says the adjustments will be approved, but the Conservatives won't make any written commitments
With peripheral risk premiums triggered and uncertainty resettled in parks, Greece continues to become entangled in the maze that leads to the exit of the great European debt crisis.
The feeling that the "consensus" of the new Hellene Transitional Government is rather imposed by the demands of Brussels than by a real conviction of the political forces begins to be confirmed by the facts.
While the new Prime Minister, Lukas Papadimos reported yesterday that there is no alternative for structural reforms and that the hundred-day period established in his appointment may be short to apply them, the leader of the conservative New Democracy Party, Antonis Samaras, assured that he was not going to support the new austerity measures in any way and reiterated his demand for new elections.
"The main task of this Government is to implement the decisions of the summit on October 26," Papadimos said when he opened a parliamentary debate yesterday that will end tomorrow with a vote of confidence for the new Executive.
A package of 24 programmes to boost employment, the reform of the Disciplinary Code of Officials and new measures against tax evasion will be the first initiatives.
However, Samaras no's message exudes doubts about his position: "We are committed to helping the Transitional Government, but we are not committed to anything more."
The Conservative leader has gone a step further in its pulse than the EU and IMF, the agencies that have to release the 8,000 million that Athens urgently requires, and has made it clear that it will not approve any letter of commitment to make the adjustments.
But the European Commission, irritated again by the impact provoked by the hall of Papandreu's referendum, reiterated yesterday that its demand is firm: it will not release aid if the new Government and the leaders of the main Hellene parties do not sign "a written commitment without ambiguities as soon as possible".
Economical Affairs spokesman Amadeu Altafaj explained that troika inspectors would return "very quickly" to Athens to discuss with the new Government "the elements necessary to pay the sixth tranche" of 8,000 million, even if there is no definitive date either.
The Greeks still have a lot to do.
Queen Sofía: "These are instruments for progress and hope"
The Queen underlines Spain's "predominant" place with regard to this type of finances
Spain "has reached a predominant place worldwide in the role of micro-financies".
Queen Sofía recalled that in the last two decades the country has become the world's second largest donor with regard to this instrument of cooperation whose "essence" lies in the fact that it is an "instrument for social progress, justice and hope in a better future for all of humanity, based on confidence in the human being".
The Queen yesterday inaugurated the World Microcredit Summit in Valladolid with the certainty that "there is no doubt" that this type of operations are "absolutely essential" to achieve the Millennium Development Goals established by the United Nations for 2015, and "very especially, in the goal of reducing to half the volume of people who live with incomes under one dollar a day, to which the "threshold" is set
Spain "continues to work" to enlarge the network of beneficiaries "in all corners of the planet, and assumes this task with "responsibility", said Queen Sofía, who hoped that initiatives and contributions origins during the Summit, which will continue until Thursday, "guarantee the creation of new capacities and better opportunities for the poorest and especially for women", the main beneficiaries of these loans.
We’ve “achieved a lot of goals” and “we’re facing a lot of challenges.”
The Microcredit Summit is envisioned not only as a forum for debate, but also as the platform from which "two basic goals" must be impelled.
Thus, His Majesty explained that the goal is that by the end of 2015 there are 175 million poor families who have accessed these financial services and that one hundred million of these families are coming out of the poverty line in which they now live.
The impulse in the design of microcredit in Spain and in the rest of the world "followed the path initiated a few years ago in a pioneering way" by Muhammad Yunus, stressed Queen Sofía as an "act of justice and due recognition".
And she described the father of this instrument as an "admirable idealist in the universal cause of the fight against poverty", the work she has known since 15 years ago, when the Queen traveled to Bangladesh to know on the ground her lending policy.
This experience was followed a year later by the first World Summit in Washington and other meetings such as those celebrated in Côte d'Ivoire in 1999, Canada in 2006, and "at each meeting I was able to see a continuous evolution", explained His Majesty, whose relationship with this project brought him yesterday the qualifier of "leader" gathered in Valladolid from the "banker of the poor".
Finally, Queen Sofía finished her intervention by encouraging those present to participate in the debate and in the search for solutions to move forward in the fight against poverty.
"Imperative Etical"
Foreign Minister Trinidad Jiménez warned that solidarity "is an ethical imperative" for any democratic state.
In this sense, she explained that it would be a mistake to vinculate cooperation with developing countries at times of economic prosperity, because it is a "permanent obligation".
Jiménez considered that micro-credits presuppose "a serious and rigorous policy dedicated to the eradication of poverty", and now "more than ever" the governments of developed countries must work under the criteria of solidarity and do not leave that it is the victim of cuts.
For Spain, microcredit is "one of the main lines of development cooperation".
The Secretary of State for International Cooperation, Soraya Rodriguez, applauded "the success" of giving "confidence" to the "excluded people to whom all close the door"; and the mayor of Valladolid, Francisco Javier León de la Riva, welcomed those present in a city that "opens its contaminated doors of the spirit of solidarity".
The CDU proposes to elect the European President by universal suffrage
The first day of the Congress of the Christian Democratic Union (CDU), Monday, November 14, in Leipzig, took place without real difficulty for Angela Merkel, Chancellor but also President of the Christian Democratic Party.
Europe's future was not initially planned for the programme, but was imposed in the autumn, in particular to satisfy the activists who consider the government's policy too laxist vis-à-vis the Greeks today and perhaps the Italians tomorrow.
The motion on the presidency of the European Commission, proposed by management, has been adopted almost unanimously.
Out of 1001 delegates, only nine voted against and ten abstained.
The standing ovation of several minutes to which, at the end of their interventions, Angela Merkel then, a few hours later, Wolfgang Schäuble, the very europhile minister of finance, did not leave room for doubt.
The idea-master of the chancellor - and of the motion - holds in one slogan: the response to the crisis "is no less from Europe but from Europe".
Not only because Europe is peace, but because "nine million jobs depend directly on the euro".
For the chancellor, "the time has come for a breakthrough in Europe".
This could result in changes to the Treaty of Lisbon to strengthen EU control over the budgetary policies of the euro area countries, but it could also lead to the election of the President of the European Commission by universal suffrage "in order to give a face to Europe".
In any case, this is one of the proposals of the motion adopted by the Congress whose inspirator is clearly Wolfgang Schäuble.
Certainly, it is unlikely that the Twenty-Seven will quickly agree on this revolution: Britain is hostile to it and the leaders of the CDU believe it knows that Nicolas Sarkozy is too.
But the stake is laid.
Similarly, delegates overwhelmingly rejected the proposal to weight the weight of each country on the Executive Board of the European Central Bank according to the weight of each economy, which obviously amounted to strengthening Germany's weight.
Anxious to appease the climate inside her party, the chancellor has multiplied the references to Konrad Adenauer and Helmut Kohl But she has also not sought to win her congress on the backs of "small" European countries or countries in difficulty quick to criticize the arrogant Germany.
If the motion on Europe enshrines a paragraph on the importance of "Franco-German friendship", Angela Merkel did not mention France.
Excess in hollow.
Recalling that two centuries ago, Leipzig had been at the heart of a bloody battle.
In fact, it was one of the most humiliating defeats of the Napoleonic army.
What's sick leave fraud?
Following the allocations, the UMP and the government launch the offensive on sick leave.
Nicolas Sarkozy will be Tuesday, November 15 in Bordeaux to visit a family allowance fund, and give a speech on the theme - recurring since 2007 - of the fight against social fraud and assistantship.
And to accompany this offensive, which starts from a desire to make savings in a context of rigour, but is no less heralding of an axis of the presidential campaign on the right, the majority wants to focus on sick leave.
First for the purpose of budgetary savings: the government plans to impose on private employees a fourth day of deficiency before taking care of a work stoppage.
Clearly, without a specific company agreement, an employee who falls ill would lose four days of salary, so at least 15% of his monthly income.
A measure that is likely to arouse anger, but that would make it possible to earn 280 million euros, according to Les Echos.
And that would be accompanied by another measure, of more symbolic scope: to impose on civil servants one day of uncompensated deficiency in the event of a stoppage of work (they so far do not have a waiting period, against three days in the private sector).
While these issues are under discussion, the majority is also relaunching the theme of fraud, this time targeting abuses related to work stoppages.
"The controls are going to be much more important and, in addition, if you're caught, you'll pay back," Xavier Bertrand told RTL on Sunday, November 13.
As in other cases, the announcement effects seem somewhat disproportionate to the reality of workplace arrest fraud.
It has been the subject of constant follow-up and political offensive since 2002, which have most often borne fruit.
The French not sicker than elsewhere in Europe.
Let's focus first on the national figures.
According to the National Health Insurance Fund (CNAM), more than 237 million euros in daily allowances were issued in 2006.
According to the Institute of Research and Documentation in Health Economics, daily allowances accounted in 2008 for 5% of health expenses, with 11.3 billion euros.
Of this sum, 46% concerned maternity leave and accidents at work, and 54% concerned sick leave, i.e. 6.2 billion euros.
And therefore 2.5% of health expenses.
In 2010, the French experienced an average of 14.5 days of work stoppage, compared to 17.8 in 2009, according to a study by the Alma Consulting group.
An average that hides certain differences: another survey, published in 2007 by the career management site Monster.com, showed that 75% of the 40,000 French employees surveyed said they did not take any sick days.
Another study, this time from Health Insurance , specifies that in 2010, 37% of sick leave were less than 8 days, 22% of 8 to 14 days, 15% of 15 days to a month, 15% of one to three months and 11% beyond.
When we compare them with their European neighbors, French employees are not more prone than elsewhere to work stoppages.
A lengthy study conducted in 2010 by two CNRS researchers showed that between 1994 and 2001, the overall absence rate (where for health reasons or not) oscillated, in France, between 10% and 11%, against 20% and 28% in Denmark, 15% in the United Kingdom or 16% and 18% in the Netherlands.
Sick leave fraud represents very little compared to undeclared work.
As for fraud, it reaches only a small scale.
The strengthening of legislation since 2002 means that controls are systematic on sick leave of more than 45 days.
In 2008, out of 1.5 million checks, CNAM found that 13% of the 285,000 carried out for short-term shutdowns were "unjustified or too long", i.e
In the case of judgments of more than 45 days, systematically controlled, there were 11% of "unsuitable or unjustified" cases out of 1.2 million, or 132,000.
In total, so 169 000 "unjustified" cases, out of several million employees who had a sick leave.
A figure that represents very little in the face, for example, to that of black work.
According to MP UMP Dominique Tian, author of a report on the issue in June, it would represent 9 to 15 billion euros of tax shortfalls per year, more than the total cost of daily allowances, and therefore infinitely more than fraud to them.
Papadémos: Greece will have to implement a new adjustment plan
Greece will need a new adjustment programme to recover its economy.
This is what Prime Minister Lucas Papadémos told Parliament on November 14, adding that the one-hundred days given to his unity government would not be enough to accomplish this task.
"To continue economic recovery efforts, we need the support of our European partners and a new budgetary adjustment programme," said the head of government, who delivered his first public speech at the opening of the debate on the vote of confidence in parliament on Wednesday.
The implementation of the decisions taken at the euro area summit on October 27 will be the "main task" of the new government, because the country's participation in the eurozone "is at stake", he added.
As a result, Papadémos predicted that the country's public deficit will be reduced "around 9%" of GDP by the end of the year, after being 10.6% in 2010 and 15.7% in 2009.
The right rejects new austerity measures
Antonis Samaras, the leader of the right, on Monday gave his support to the measures already approved to try to get out of the debt crisis but warned that his party would not commit to additional austerity.
"We will not vote in favour of new measures," he said at a meeting with his party deputies, New Democracy.
He agreed with the objectives of reducing the deficit and debt, and the fight against waste but hostile to any policy that would prevent economic recovery.
In Olli Rehn, the European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs, who warned that the IMF and the EU would not release the loan of 8 billion euros necessary without a written guarantee of all the parties they would support the planned measures, Antonio Samaras replied that his word was enough and that he would not sign anything under external pressure.
Euthanasia and assisted suicide must be legalized, says a committee of experts
Canadians live in denial of death, report by Royal Society of Canada
On palliative care, the committee calls for governments, health care institutions, and physicians to work together to ensure better palliative care beyond cancer.
Canadian society lives in the denial of death.
Only 9% of Canadians agree to talk with their doctor about the conditions in which they want to die and make arrangements.
This is one of the findings of an impressive Royal Society of Canada report, in which six experts involved in a variety of fields, including ethics, law and medicine.
This committee recommends that the government amend the Criminal Code to permit assisted suicide and euthanasia, where this choice is required, with a patient deemed competent to make such a decision.
From the outset, experts cite a recent survey by the Economist Intelligence Unit, which compared the quality of death in 40 countries around the world.
If Canada ranked 10th, the study notes that "the medicalization of death in Canada has spawned a culture in which people are afraid to address the subject of death."
Medicalization or not, 77% of Canadians do not have access to palliative care, the Royal Society of Canada’s experts report.
And despite most Canadians to die at home, 68.6% end up dying in the hospital.
Overall, across the country, it is in Quebec that this rate is the highest, with 86% of deaths occurring in the hospital.
On the sensitive issue of euthanasia and assisted suicide, the authors of the report come up with conclusions that go against the position of the federal government, which has already said that it does not want to change anything in the Criminal Code regarding euthanasia and assisted suicide.
However, according to the experts, who have also reviewed all countries where these approaches are legalized or decriminalized, there is no evidence that this decriminalization leads to abuses the application, i.e. the practice of euthanasia or assisted suicide on non-competent or non-consenting persons.
On the other hand, Jocelyn Downie, co-signatory of the report, said yesterday by teleconference, there are more cases of these overflows precisely in countries where euthanasia and assisted suicide are not legalized.
"Euthanasia is practiced in Canada, where it is clearly illegal."
“Assisted suicide is practiced in Canada, where it’s clearly illegal,” Downie said.
Canadians in favor of euthanasia
85% of Canadians have already voted in favour of euthanasia because they believe it would allow end-of-life people to alleviate their suffering, according to a 2010 Angus Reid poll.
And 66% believe that legalizing euthanasia will not send the message that the lives of the elderly or disabled are less valuable.
As for people who have practiced assisted suicide, respondents estimate at 41% that they should not be prosecuted.
When it comes to maintaining or ending care with a patient, the situation is less clear, the report’s authors say.
Therefore, in the list of their recommendations, they suggest that it should be stated in the Criminal Code that the withdrawal of care for which there has been a legally valid refusal is not criminal.
The committee of experts also calls for health professionals to be trained on the duty to respect a denial of end-of-life care, without fear of criminal prosecution.
On palliative care, the committee calls for governments, health care institutions, and physicians to work together to ensure better palliative care beyond cancer.
Finally, with regard to palliative or terminal sedation, already widely used in Canadian health institutions, the committee requests that it be considered, when it is not used to reduce physical pain, such as euthanasia, and that it be subject to the same procedures.
Reservations from other experts
Some experts in the field of health law have already expressed reservations about the findings of the report of experts from the Royal Society of Canada.
Pierre Deschamps, a specialist in health law, described as "extreme" the position of the experts signatories to the report, among other things because it does not limit the practice of euthanasia to terminally ill patients.
"This opens the door to a situation where everyone who would be tired of life and who would be over the age of 18 could ask that we help him commit suicide," says Deschamps, who yesterday consulted a summary of the report.
Deschamps also points out that the authors of the report seem to place the autonomy of the person above all the other values that participate in the social fabric of society.
However, "in society," he said, "there are beacons and constraints."
Health law specialist Margaret Somerville also expressed "strong opposition" to the report's findings, which she described as a "pro-euthanasia protest."
Margaret Somerville, who is herself a member of the Royal Society of Canada, in particular disputes the report's data on the absence of abuse in some countries where euthanasia and assisted suicide are legalized, namely in the Netherlands and Oregon.
King of Jordan Calls on President Assad to Leave Power
Damascus denounces "conspiracy"
Syria is increasingly isolated
Supporters of Bashar al-Assad protested yesterday outside the premises of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
Diplomatic pressurise has escalated yesterday on the Syria of Bashar al-Assad, who, with the support of Russia, resists them and denounces a "conspiracy" against the country.
Two days after the suspension of Syria by the Arab League, the European Union tightened its sanctions against Damascus while Turkey and Jordan, Syria's neighbors, took a position in favor of the departure of President Assad.
King Abdullah II of Jordan was yesterday the first Arab leader to call President Bashar al-Assad to "leave power" in Syria where about 40 civilians and members of the regime's forces were still killed.
"If Bashar had his country's interest at heart, he would have to resign, but he would also have to create the necessary conditions for a new phase of Syrian political life," he said.
For his part, Turkish Foreign Minister Ahmet Davutoglu deplored that the Turkish mediation efforts undertaken since the beginning of the year with the Syrian regime have failed.
“Those who are not at peace in the Middle East with their people and cannot satisfy them will leave,” he said, alluding to Syria, a neighboring country for which Turkey is adopting an increasingly harsh line.
The United States, for its part, welcomed a "strengthening of the consensus against Assad and the actions of the Syrian regime", following the decisions of the Arab League and the European Union.
"The international community, the United States, the EU, the Arab League, countries like Turkey are adopting an increasingly harsh tone" in the face of the crackdown in Syria, State Department spokesman Mark Toner said.
Meanwhile, 16 civilians and at least 19 members of the regime’s forces have perished in Syria’s southern region of Daraa, the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights (SOHR) said.
Two other civilians perished "during exchanges of fire and shelling to heavy machine guns in Jobar" in the besieged city of Homs, according to the NGO.
During a press conference, Syrian Foreign Minister Walid Mouallem considered that the country was "moving towards the end of the crisis".
He also reacted sharply to the decision of the Arab League to suspend Damascus of its work, which he said was "a dangerous step".
"Syria "will not flex," he added, assuring that "heaved plots against Syria will fail."
Following its decision to suspend Syria, the Arab League is now considering a "mechanism for the protection of civilians" and wants 500 members of Arab human rights organizations, media outlets and military observers in the country.
French Foreign Minister Alain Juppé has also said he is in favour of sending UN observers to help protect civilians from the regime's crackdown, which he says is locked up in "paranoia".
A new extraordinary meeting of the League is planned tomorrow in Rabat to take stock of the implementation of the crisis exit plan decided on 2 November, including the withdrawal of armed forces from cities plagued by protest and the release of arrested protesters.
Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov said the Arab Leage’s decision was “incorrect” yesterday, while China urged Syria to implement the end-of-crisis plan, while refraining from supporting possible sanctions against Damascus.
Italy
Monti demands time for markets and sacrifices for Italians
The future head of the Italian government, Mario Monti, has asked the markets for some time to train his team and implement a program, which will contain "sacrifices", in order to recover the lost credibility in the last months of the Berlusconi government.
The former European Commissioner was in discussions with the political parties yesterday before meeting the trade unions and employers today.
It is expected to set up a relatively restricted cabinet composed of technocrats outside Parliament.
"Monti spoke of an important program with a lot of sacrifices," said Francesco Nucara, deputy of one of the many small parliamentary groups engaged in the talks, after meeting with the President of the designated council.
The Speaker of the Chamber of Deputies, Gianfranco Fini, for his part, said he expects Mario Monti to ask Friday for a vote of confidence in Parliament to ensure that the new government has sufficient support.
The consultations will conclude with Mr. Monti's presentation of a short list, presumably by a dozen ministers, to the Head of State, Giorgio Napolitano.
Mr. Monti commented the nervousness of the stock and bond markets yesterday, asking them for time.
In “democracy, there are precise deadlines” to prepare a government and its program, he stressed, calling itself “certain that the markets will be patient and understand.”
Its appointment by the president was initially welcomed by the markets, but concern regained the upper hand especially after an unexpected drop in industrial production in the eurozone in September.
The Prime Minister-designate is expected to form a team consisting mainly of technocrats, even though he stressed that he would like to include "policies".
The new government is expected to last until 2013, the date of the next legislative elections, he said.
According to the President of the Chamber of Deputies, Gianfranco Fini, the new government will obtain the confidence of Parliament by Friday.
Mr Monti pledged, once taken office, to work urgently and with the aim that Italy "becomes protagonist again" in Europe.
A spokesman for European Commissioner Olli Rehn stressed that "even with a new government, our diagnosis of the Italian economy does not change".
The EU, convinced that Rome will not achieve its objective of balanced budgets in 2013 despite the austerity plans adopted in recent months, has called for new measures of rigour.
Ital’s “boss” Emma Marcegaglia, who will see it on Tuesday, also stressed the need to revive the economy, “because a country that has no growth cannot meet deficit parameters.”
Mr Monti, renowned for his competence and independence as European Commissioner (1994-2004), represents an "era change" for Italy after 17 years of "Berlusconism", the "professor" symbolizing "the challenge of seriousness" and "another Italy", according to columnists.
The big unknown will be the longevity of his team.
President Napolitano would like to avoid early elections, as Italy has to place 200 billion euros of government bonds by April 2012.
A Russian scientist has helped Iran’s nuclear program
A Russian scientist, Vycheslav Danilenko, has helped Iran develop a detonator that can be used with a nuclear weapon, an U.S. NGO specializing in atomic risk said Monday.
The Institute for International Science and Security (ISIS) relies on the recent report of the International Atomic Security Agency (IAEA) and other documents from this UN agency to identify this researcher.
Last week, the IAEA voiced its "serious concerns" about a "possible military dimension" of Iran's nuclear program.
According to ISIS drawing heavily on IAEA documents, Mr. Danilenko, born in 1934, worked for three decades from the 1960s at a Soviet military nuclear center in Chelyabinsk (Ural), and was allegedly involved in the manufacture of synthetic diamonds by explosion.
In 1989 or 1991, he reportedly left the center to establish a company producing "nano-diamonds" in Kiev.
The economic difficulties of his company reportedly led him to contact the Embassy of Iran in Ukraine in 1995.
He then cooperated with the Iranian program from 1996 to 2002, before returning to Russia.
In its latest report, the IAEA mentions "strong indications that the development by Iran" of a system of nuclear detonation "has been aided by the work of a foreign expert, who not only knew this technique well, but who, as a Member State has informed the Agency, worked during most of his career on this technology within the military nuclear program of his country of origin".
Anti-Muslim crimes and crimes have increased by 50%
Crimes against Muslims rose by nearly 50 percent from 2009 to 2010, while at the same time, other racial and religious violence declined slightly or little, according to FBI statistics released Monday.
According to these figures, the total number of violence perpetrated against Muslims rose from 107 in 2009 to 160 in 2010, or 49% increase, against an increase of 13% in violence against Catholics, a 4% decrease in offenses against Jews and an overall increase of 14% in anti-religious crimes and crimes.
The total number of "hate crimes" increased very slightly to 6,628 cases, including 47.3% motivated by differences in race and 20% by differences in religion, according to the FBI.
"After a decline in 2009, it is disturbing to see these crimes and offenses increase again," the human rights organization Human rights First stressed, "the increase in anti-Muslim violence is particularly significant," the organization said in a statement.
“Human Rights First has long emphasized that violence against Muslims, as well as all forms of hate crimes, must be seen as a serious violation of human rights,” said Paul Legendre.
“The U.S. government can and must do more to address these abuses,” he said, adding that it could go through improved police reports of “hate crimes.”
"Hate crime" in U.S. federal law is a legacy of the era of the struggle for civil rights.
This legislation was passed after the death of Martin Luther King to punish violence related to race, skin color, religion, origin, and now gender.
Twitter is changing its platform and looks more like Facebook
The micro-blogging site Twitter on Monday introduced changes in the presentation of its platform, to allow its users in particular to observe the activity of those they follow, which makes its service look a little more like that of Facebook.
A new tab "activity" and another "name" make it possible to follow the activity of other "Twittos", the Internet users whose "tweets") and those who follow you will be followed.
This makes it possible to know if they forward messages, if they subscribe to new accounts, if they have favorite messages, etc.
This new Twitter formula was tested "on a small percentage of users in August" before being broadcast to the entire network on Monday, Twitter spokeswoman Carolyn Penner told AFP.
Twitter, which was known for its uninstinctive operation, with somewhat esoteric terms (RT to rebroadcast a message from someone else, #followfriday to recommend following another user) doing the happiness of insiders, thus opens to a wider audience with a handling more comparable to that of Facebook and Google+ consumer networks.
On the network, users were debating "tweets" decided on the interest of this innovation.
"It's useless, invasive, and it looks too much like Facebook," writes @jemb123.
"The Activity feature will help me better track some elements of my Twitter feed," @dannykronstrom said.
Austria
Including a "golden rule" in the constitution
Austria's ruling social-democratic/democratic-democratic-democratic-democratic-democratic grand coalition government decided on Tuesday to include a fiscal "golden rule" in the constitution in order to reduce public deficits and thus avoid a possible deterioration by the rating agencies of Austria's sovereign rating (triple A).
Social Democratic Chancellor Werner Faymann himself announced this decision at the end of a Council of Ministers, while the Austrian public debt is 74.6%, well above the maximum 60% criterion set by the Maastricht Treaty, but clearly below the debts of countries such as Italy, Greece or Spain.
"If Austria's solvency were lowered by only one notch, from AAA to AA +, we would have to pay an additional three billion euros in interest each year," said Michael Spindelegger, vice-chancellor and Christian Foreign Minister.
Austria is thus responding to a request from the Brussels summit of the member countries of the eurozone on 26 October: the Heads of State and Government had then called for the inclusion by the end of 2012 of the reduction of public deficits and a return to the balance of the public accounts in the constitution or in a law of the same value.
The scheme adopted by the Austrian government is moving closer to the "German model", with Germany having been the first European country to adopt such a "rule".
By 2017, the structural public deficit will have to decrease by 0.75% of gross domestic product (GDP) each year and, as of 2017, the structural public deficit of the federal state will not have to exceed 0.35% of GDP each year, while the regional states and municipalities will have to present balanced budgets.
The objective is to increase public debt by 2020/21 under the criterion of 60% set by the Maastricht Treaty.
Yen strong
Noda threatens further intervention in the foreign exchange market
Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda warned on Tuesday that the Japanese authorities would again intervene in the foreign exchange market if the yen were to continue.
"We'll intervene as we did last time if we see excessive volatility" in the yen exchange rate, Noda told the Senate.
On Tuesday, the yen rose not far from the historical levels that prompted the Japanese authorities to intervene on October 31.
That day, Tokyo massively sold yen for dollars in order to lower the value of the Japanese currency which had just set a new record of vigor since 1945 against the greenback.
But the yen is regularly raised ever since, boosted by purchases from investors who see it as a "refuge value" in these uncertain times of global economic slowdown and debt crisis in Europe.
The dollar returned on Tuesday under 77 yen and the euro fell below the symbolic bar of 105 yens, close to the levels that triggered the last intervention.
Prior to the 2008-2009 financial crisis, the dollar cost around 120 yen and the euro more than 160 yen.
This surge in the Japanese currency harms the archipelago's exports because it increases the cost of Made in Japan products and reduces the value of income derived from abroad by Japanese firms, when they convey them into national currencies.
"Japan has been fighting to rebuild" since the March 11 earthquake and tsunami devastating the Tohoku region (northeast), said Noda, who fears that this rising yen will kill in the bud the fragile recovery.
The world’s third-largest economic power returned to growth in the third quarter -1.5% compared to the previous quarter – for the first time in a year, thanks to a rebound in exports and consumption of households leaded by the disaster.
But the prime minister judged that the dearness of the yen did not reflect "Japan's economic fundamentals".
Finance Minister Jun Azumi, for his part, called the Bank of Japan (BoJ), which opened a two-day meeting of its monetary policy council on Tuesday, to take all possible steps to weaken the yen.
But the institution's deputy governor, Hirohide Yamaguchi, warned that the BoJ had already taken "appropriate measures", hinting that no additional monetary easing was expected on Wednesday.
At its last meeting at the end of October, the issue institute increased its purchases of treasury bills by 5,000 billion yen (519 billion euros) to 55,000 billion yen (519 billion euros) on the amounts it spends on the purchases of miscellaneous financial securities to flood the liquidity market.
Euronizon:
rates are rising, stock markets are flinching
The risk of contagion from the debt crisis still undermines European indices.
Despite the appointment of new heads of government in Greece and Italy and better growth figures in France and Germany, rates for weakened countries continue to rise.
Still worried for the stability of the eurozone, the European stock exchanges are sinking a little more into negative territory, this Tuesday, November 15.
In Paris, at 10 a.m., the CAC 40 lost 1.4% to 3,064 points.
The Dax in Frankfurt sells 0.4% and London 0.1%.
“With the new strains on debt yields from troubled eurozone states, equity markets around the world continue to return some of their recent gains.”
“Investors, nervous, are all too aware of the risk of contagion,” Terry Pratt, an institutional broker at IG Markets told Reuters.
The Tokyo Stock Exchange closed down 0.7%, suffering in turn from the renewed fears of market participants about Europe’s ability to contain the debt crisis.
“Japan, like the rest of the world, has its eyes on Europe and exchanges will continue in a corridor of narrow variations as long as investors do not have evidence that the situation is stabilized,” said Mitsushige Akino, fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management.
This renewed concerns about the future of the eurozone penalizes the single currency, which continues to fall on Tuesday.
The euro is trading at its lowest level in a month and a half.
Around 10 a.m., the European currency is worth US$1.3570, down 0.4%.
According to the brokers, it backed down after Chancellor Angela Merkel’s party, the CDU, meeting in congress on Monday, adopted a motion including the possibility for a country in difficulty to leave the eurozone without leaving the European Union.
Italian and Spanish rates well beyond 6%
Borrowing rates from Italy and Spain remain at very worrying levels.
This Tuesday morning, the yield of 10-year Italian bonds reached 6.85%, that of Spanish securities of the same maturity 6.2%.
These tensions on the interest rates of the countries of the south, but also in France, continue to fuel the fears of the speakers.
The head of the future Italian government Mario Monti has demanded time from the markets to restore the situation in Italy, threatened with suffocation by the weight of his debt.
The new Greek Prime Minister Lucas Papademos affirmed that the implementation of the decisions taken at the eurozone summit on October 27 would be the "main task" of his government.
Spain finds itself in the line of sight of the markets, while parliamentary elections in the country are held on Sunday.
The spread between the 10-year bonds of Germany and Spain has reached a new all-time high.
Awaiting growth figures in the eurozone
On the statistical side, France recorded an economic growth of 0.4% in the third quarter, slightly better than the expected 0.3%, driven by household consumption.
INSEE, however, downgraded the figure for the second quarter, with a 0.1% decline in Gross Domestic Product (GDP) instead of stagnation.
And, it is really the fourth that worries economists the most.
Germany, for its part, grew by 0.5% over the same period.
A first estimate for the entire eurozone is expected at 11am, while the European Commission has just warned against the risk of a further recession.
Energy policy:
The facts must prevail over dogmas
If there is a subject that cannot be dealt with from short-term considerations or under the influence of emotion, it is indeed energy policy.
The availability and cost of electricity directly affect the purchasing power and competitiveness of companies.
It is healthy that an electoral meeting is an opportunity to reflect on such issues and the role of nuclear power.
The debate will only be fruitful if it explores all the economic, social and environmental consequences of the proposed options.
The French must base their opinion on objective facts, rational data.
While there are 7 billion people on the planet, the demographic evolution will lead to a doubling of the demand for electricity by 2050, unless depriving a huge part of the world's population of a vital element.
At the same time, it will be necessary to compensate for the scarcity of fossil resources to continue to produce electricity permanently, without forgetting the urgent need to reduce greenhouse gas emissions to fight against climate change, a subject that disappeared from the ecological debate.
The Fukushima accident does not change any of these parameters.
That is why Germany remains isolated in its decision to give up nuclear power.
This is not the case of Belgium, which submits itself to the need to be able to find a replacement source capable of substituting itself, nor of Switzerland, which just intends to build only state-of-the-art power plants.
Conversely, Great Britain, Finland, Poland, the Czech Republic, the Netherlands, Sweden, South Africa, China, India, Brazil, to name but a few, continue their projects with determination.
The United States, meanwhile, decided after Fukushima to add a unit to their park by resuming the construction of a plant interrupted after the Three Mile Island accident.
If the German model, focused on renewable energies, is sometimes presented as an example, reality thwartens discourse.
Berlin's choice will result in the increase in the price of electricity, an energy dependence aggravated by the increased use of imported gas, in particular from Russia, a surge in CO2 emissions driven by the construction of gas and coal-fired power plants.
In a few months, all these unfortunate effects are already felt.
The case of Denmark should be meditated.
European champion of wind, from which he draws 30% from his electricity, he is also one of the largest users of coal and gas because of the intermittency of the wind; according to which, his CO2 emissions and the price of his electricity are respectively 65% and 50% higher than the European average.
Conversely, thanks to the consistency of our energy policy, our neighbors pay an electricity bill 40% higher than that of French households.
While in a globalized economy the cost of energy is a key factor of competitiveness and a guarantee of the maintenance of the industrial tool on the national territory, French companies benefit from the cheapest electricity in Europe.
As a result, any significant reduction in the share of nuclear power would lead to a sharp rise in the price of electricity, which would make illusory the beautiful declarations on the reindustrialisation of our country and the defense of purchasing power.
A price that a large majority of French people refuse to pay, especially since they do not show opposition in principle to this energy, the polls show it.
Recall that the energy policy of our country has made it possible to build an industrial sector of 125 000 direct jobs and 410 000 induced jobs.
Highly exporting, she drives a galaxy of SMEs specializing in international markets and makes them very creative of non-relocatable jobs.
If France were to decide to get out of nuclear power, it would be sacrificing many of these SMEs by reducing as a skin of sorrow the 6 billion euros of French exports of nuclear equipment and services.
Which electrician would order an EPR designed to operate sixty years while the decline of its manufacturer would be programmed?
To solve the energy equation, it must be admitted that there is no source of production of miracle electricity, any more than there is a diabolical.
Areva's development of low-carbon, nuclear and renewable CO2-emitting solutions demonstrates the complementarity of energy sources.
For nuclear power, maintaining the highest level of safety is an absolute imperative.
We have in France a safety authority that exercises uncompromising and transparent control over existing plants as well as on the Flamanville construction site.
Our teams, whose professionalism is recognized, are inhabited by the same safety obsession.
In half a century, the nuclear industry experienced three serious accidents.
Only one, Three Mile Island, intervened under normal operating conditions; it remained free of human or environmental consequences.
In Chernobyl, the design of the reactor and unforgivable human faults provoked drama.
In Fukushima, some seem to forget that the accident is the result of two natural disasters on an unprecedented scale that have caused the deaths of tens of thousands of people.
The nuclear industry will learn from this accident, as it did for the previous two.
As a result of this approach and cooperation between the French and German security authorities, operators and builders, the EPR presents a design making it able to withstand such phenomena.
While it is considered by all electricians wanting to build a plant, stopping its construction in Flamanville would be a magnificent gift offered to the competitors of Areva and EDF by leaving them the free way to seize leadership in the unmissable development of nuclear.
It is essential and legitimate to seek the best energy mix for our country, but it would be irresponsible to leave the emotion, dogmas and partisan maneuvers dominate such a decisive debate for the economic, social and financial situation of our country.
Is immigration a burden or an opportunity for the economy?
The immigration policy conducted under Nicolas Sarkozy has been criticized from a variety of angles - engorgement of police, judicial and administrative services subject to the policy of the figure, compatibility of this policy with the self-proclaimed status of "countries of human rights" of France.
More recently, it is the administrative harassment of foreign students who made the news, the Minister of the Interior (see Libération of May 23) proclaiming in passing that France "does not need foreign talents, masons and restaurant servers".
However, it is rarely analysed from an economic point of view.
It is that there is a fairly broad consensus on this subject between left and right.
On the right, the tone was set by Jacques Chirac who declared in 1976 that "900,0000 unemployed should not be a problem in a country comprising nearly 2 million immigrant workers", and on the left, by Michel Rocard explaining that France "can not accommodate all the misery of the world" in 1990.
The only difference, the degree of generosity
In 2005, the fear of the invasion of the national territory by hordes of Polish plumbers was shared on both the left and the right.
For both edges, immigrants are perceived as a burden for the French economy and society.
The only difference lies in the degree of generosity that will be given to immigrants.
In his program, François Hollande confines himself to banalities, indicating that co-development will solve our "migration problem".
This idea of immigration as a burden, a problem, may be paying electorally; but it is economically very costly.
As economists Ian Goldin and Geoffrey Cameron point out in a recent book synthesizing the most up-to-date knowledge on immigration (Exceptional People: How Migration Shaped Our World and Will Define Our Future, May 2011, Princeton University Press, 352 pp.), there is a broad consensus among scholars on the positive impact of migration flows on economic growth, wages and employment in immigrant countries.
Restricting immigration anaemia growth and harming employment.
Fears about the impact of migrants are based on the idea that they are likely to replace national workers, especially low-skilled workers, exerting downward pressure on their wages.
But experience shows that, in reality, immigrants are much more complementary than substitutes for national employees, working for the most part in sectors with employment shortages.
Low-skilled immigrants work in sectors that do not attract national wage earners, and the most qualified in dynamic sectors in which training does not follow the supply of jobs.
A Positive Effect on Wages
In the same way that a surgeon will have difficulty working in a country experiencing a shortage of anesthesiologists, these complementarities between nationals and migrants mean that the arrivals of immigrants have a positive effect on the wages and employment of nationals.
Giovanni Peri has calculated that an increase of 1% in migratory flows leads to an increase of between 0.6 and 0.9% in long-term real wages.
And this without taking into account the fact that the diversity provided by immigrants contributes to the creation of ideas and economic growth (high proportion of naturalized immigrants among the American Nobel laureates; Google, Intel, Paypal, eBay and Yahoo were founded by immigrants).
Migrants are also net contributors to social systems, on average up to 1% of the total budget in European countries.
For example, the World Labour Organisation estimates that, in Germany, an immigrant arriving at the age of 30 will make a net contribution (less-expenditure revenue) of 150000 euros to public budgets on average over his lifetime.
Nicolas Sarkozy wanted, at the beginning of the mandate, to look for the missing growth points "with the teeth".
The teeth in question, used to deter immigrants, had the exact opposite effect.
Europe has invented democracy, it remains to think of European democracy
Democracy is a precious asset; it is also a fragile good.
The European continent should be able to remember it which, having invented it in Athens more than two thousand years ago, experienced throughout the 20th century the tragedies of the Great War, totalitarianisms, the Holocaust, the gulag, but also Franco in Spain, Salazar in Portugal, the colonels in Greece.
Some may have believed that it would suffice, for democracy definitively to triumph over its enemies, to exercise the vote, and to exercise it in the most direct way possible.
We know, since Napoleon "the Little", that this was an ease of thought.
Universal suffrage, in order to be the necessary condition of democracy, is not the sufficient condition and can even become an "instrument of oppression" as the republican philosopher Etienne Vacherot once said.
No, democracy requires much more: a public space, a public spirit, values, an organization, a separation and control of powers, education, Enlightenment, economic and social solidarities, justice.
The crisis in Europe that we are experiencing is an economic crisis, but it is just as much, first and foremost a crisis of politics and democracy.
The palinody of the true-false Greek referendum will have had in this regard the value of a double revealer: that of the need for a powerful return of politics, but also that of the disturbing feeling of its impossibility, its incongruity.
This ambivalence must be thought to be outdated.
The truth is that the front lines of the democratic struggle have now shifted, and for many of them Republican soldiers are no longer pulling in the right direction.
We have nothing to gain from demanding a succession of national referendums, organized according to necessities and in dispersed order.
A white-seing to the European absolute monarch or a leap together in the vacuum of nationalist retreat: what a choice for the citizen!
No, we do not want cleaves but an exercise, common, by right, serene and continuous, of choice and democracy, this time at European level.
There are no crises in Greece, Italy, Spain, Portugal - we can no longer stop the enumeration - because there is only one crisis, and it is in Europe.
It is the whole Europe that bends to the dictates of markets and rating agencies, which suffers from its powerlessness and its lack of solidarity.
The whole of Europe is humiliated on the international stage.
We are worried about transfers of sovereignty to Europe - but it is the whole Europe that slowly loses its sovereignty, and with it each of its nations.
The urgency is to restore popular sovereignty the steering of the European project.
Because it is together that Europeans will be able to reduce the weight of their debts, free themselves from the markets and prepare for the future by investing.
Exiting from the crisis will involve decisions at European level that cannot be taken without total democratic legitimacy, except of course to lead us to disaster.
Having long struggled with this proposal of the Socialists and Jacques Delors, everyone today is calling for the European economic government.
Well, either.
But to think without a stronger political and democratic integration, and experienced as such by the citizens, would be a new madness and a new impasse.
We have to take on a new stage of European integration.
To make the Republic in France, it was necessary to "make" republicans.
The revolution in the interests could not suffice.
This lesson is still valid.
We need to build a more economically integrated Europe.
This can only be achieved by building a more politically democratic Europe, a genuine European popular sovereignty.
In order to do Europe, let's make Europeans.
"Contactless" smart cards are becoming widespread
The world smart card show opens Tuesday in Villepinte, near Paris, in an optimistic climate.
The year 2011 is expected to end with an 11% increase in global sales of microprocessor cards (bank card, phone with SIM card...), to more than 6 billion units, according to Eurosmart.
The most promising segment is that of contactless technology that allows you to pay for public transportation or pay your purchases by simply approaching the card of a reader.
This year, 460 million contactless smart cards are expected to be sold around the world, compared to 320 million a year earlier!
Their share is expected to increase again in 2012 (580 million units expected) and represent nearly 10% of the overall smart card market (6.925 million cards).
Long lagging behind in the Asia-Pacific region, Europe is gaining ground for contactless payment.
Some 26 million Visa credit cards are already equipped with this feature and 75,000 payment terminals accept it.
In France, the beginnings are more modest: 400,000 Visa contactless cards have been deployed.
But the supermarkets put the bites double.
Carrefour issued 2.5 million contactless MasterCard pass cards this year.
To get to the next level, banks now need to equip their customers.
"Two major French establishments are committed to systematically distributing bank cards equipped for contactless to their customers in the future," said Gérard Nébouy, Managing Director Visa Europe France.
Banks are testing payment by mobile
But this is only a first step towards the payment method of the future: the mobile phone.
Few devices are now equipped with NFC (Near Field Communication) technology required to carry out transactions.
"They're late."
"But they should finally experience a real boom next year."
"We expect the sale of 80 to 120 million units in 2012 around the world," explains Marc Bertin, president of Eurosmart.
Banks take the lead.
The Crédit Agricole announced yesterday the launch of a test from December to June in Caen.
The iPhones of some 200 customers and collaborators will be equipped with a case authorizing contactless payment.
BPCE has a similar project in Strasbourg and Marseille.
And Societe Generale, too, said yesterday it was preparing the placing on the market of an offer allowing its customers to pay without contact with their mobile.
From the Sahara to Nigeria, the terrorist front expands
Paris is concerned about the links between al-Qaeda in the Islamic Maghreb and the Boko Haram sect.
Suspected for a long time, the links between the terrorist group al-Qaeda in the Islamic Maghreb (AQIM) and the Nigerian sect Boko Haram are confirmed.
Algeria's Deputy Foreign Minister Abdelkader Messahel on Sunday even claimed that Algiers has now "acquired certainty".
"The way the two organizations operate and the reports of the intelligence services show that there is good cooperation," he said.
Paris appears to be doing a similar analysis.
The investigation into the abduction of two Frenchmen in Niger last January reportedly uncovered footbridges.
Kidnapped in a Niamey restaurant by relatives of Aqmi, Antoine de Léocour and Vincent Delory had to be killed the next day at the same time as several of their captors during an operation launched by the French special forces.
In the rubble of the intervention, French and Nigerian investigators had to find several phone chips belonging to the terrorists.
Terrorist axis
The analysis of calls directed to several interlocutors based in Mali, Niger and Nigeria.
According to Radio France Internationale (RFI), there are two issues of particular interest among investigators: one leads to a Nigerien who has stayed at length in Maiduguri, a city in northern Nigeria, the cradle of Boko Haram and the other to a man who claims to be close to Aqmi and the sect.
"This is an advance, but we must probably wait for more formal evidence before talking about operational cooperation between AQIM and Boko Haram."
"But it's possible."
"We have known for years that the sect enjoys external financial and intellectual support and that it is becoming more and more active," said Kunle Amuwo, a Nigerian researcher.
In recent months these "supports" had been increasingly evident in the evolution and discourses of Boko Haram or in its operating procedures, which now involve the use of suicide bombers or explosive devices that are increasingly complex and of the same invoice as those used by AQIM.
The international community, starting with Washington, has therefore pressured Abuja to take into account the problems posed by Boko Haram.
Indeed, the Nigerian authorities have long regarded the group as an illuminated community of little interest.
A quick success
Founded in 2002 in Maiduguri around a mosque, a school and the fundamentalist iman Mohammed Yusuf, Boko Haram - which in hausasa means "Western education is a sin" - advocates the establishment of an Islamic emirate in the Muslim north of Nigeria.
In this poor region, the association enjoys rapid success.
It is politicized and demands strict respect for Sharia while its activists engage in the fight against the unholy central government.
The latter first target churches, bars, administrations.
In 2009, Boko Haram activists were particularly active, increasing attacks on police stations.
Abuja's reaction is, as usual, without nuances.
The army's occupying the city, there are 800 dead.
Mohammed Yusuf, arrested alive, is killed in prison.
From then on, the sect will radicalize and leave its northern strongholds.
Last Christmas, she claimed responsibility for an attack that killed 80 people in Jos, then another in Abuja.
In June, it is still in Abuja that Boko Hamar attacks the secure headquarters of the police during a suicide attack, his first.
In August, the United Nations Headquarters will be its first international target.
The creation of a terrorist axis in Africa that would sail from Mauritania to Somalia through Nigeria is now of concern to all security specialists.
While Aqmi has invaded Mali and Niger, which al-Shebab Somalis are operating in Kenya, Boko Haram appears as a new threat in the heart of the continent.
Last week, during a meeting in Nigeria with President Goodluck Jonathan, Alain Juppé warned against Boko Haram and said he was “ready to share all intelligence.”
The World According to Republican Presidential Candidates
On China, on Iran, on aid to Israel, Mitt Romney, Herman Cain, and Rick Perry compete for simplism and ignorance.
Causes an America weakened by the crisis manage a complex world with simple, even simplistic ideas?
Listening to Republican presidential candidates, voters may believe that.
In recent days, Mitt Romney, Herman Cain, Rick Perry and the others have multiplied thunderous statements and shortcuts in foreign policy, traditionally a Republican strong point.
On China, on Iran, on aid to Israel, slogans - often unsupported - arise.
Mitt Romney, the best-placed in the race to nominate the "Grand Old Party", threatened to draw the Chinese before the WTO and implicated them "for manipulating their currency", a bearing theme in an America exasperated by migrating entire swathes of his industry to Asia.
It doesn’t matter if it’s going to involve starting a trade war, he said.
Jon Huntsman, Obama’s former ambassador to China and the only candidate to have a sophisticated vision of foreign policy, almost strangled himself in the face of an attitude that “flattenes” emotions.
He called for a muscular but constructive dialogue with Beijing (Obama’s current position).
But Huntsman, who stagnates in the depths of the polls, remains inaudible.
Just liked as Reagan
Romney has also promised military strikes on Iran if the sanctions fail to stop his nuclear program.
"If I'm elected, I'll arrest Iran," he braggered.
"If Obama's re-elected, they'll have the bomb."
He also vowed to increase military aid to Israel, accusing Obama of failing his obligations vis-à-vis this partner.
Entrepreneur Herman Cain, the second-best-placed candidate, displays an embarrassing lack of knowledge of the files.
Recently, he called to counter the Chinese military threat because Beijing "is trying to develop a nuclear capability" - apparently unaware that China has held the military atom since 1964!
Texas Governor Rick Perry, who supports Israel and wants strikes against Iran, says he’s willing to engage the U.S. military in Mexico against drug cartels.
The use of torture against suspected terrorists, banned by Obama in 2009, is also advocated by Cain, Perry and candidate Michele Bachmann.
We’re away from the 2008 Republican candidate, John McCain, a heavyweight in foreign policy.
The opponents of the current president reply that Reagan also had simple ideas and that he won the Cold War.
“They point out that Obama himself was an amateur and that he had to put water in his wine when it came to anti-terrorism.
They rightly insist on the failure of his naïve dialogue with Iran, on the too hasty departure of the "boys" from Iraq, on his procrastinations in Libya, thus sticking a label of weakness.
But the attack isn't so easy.
The population considers Obama’s national security record, which eliminated bin Laden rather positive.
Since Iraq, it is wary of the military interventions that lead to bogged down.
And, arguing that we have to economically rebuild America to resurrect his leadership, Obama strikes fairer.
The Nord Stream, a new gateway to Russian gas in Europe
The Nord Stream pipeline, directly connecting Western Europe to Russia by moving to the bottom of the Baltic Sea, has come into service.
It has to provide gas for the equivalent of 30 million households.
Gazprom, the Russian gas giant, wanted to celebrate the event brilliantly.
More than 500 guests, including four heads of state and government, took part, last Tuesday, on the shores of the Baltic, between orchestra and fine buffet, in the inauguration of the Nord Stream, this new pipeline that connects directly, under the sea, Russia to Germany.
Symbolically, German Chancellor Angela Merkel, Russian President Dmitry Medvedev, French Prime Minister François Fillon and his Dutch counterpart Mark Rutte have together turned the wheel of the valve commanding the entry of Russian gas into the Western European network.
With this new pipeline, 27.5 billion cubic meters of additional Russian gas will arrive every year in Western Europe by a first tube of 1,224 km connecting the region of St. Petersburg to the Land of Mecklenburg-Pomerania.
A second tube must be completed at the end of 2012, making it possible to deliver a total of 55 billion cubic meters of gas, the equivalent of all French consumption.
And we are already talking about the possibility of building a third one on the same route.
For Gazprom, the timely completion of this huge construction site has the air of victory.
The project had been strongly contested by Poland and the Baltic countries.
The Nord Stream will make it possible to deliver gas directly to Germany, avoiding any land pass through a third country (Poland or Ukraine).
As a result, these two countries lose some of their power: it is becoming more difficult for them to influence Russia by threatening to close access to Western European markets.
At the height of the debate, Warsaw did not hesitate to compare the Nord Stream to a "new German-Soviet pact", in reference to the alliance between Hitler and Stalin to occupy Poland, in 1939.
That is to say if the subject is politically sensitive.
Western Europe, by supporting this Russian project, has gained greater security of its supplies.
But it is at the cost of a weakening of the Europe of energy, since the project has created a breach between Europeans of the East and the West.
In just five years, Russia has succeeded in its vision of a direct alliance between Russia and the wealthy Western countries, which are highly gas-consumers and whose deposits are in decline.
"This pipeline is a component of our partnership with Russia, it is a new artery that binds us organically," confirmed François Fillon, inaugurating this gas pipeline, demonstrating how political and economic major gas contracts are.
All this explains the wide smiles displayed by the leaders of Gazprom, present in large numbers in Lubmin, at the point of arrival of gas.
The construction of this pipeline is also a technical feat.
The underwater tube is made of steel sheathed with concrete in order to protect it from the anchors of the ships.
It rests on the bottom of the sea, 200 m on average.
To build it, we had to avoid minefields dating back to the Second World War.
"A hundred had to be abducted or destroyed, as a precaution," said Jens Müller, spokesman for Nord Stream.
We also had to multiply the measures to protect fauna and flora.
The tube is a long metal snake, heavy as 242 Eiffel towers, that nothing interrupts over its entire length: neither valve nor maintenance station.
A control platform was planned off Sweden, but project officials renounced while the Swedes were reluctant to see a Russian state-owned company permanently established off the coast.
"In case of damage, we stop the passage of gas in less than a minute," says Jens Müller.
In the event of a leakage, the gas contained in the pipeline therefore escapes into the air.
But this case is considered unlikely by the manufacturers who have planned the welds to experience a leak every 100 000 years.
Monitoring of the condition of the pipeline is provided by an underwater robot, dragged by a ship, for external observation, as well as by an apparatus circulating inside the pipeline.
From the shape of a cigar, it is introduced inside the tube, one meter wide in diameter, and it is pushed by the gas.
On arrival, after three full days of travel at the speed of 3 meters second, it delivers precise information on the possible deformations of the metal.
The pipeline has been designed to operate for fifty years without having to undergo any repair.
The circulation of the gas is simply ensured by pressure.
It is 200 bars at the start, and 100 on arrival.
Five companies joined forces to carry out this project.
The leader remains Gazprom, which keeps 51% of the shares.
Germany's E.ON and BASF (15.5% each), as well as the Dutch Gasunie and the French group GDF Suez (9% each).
These five companies have created a joint venture, named Nord Stream, responsible for the construction and operation of the pipeline.
It has its headquarters in Zug, Switzerland, where taxation is particularly favourable.
Gazprom then leased to this company, for twenty renewable years, all the gas transmission capacity.
“Gazprom will pay for total capacity, regardless of the amount of gas actually transported,” Müller said.
The financing of the construction of the pipeline has been provided at 30% by shareholders and 70% by bank loans.
The total cost of the work is expected to reach 7.4 billion euros for the two tubes.
For GDF Suez, for example, the investment amounted to 240 million.
But minority shareholders are sure to return to their expenses, since only Gazprom assumes the risk.
The pipeline emerges from the Baltic in Lubmin, on a sandy coastline planted with pine trees.
Valves, 4.5 meters high, form the gas exit door.
Sensors measure the pressure, the temperature and the specificities of the gas arriving, before it is transferred to the German gas pipelines.
Since last Tuesday, this terminal receives one million cubic meters of Russian gas in every passing hour.
At full capacity, it will be 3 million per hour and in 2012, 6 million.
Moleskine, a brand notebook.
Created about fifteen years ago by an Italian company, Moleskine wants to restore the taste of writing.
He has a scent of adventures, hours spent writing in the light of a candle.
The Moleskine notebook is easily recognizable, with its rectangular black cover, its elastic, its leaves of a broken white color, as if aged by time.
However, the Moleskine notebook as we know it today was born... in 1997.
The Italian company Modo Modo, based in Milan, decided at the time to launch a designable but distinguished notebook.
Maria Sebregondi, then a consultant for the company, is in charge of launching the new product.
The notebook is inspired by an old production technique, the mole skin (skin) which designates a varnished cotton used to cover benches for example.
This type of notebook was very popular with writers and travelers.
"Moleskine revived an old and forgotten object, which was an icon of avant-garde artists, until the 20th century," says Maria Sebregondi, who has since become the head of the brand at Modo Modo.
Success is there.
Between 2006 and 2010, sales increased by 26% to 12.7 million coins sold.
We are far from the confidential production of the early years, where only 3 000 notebooks were designed.
Moleskine goes beyond the Italian borders and exports to 70 countries, from France to the United States.
It is difficult to establish a portrait-robot of the buyer, because the notebook seduces both the businessman and the student and professionals.
Like Moleskine, its consumers are cosmopolitan, open-minded and cultured.
The small Italian company, which had 12 employees in its infancy, has almost 100 today and has had two offices since 2008; one in Milan and the other in New York.
She distributes her notebooks in bookstores, from Fnac to small shops in the city center and knew how to impose her identity.
“We’ve seen the air of time and this new need for writing, despite the rise of e-mails,” explains Maria Sebregondi.
This cultural signature accompanies the development of Moleskine: recently, the brand has launched an exhibition "Detour" where architects or artists exhibit their own notebook.
In August, she also launched a leopard cover at the Locarno Film Festival.
Taking advantage of its success and this cultural aura, Moleskine has diversified its products: bags, agenda, pens, computer covers... The brand is available on a whole range of supports.
In the fall of 2006, Modo Modo was acquired by the investment fund SG Capital Europe, now Syntegra Capital, writing a new page in the history of Moleskine.
Why is the unemployment rate for disabled workers remaining so high?
Approximately 19% of people with disabilities are looking for work.
The 15th Week for Disabled Employment opens today.
Despite a proactive public policy put in place in 2005, the unemployment rate for people with disabilities is still twice as high as average: 19%, compared to 9% for the entire population.
Discouraging?
"No," says Éric Blanchet, director general of the Adapt, the association that organizes the week for the employment of the disabled. "
“The progress made over the past fifteen years is considerable.”
"At the time, the unemployment rate wasn't two but three times higher."
"However there is still a lot to do."
It must be said that for unemployed people with disabilities, there are still many obstacles before access to employment.
They are rather old: 37% are older than 50 (17% on average).
They have a low level of qualification: 80% do not have the baccalaureate and of the 2.3 million students of higher education, only 10 000 are disabled.
Finally, long-term unemployment is widespread: 53% for the disabled, compared to 38% on average.
In short, so many criteria that particularly complicate the return to employment, and that "constitute the knot of the problem", according to Christian Grapin, managing director of Tremplin, an association created in 1992 to help companies to recruit disabled workers.
That's for the objective brakes.
Remains unsaided, the mentalities that evolve, of course, but too slowly.
A study carried out in April by the association IMS-Entreprendre pour la Cité indicated that the managers surveyed have "a low level of knowledge of disability", with an "over-representation of people in wheelchairs, psychic or intellectual deficiencies".
As a result, private companies with more than 20 employees are still far from reaching the employment target of 6% of disabled workers, set out in a law of... 1987.
The latest known figures, dating back to 2008, report a ratio of 2.6%.
"We are now around 3%," says Pierre Blanc, CEO of Agefiph, the agency responsible for collecting contributions from companies that do not comply with this law.
The proof: the number of "taxed" establishments, as well as the amounts paid, decreased between 2007 and 2011, increasing 59 000 to 46 000 structures (and from 606 to 480 million euros).
"The 2005 law, which increased contributions, created strong pressure on companies," explains Pierre Blanc.
"But this improvement also reflects a real awareness, especially in large groups."
According to the Ministry of Employment, only 59% of establishments with 20 to 49 employees employ disabled workers, compared to 82% of companies with 100 to 199 employees.
Hazard: "Only happiness"
Before facing Les Bleus Tuesday night (20h45) Eden Hazard and Georges Leekens did not hide the pleasure that this friendly match gives them.
We won't go so far as to write that there was collusion between them, but the image is symbolic.
Sitting side by side on the platform of the auditorium of the Stade de France, Georges Leekens and Eden Hazard did indeed make peace.
Five months after the mood gesture of his striker against Turkey, the Belgian coach has only praise to do about him.
"I'm very happy with Eden, his progress and the fact that he's Belgian."
"I want to move forward," assures the Lillois.
As for the contrast between his performances in the club and in selection, "it's a whole, he justified himself."
"In Lille, I'm there all year, which is not the case with Belgium."
"I have to take my marks."
Housed against Romania Friday night in view of this match against France, Hazard comes to Saint-Denis to finally "shine".
Without omitting the notion of "pleasure".
Les Bleus, he has encouraged them since the age of 7 and the victorious campaigns of 1998 and 2000.
“I’ve always been a supporter,” he says.
Necessarily, this helps to further strengthen the "particular" nature of this meeting.
Especially since in front, "there is Mathieu (Debuchy), Yohan (Cabaye) and Adil (Rami) ", he recalls.
"We ran into each other earlier."
"To be able to play against them, it's only happiness!"
With bonhomie, Leekens spoke of "pride".
"Prided" to play "a gala match".
"Prided" also to note - as a "neighbour" - that France has "became again a team of very high level" a little more than a year after the trauma of Knysna.
"Laurent Blanc has done a thunder job," he said.
While Belgium will miss the next Euro, the technician would see himself sharing the plane with Les Bleus direction... Brazil in 2014.
“Our little Belgians are getting big,” he said.
"We're building something, the players are ambitious, they're hungry."
"You're going to realize it in the next few matches". The one against France included.
Portugal very confident
The Portugal of Paulo Bento begins with a lot of optimist its dam back against Bosnia (22h00), this Tuesday in Lisbon.
To go, both teams lacked realism.
"At the end of these 90 minutes, we will be a happy team and, above all, a happy country."
Paulo Bento is already seen in Poland and Ukraine.
For him, there is no shadow of a doubt, the Selecçao, of which he has been in charge since September 2010, will qualify for Euro 2012.
Portugal is only 90 minutes away from happiness.
But there's a game to play, and an opponent, Bosnia, to debolt.
In view of the first leg (0-0), Friday in Zenica's hell, it seems obvious that the games are not made.
Portugal dominated the game for more than an hour, certainly, but he was unable to score this precious goal, which would have relieved him.
"It's a good result, but it carries some risk because we didn't score a goal away"
But at home, in their luminous Stade de la Luz in Lisbon, the Portuguese are convinced to pass, in front of an audience that should also be boiling, even if the game is obviously "very tight", says an optimistic but lucid Bento.
“We’re looking forward to a very close game, but we’re quite convinced that we can get the upper hand,” the 42-year-old said.
"We continue to have a 50% chance of being at Euro 2012," said Carlos Queiroz.
The draw reported Friday from Bosnia forces the Portuguese to score and win.
A draw can be enough for the Bosnians, who also need to score.
“It’s a good result, but there’s a some risk because we didn’t score an away goal,” Bento said.
“We’re going to play against a team that has a lot of technical qualities and has players with a lot of individual qualities,” he added.
As in Zenica, the Portuguese will intend to put maximum pressure on the Bosnians to enter in order not to "give them time to reflect."
A good idea, as long as you’re realistic this time.
